/* search-index.js — prebuilt full-text index (generated; excludes flashcards & exam questions). */
window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Key Terms & Learning Outcomes","secs":[{"s":"Key Terms & Learning Outcomes","a":"","k":["Exam Prep"]},{"s":"Introduction to PMH Nursing","a":"intro","k":["Introduction to PMH Nursing","Key Terms","Mental health (WHO) — Well-being in which the individual can realize their own potential, cope with the normal stresses of life, work productively, and contribute to the community.","Mental illness (APA) — Health conditions involving changes in emotion, thinking, or behavior, associated with distress and/or problems functioning in social, work, or family activities.","Diathesis-stress model — The most accepted explanation for mental illness. Diathesis = a biological predisposition; stress = any emotional or environmental stress or trauma. Many carry the predisposition. It becomes illness only when a cert…","Resilience — The ability and capacity to secure the resources needed to support personal well-being: optimism, competence, and a sense of mastery over obstacles. Determines the incidence, severity, and prognosis of mental illness.","Recovery — A process of change through which an individual improves their health and wellness, lives a self-directed life, and reaches their full potential. Possible even where cure is not. The recovery model emphasizes hope, self-determina…","Comorbidity — Two co-occurring chronic illnesses: two mental health disorders (depression and anxiety), or a mental and a medical one (depression and diabetes).","Dual diagnosis — A co-occurring mental illness and substance use disorder. One of the two must be a substance use disorder. That is what separates it from comorbidity.","Adherence (not compliance) — Following the plan of care, and the term that replaced compliance. \"Compliance\" carries a strongly negative connotation, so the language change is itself a way of breaking down stigma.","Deinstitutionalization — The mass movement of severely mentally ill people from state hospitals to outpatient care, closing many hospitals. Accelerated by Kennedy's 1963 Community Mental Health Act.","Revolving-door treatment — Repeated ED visits and inpatient admissions. Began in the 1960s: psychotropic drug development plus deinstitutionalization into communities that lacked services, leaving patients without the skills to function ind…","Assault — A threat to touch another person without consent. A verbal threat is enough. No contact has happened yet.","Battery — The actual harmful touching of another person: giving an injection to a patient who has refused the oral form. Contact is what separates it from assault.","Negligence — Failing to provide correct and adequate treatment.","False imprisonment — Restraining or confining a patient without cause.","Mental Health Parity — Insurers must give equal coverage for psychiatric illness as for physical health: deductibles, number of covered visits, and out-of-pocket expense. Also covers substance-related disorders (1996).","Acute care hospitalization — Highly structured, 24-hour locked setting that optimizes safety and addresses crisis intervention. Average stay 3–7 days, and about 3–5 days on an adult unit.","Long-term hospitalization — Where the client is referred if they need more than 7 days to stabilize.","Partial hospitalization (PHP) — Transitional step between inpatient and fully independent home life, for people needing some supervision but not hospitalization. About 6 hours of treatment, multiple days a week.","Day treatment — One step below PHP: structured activities and group for ongoing, usually chronic mental illness, so family members can work. 2–3 times a week for about a month.","Learning Outcomes","The continuum: well-being → emotional problems or concerns → mental illness. People move along it in both directions.","What contributes to mental health: biologic, psychological, and sociocultural influences. Characteristics include rational thinking, resiliency, self-esteem, self-awareness, emotional growth, and self-care.","Stigma: seeing someone in a negative way because of a characteristic, attribute, or personal trait. It worsens the course and outcomes of mental illness.","Counter stigma by talking openly about mental health, educating yourself and others, using person-first language, decriminalizing, pushing back on media portrayals, and advocating for reform.","Barriers to care: too few providers, transportation, two-to-three-month waits for outpatient follow-up, low income and cost, the limits of insurance parity, and stigma itself.","Admission types: voluntary: the client signs themselves in and consents. Involuntary: admitted against their will on a 72-hour hold, not counting weekends and holidays. Form 202A is filed by the APRN or MD for longer commitment.","Admission criteria for an acute unit: danger to self, danger to others, or unable to care for basic needs and/or gross impairment of judgment.","Least restrictive means, in order: assess → verbally intervene → reduce stimulation → provide diversion → PRN by mouth → PRN IM → escort to seclusion → restraints last.","Chemical restraint is less restrictive than physical or mechanical restraint, and PO is always offered before IM.","The therapeutic milieu, five components: containment, support, validation, structure, involvement.","Consumers is the term for individuals who have mental illness. The consumer movement demanded involvement in their own treatment decisions. NAMI (1979) is the largest grassroots organization, and advocates person-first language: not \"a schi…","Patient rights: to have or refuse visitors, to receive or refuse treatment, and rights regarding psychiatric advance directives. Informed consent means risks, benefits, and alternatives were given and treatment voluntarily accepted.","Capacity versus competency: capacity is the ability to make an informed decision. Competency is the legal term for the mental soundness required to decide or act.","Confidentiality breaks in exactly two circumstances: a specific threat to kill someone (duty to warn and protect), and suspected child or elder abuse.","The five components of the therapeutic milieu, \"Contain Some Very Stubborn Individuals\": Containment, Support, Validation, Structure, Involvement."]},{"s":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","a":"neurobiology","k":["Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Key Terms","★ The eight neurotransmitters — Inhibitory: serotonin, GABA. Excitatory: dopamine, glutamate, epinephrine, norepinephrine. Other: acetylcholine, histamine.","Amygdala — The emotional center of the brain: emotion, anxiety, fear, and pleasure. Part of the fight-or-flight system, alerting us to danger.","★ Neuroplasticity — The ability of the brain to change with learning by reorganizing neural pathways. Why repetition of a skill or a behavior change matters.","Synaptic pruning — The process that removes unnecessary or damaged neuronal structures, weeding out weaker, seldom-used synapses. Surges prenatally through age two, in the preteen years, and at midlife.","Discontinuation syndrome — A sudden decrease or stop of certain psychotropics. The original symptoms rebound, and new physical or psychological symptoms appear. Antidepressants are not addicting, but the body does get used to them.","Psychopharmacology — Start low and titrate up slowly · start only one new medication at a time · use the lowest effective dose · weigh risks against benefits · hold or switch for significant side effects · taper, never stop abruptly.","The eight neurotransmitters, \"Silent Guards Inhibit; Dangerous Gangs Excite Neighborhoods\": Serotonin and GABA inhibit. Dopamine, Glutamate, Epinephrine and Norepinephrine excite. The two left over are the \"other\" pair: acetylcholine and hi…","Learning Outcomes","★ Five structures: thalamus, hypothalamus, cerebellum, brainstem, amygdala.","Thalamus: the main relay station of the CNS. Hypothalamus: temperature, appetite, sleep, sex drive, heart rate, blood pressure.","Hippocampus: long- and short-term memory. Heavily damaged in chronic alcoholism, leading to Korsakoff syndrome and Wernicke's.","Illusion versus hallucination: in an illusion something really is there but is misperceived. In a hallucination nothing is.","Serotonin is decreased in depressive and anxiety disorders. Blocking its reuptake treats both very successfully.","GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Increase GABA to decrease anxiety. Decreased GABA: anxiety, mania, and schizophrenia.","Dopamine in excess produces auditory hallucinations and plays a role in schizophrenia. Glutamate is decreased in psychosis, the opposite pattern from dopamine.","Excess glutamate over-stimulates the brain and causes seizures. Excessive release leads to excitotoxicity and cell death.","Acetylcholine is decreased in Alzheimer's and sleep disorders, increased in depression.","Norepinephrine is decreased in depression, increased in mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia. Epinephrine works with it in fight-or-flight.","Imaging: structural (CT, MRI) shows anatomy. Functional (PET, SPECT, fMRI) shows activity and physiological change.","Over-pruning is one biological theory of schizophrenia, timed to the pruning surge in late adolescence and young adulthood.","Plasticity in children: young brains are more receptive to environmental input than old ones, so intervention succeeds more readily the younger the child. Rich sensory experiences and protective factors produce fewer maladaptive responses. …"]},{"s":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","a":"anger","k":["Anger, Aggression & Violence","Key Terms","Anger — An emotional response to frustration of desires, a threat to one's needs, or a challenge, from mild irritation to intense fury. A normal emotion, capable of being under personal control. A problem only when expressed aggressively.","Aggression — Verbal or physical attack intended to threaten or injure the victim's security or self-esteem. Virtually always designed to punish, but not always inappropriate, and sometimes necessary for self-protection.","Violence — Intentional use of force that results in, or could result in, injury to another. Always an objectionable act. That is what separates it from aggression.","Restraint — Any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material, or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body, or head freely.","Seclusion — Involuntary confinement of a patient alone in a room they are physically prevented from leaving. Less restrictive than restraints and never punitive. Only for violent or self-destructive behavior that jeopardizes immediate physi…","★ Critical incident debriefing — Immediate and mandatory after a restraint episode, with patient and staff.","Learning Outcomes","Four predictors of violence: history of violence, recent acts of violence, using profanity, hyperactivity.","Of those four: hyperactivity is the most important predictor of imminent violence. A history of violence is the single best predictor of future violence.","Precipitants: anxiety (catch it early), withdrawal and substance use, sleep deprivation, pain, feeling out of control, and poor communication.","Warning signs: pacing and restlessness, clenched jaws or fists, changes in speech rate or volume, verbal abuse, glaring or wholly avoided eye contact, and uncharacteristic withdrawal.","Aggression occurs most often in the context of limit setting.","The four levels: anxiety → loss of rationality (defensive) → hostility, abuse or aggression → tension reduction, the reachable and teachable moment.","De-escalation: speak slowly in shortened sentences with a low, calm voice. Offer two options to reduce powerlessness. Give feedback on what you see. Never yell.","Nurse safety: stay about one foot further than the patient can reach. Keep an escape route and never let the patient between you and the door. No dangling jewellery. Only one person talks.","Restraint hard rules: never prone, never a PRN or standing order, order and face-to-face assessment within one hour, two fingers under the restraint.","What silently counts as a restraint: four side rails up (except seizure precautions), sheets tucked so tightly the patient cannot move, holding someone against their will.","Seclusion is tried before restraint. Both are a last resort, only when the patient is a danger to self or others and less restrictive interventions have been found ineffective.","A restrained patient is never left alone. Remove restraints and do range of motion on one limb at a time, and always place a wedge pillow under the head of anyone restrained in bed.","Six contraindications to seclusion and restraint: extremely unstable medical or psychiatric conditions, COPD, spinal injury, seizure disorders, pregnancy, delirium or dementia.","The six contraindications, \"Pregnant Patients Should Simply Delay Cuffs\": Pregnancy, Psychiatric or medical instability, Spinal injury, Seizure disorders, Delirium or dementia, COPD."]},{"s":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","a":"therapies","k":["Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Key Terms","★ The five behavioral therapies — Modeling = role play · operant conditioning = tokens and positive reinforcement · systematic desensitization = relaxation plus graded exposure · aversion therapy = negative stimulus · biofeedback = control …","Interpersonal therapy (IPT) — Targets relationships by improving functioning and communication patterns. Very short-term: 12 to 16 sessions.","Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) — Our thoughts cause our feelings and behaviors, not external things like people or situations. Change the way we think and we change the way we feel, and act better even if the situation does not change. …","Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) — The integration of opposites: cognitive and behavioral techniques combined with mindfulness. Developed to treat chronically suicidal individuals, many of whom had borderline personality disorder.","The five behavioral therapies, \"My Old Son Ate Bugs\": Modeling, Operant conditioning, Systematic desensitization, Aversion therapy, Biofeedback.","Learning Outcomes","Purpose of the PMH assessment: establish rapport, understand the current problem, review physical status, assess risk and safety, assess psychosocial status, identify mutual goals, formulate the plan of care.","Depressed mood obliges a suicide assessment. Ask directly: \"Are you depressed?\" and then \"Do you feel that you might hurt yourself?\"","Affect versus mood: affect is what you observe. Mood is the emotional state in the person's own words.","Affect range, most to least expressive: full/broad, constricted, blunted, flat. Flat resembles the masking seen in Parkinson's.","Affect congruency: congruent when mood and affect match. Incongruent when they do not: \"I'm doing great,\" said while crying.","Mood terms: euthymic (normal), dysthymic (chronically low), elated or euphoric, labile (up and down), cyclothymic (cycling).","Psychosocial interventions are non-pharmacological techniques addressing the societal, familial, and cultural aspects: education, positive self-talk, self-monitoring, limit setting, relaxation, spiritual support, and discharge planning.","The three IPT techniques: identification of emotion, expression of emotion, and dealing with emotional baggage carried from past relationships.","Aversion therapy example: disulfiram (Antabuse) in alcohol dependence: drinking on it causes nausea, dizziness, and flushing.","DBT's four skill sets: emotional regulation, interpersonal effectiveness, distress tolerance, mindfulness.","DBT's four modes of treatment: group skills training, individual psychotherapy, telephone contact, and therapist consultation. It takes one to two years before the best results show."]},{"s":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","a":"group-physio","k":["Group & Physiologic Therapies","Key Terms","Group — Two or more people pursuing common goals or interests, benefiting by mutually giving and receiving feedback. Patient and family education counts as leading a group.","Phototherapy (light therapy) — A fluorescent light box with a strong UV filter, about 10,000 lux, set off to the side. 30 to 45 minutes a day, usually in the morning. First-line for major depressive disorder with a seasonal pattern.","Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) — The most effective treatment for depression, and non-invasive. Done under anesthesia with a muscle relaxant. Two to three treatments a week, 6 to 12 in total.","Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) — Non-invasive MRI-strength magnetic pulses to focal areas of the cerebral cortex. FDA approved for treatment-resistant depression. Outpatient, 30 minutes, five days a week for four to six weeks. The …","Vagus nerve stimulation (VNS) — Invasive: a pulse generator in the chest wired around the left cervical vagus nerve, pulsing 30 seconds every 5 minutes. FDA approved for partial onset seizures and depression.","Deep brain stimulation (DBS) — The most invasive and very seldom used: electrodes implanted in the chest, threaded up the neck, with probes placed into the brain. A Parkinson's treatment now investigated in depression.","Herbal treatments — Not regulated by the FDA, and concentrations vary between brands. St. John's Wort for mild-to-moderate depression · ginseng for stress or fatigue · valerian root and chamomile · SAM-e.","Exercise — Alters serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine, and dampens the HPA axis. Less expensive than ECT, with fewer side effects than medication.","Learning Outcomes","★ The seven physiological treatments: phototherapy, ECT, VNS, TMS, DBS, herbal treatments, exercise.","★ Task roles: initiator-contributor, information seeker, information giver, coordinator, orienteer, recorder. Building and maintenance roles: encourager, harmonizer, gatekeeper, group observer, follower. Individual roles are the non-product…","Group characteristics: size, defined purpose, degree of similarity among members, rules, boundaries, and content and process.","Who can lead what: an RN may lead activity, educational, task, and support groups. Group psychotherapy requires an advanced degree, because it aims at personality change.","Psychoeducational groups teach content: medication or health education, dual diagnosis, symptom management, recreational activity, interpersonal skills. The therapeutic community meeting is the essential inpatient group.","Advantages of group therapy: engages several clients at once, members benefit from others' feedback and life experience, a safe setting to try new behaviors, and promotes belonging.","Disadvantages: time constraints, confidentiality cannot be guaranteed, and disruptive members. Someone acutely psychotic, manic, or intoxicated does not benefit from group.","Leadership styles: autocratic (leader exerts control, little interaction), democratic (extensive interaction in problem-solving), laissez-faire (members set the direction).","Rule out bipolar disorder before starting light therapy. It can trigger mania or hypomania.","ECT: an electrical current lasting 1 second or less produces a brief seizure of 30 to 60 seconds. One extremity is left un-paralyzed so the convulsion can be observed.","ECT is contraindicated in brain tumors and subdural hematoma. The heart is heavily stressed at the onset of the seizure and for up to ten minutes after.","After ECT: wakes about 15 minutes later, confusion and disorientation for several hours, and retrograde amnesia for the events leading up to and including the treatment.","TMS's one contraindication is metal in the area of stimulation: brain stimulators, cochlear implants, medication pumps, bullet fragments. Also not used with a pacemaker or a history of seizures.","VNS side effects: voice alteration in nearly 60%, plus hoarseness, headache, neck pain, cough, paresthesia, and dyspnea, all decreasing with time."]},{"s":"Therapeutic Communication","a":"therapeutic-communication","k":["Therapeutic Communication","Key Terms","Reflecting — Directing ideas, feelings, questions, or content back to the patient. Encourages them to accept their own ideas and feelings, acknowledges their right to hold opinions and make decisions, and encourages them to think of themsel…","Seeking clarification — Helping patients clarify their own thoughts and maximizing mutual understanding between nurse and patient. \"I'm not sure I understood that,\" or \"What would you say is the main point of what you just said?\"","Learning Outcomes","The therapeutic techniques: listening, maintaining silence, maintaining neutral responses, broad openings and open-ended questions, focusing and refocusing, restating, clarifying and validating, sharing perceptions, reflecting, providing ac…","The barriers: giving advice, which terminates the conversation and fosters dependency by implying the patient cannot decide for themselves · giving approval or disapproval · asking \"why,\" which is heard as accusatory · any judgmental statem…","The most therapeutic response focuses on feelings and encourages the widest range of patient responses, making the patient an active participant in the conversation.","To begin re-socialization, start with the patient's own interests: what they enjoyed in the past. Transportation, money, and who they would go with are relevant, but they come after."]},{"s":"Depressive Disorders","a":"depressive","k":["Depressive Disorders","Key Terms","Lability — Rapid, exaggerated shifts in emotional expression: the mood switches back and forth, typically every one or two hours.","Anhedonia — Loss of interest or pleasure in almost all activities. From an (\"without\") + hedone (\"pleasure\"). One of the two symptoms at least one of which must be present for major depressive disorder.","Psychomotor retardation — Visible slowing of physical activity: movement or speech. Slow talking, long pauses before speaking, taking a long time to cross a room, slow chewing. Associated with severe depression.","Psychomotor agitation — An increase in activity brought on by mental tension. Restlessness, pacing, tapping fingers or feet, abruptly starting and stopping tasks. Associated with agitated depression.","Learning Outcomes","Etiological theories and contributing factors: biological and genetic vulnerability, neurotransmitter dysregulation, cognitive theory, learned helplessness, and stressors that differ across the lifespan.","The four depressive disorders, and how to tell them apart: major depressive disorder, disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, premenstrual dysphoric disorder, persistent depressive disorder. Know the symptoms, treatments, and nursing care o…","★ Learn the medications by classification first, then the drugs inside each. Most of a class shares its side effects. SSRIs and SNRIs group together as serotonin reuptake inhibitors. Know the side effects and what to watch for.","★ Serotonin syndrome: what causes it, its symptoms, and how to treat it. Untreated it can be fatal.","★ Hypertensive crisis: what causes it, its symptoms, its treatment, and how it is prevented.","★ Anticholinergic effects: what they are, what causes them, and how they are treated.","Antidepressant principles: 4–6 weeks to symptom improvement. Physiological symptoms improve before psychological ones, which is the window of highest suicide risk. Medication continues 6–9 months past symptom relief. Not addictive but never…","★ Symptoms of depression to recognize: hopelessness, sadness, worthlessness, shame, anhedonia, anxiety, self-harming behavior, and feeling unable to keep oneself safe.","★ Apply the nursing process and clinical judgment model to the care of individuals with depressive disorders."]},{"s":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","a":"bipolar","k":["Bipolar & Related Disorders","Key Terms","Mania — At least 1 week of elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased activity or energy, causing marked impairment in functioning or requiring hospitalization. Psychotic features may be present.","Hypomania — At least 4 consecutive days of the same change, uncharacteristic of the person and observable by others, but not severe enough to cause marked impairment or require hospitalization. No psychotic features. Any psychosis makes the…","Rapid cycling — Four or more manic episodes for at least 2 weeks in 12 months, with partial or full remission for 2 months at a time or a switch to the opposite pole. Not the same as mood lability, which shifts every one or two hours.","Learning Outcomes","★ Signs and symptoms of bipolar I and bipolar II. Bipolar I: at least one manic episode, the most severe form, highest mortality. Bipolar II: at least one hypomanic plus at least one major depressive episode, never a manic one.","Distinguish a manic from a hypomanic episode: 1 week versus at least 4 consecutive days, marked impairment or hospitalization versus neither, psychotic features possible versus none.","★ The common medications used for bipolar disorders: lithium carbonate, the anticonvulsant mood stabilizers, the second-generation antipsychotics, and benzodiazepines for severe agitation.","Distinguish early from severe lithium toxicity: therapeutic 0.6–1.4 mEq/L. 1.5–2.0 early toxicity, withhold the dose and draw a level. 2.0–2.5 advanced, hospitalize. Over 2.5 severe, hemodialysis.","★ Side effects of the bipolar medications, including the lamotrigine rash, which can be fatal if the drug is not stopped.","★ Treating an acute manic episode is not the same as maintenance treatment. An acute episode gets something heavier added on top, usually an antipsychotic, which comes back off once the mania is controlled.","Nursing care for an individual with a bipolar disorder. Through the first days of acute mania the focus is degree of exhaustion and nutrition. The primary outcome is injury prevention."]},{"s":"Suicide","a":"suicide","k":["Suicide","Key Terms","Suicidal ideation — Thinking about, considering, or planning to kill oneself.","Suicidal plan — The organized plan and time frame for killing oneself.","Suicide intent — At the time of the injury or death, the person had thoughts of killing themselves or wished to die.","Suicide attempt — A non-fatal self-directed potentially injurious behavior with any intent to die.","Suicide / death by suicide — The voluntary and intentional act of taking one's own life. Also called fatal self-directed violence. All three describe the same thing.","Suicidal self-directed violence — Behavior that is self-directed and deliberately results in injury or the potential for injury to oneself, with evidence of suicidal intent.","Non-suicidal self-directed violence — The same behavior, but with no evidence of suicidal intent.","Interrupted self-directed violence, by other — The person takes steps to injure themselves but is stopped by another person before the fatal injury.","Interrupted self-directed violence, by self — The person takes steps to injure themselves but stops themselves before the fatal injury.","Suicide contagion — An increase in suicides when stories about suicide increase, are reported at length or prominently, concern a broadly known person, or carry a dramatic headline. Detailing a method increases the use of that method.","Suicide survivor — The family and friends grieving a loved one's suicide. Not the person who attempted.","Learning Outcomes","The unacceptable terms: \"committed suicide,\" \"successful\" or \"failed\" attempt, \"completed suicide.\" Say died by suicide and suicide attempt instead.","Components of a comprehensive risk assessment, screening, and safety planning: the C-SSRS, SAFE-T, and the Stanley-Brown safety plan.","Risk factors, protective factors, and the warning signs. Warning signs include giving away prized possessions or pets, looking for the means, increasing substance use, and withdrawing from friends and family.","Key elements of suicide precautions and environmental safety in the hospital: remove sharps, belts and dangerous articles. Search belongings and do a full skin assessment on entry. Levels of observation. Ask the patient to report suicidal t…","Treatment and management using basic-level interventions, and how to promote recovery in a patient status post attempt. Encourage verbalization of feelings rather than avoiding the attempt.","Short-term and long-term management, including pharmacological interventions: low-dose lithium, about 25 mg PO daily for a few weeks, reduces suicidal ideation quickly.","★ Safety is the priority on any question where suicide risk is in play. Ask about it directly and before anything else. Asking does not increase the risk or plant the idea."]},{"s":"Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","a":"nssi","k":["Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","Learning Outcomes","Define NSSI: deliberate, self-inflicted destruction or injury of body tissue without suicidal intent. That absence of intent is the whole distinction from a suicide attempt.","Epidemiology, risk factors, comorbidity, and clinical picture. DSM-5 criteria: at least 5 days in the past year, not necessarily consecutive. Done to relieve negative feelings, resolve an interpersonal difficulty, or induce a positive state…","Nursing care: establish a therapeutic relationship, care for the wounds, and build healthy coping mechanisms to use instead of self-harm. Assess along four lines: what kind of injury, how often, what triggers it, and what has helped before.","Biological treatments and psychological therapies: CBT and DBT, both successful inpatient or outpatient, plus group therapy and psychopharmacology."]},{"s":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","a":"sud","k":["Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","Key Terms","Blood alcohol concentration (BAC) — Alcohol intoxication is 80 to 100 mg ethanol per deciliter of blood (mg/dL), also written 0.08 to 0.10 g/dL. The Kentucky DUI threshold is 0.08.","Countertransference — When a healthcare worker unconsciously displaces feelings related to significant figures in their own past onto the patient. Prevented by self-assessment of one's own attitudes about substance use.","Delirium tremens (DTs) — The most severe form of alcohol withdrawal: a medical emergency that can result in death. Appears any time within the first 72 hours after the last drink. Hepatitis or pancreatitis increases the likelihood.","Wernicke encephalopathy — The acute, reversible first stage of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, from thiamine (B1) deficiency. Confusion, ataxia, double vision, droopy eyelid, nystagmus. Treated with large IV thiamine doses 2–3 times daily for …","Korsakoff syndrome — The chronic, debilitating and not typically reversible second stage. Adds severe, irreversible memory impairment, confabulation, hallucinations and repetitious speech. Thiamine replacement for 3–12 months, with a recove…","Dual diagnosis — Co-occurring mental illness and a substance or addictive disorder. Distinct from comorbidity, which is any two or more co-occurring chronic disorders.","SBIRT — Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment: early intervention and treatment for people with a substance use disorder in general, not alcohol alone, and those at risk. Usable by any healthcare professional in any setti…","AUDIT — Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test, a 10-item WHO tool covering alcohol consumption, drinking behaviors and alcohol-related problems. Used for adults. CRAFFT is the adolescent tool.","CRAFFT — Six questions for ages 12–21: Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Family or friends, Trouble. A score of 2 or more needs further evaluation. 4 or more suggests dependence.","CAGE — Cut down · Annoyed by criticism · Guilty about drinking · Eye-opener, a morning drink. A score of 2 or more is significant. A score of 1 requires further assessment.","CAGE-AID — The same four questions Adapted to Include Drugs, not alcohol alone.","Learning Outcomes","★ Differentiate the signs and symptoms of the two main withdrawal syndromes, and know how to treat each. Opioid withdrawal: tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, enlarged pupils, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, piloerection, muscle spasms, bone…","★ Know the opioid withdrawal symptoms on the COWS: resting pulse rate, sweating, restlessness, pupil size, bone or joint aches, runny nose or tearing, GI upset, tremor, yawning, anxiety or irritability, gooseflesh skin.","★ Explain the symptoms, progression and management of Wernicke's encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome: both stages, caused by thiamine (B1) deficiency, the first reversible and the second not.","★ Know these eleven medications: acamprosate, diazepam, disulfiram, lorazepam, thiamine, clonidine, naloxone, buprenorphine, buprenorphine & naloxone, methadone, naltrexone.","Describe addiction, withdrawal, countertransference and intoxication as they relate to substance use and nursing care.","Demonstrate non-stigmatizing, person-first language in care and documentation — \"a person with alcohol use disorder,\" never \"an alcoholic.\" \"A person with a substance use disorder,\" never \"an addict.\"","Identify common risk factors: genetic predisposition, neurotransmitter disruption, brain structure and function, immature adolescent brain development, the craving and relapse cycle, tolerance and withdrawal. Plus chronic stressors, socioec…","Describe the neurobiological process and the neurotransmitters involved: the mesolimbic reward pathway: dopamine surge up to 5–10 times normal, reinforcement, craving, repeat use. Dopamine is most central. Serotonin, GABA, glutamate and end…","Discuss clinical manifestations of use, intoxication, withdrawal, tolerance, overdose and treatment. Opioid overdose is an emergency. The cardinal signs are coma, pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression. Give naloxone and repeat if signs…","Identify pharmacologic and psychological treatments for withdrawal and recovery. Alcohol withdrawal: diazepam or lorazepam at a CIWA-Ar of 8–10 or more. Recovery: naltrexone, disulfiram and acamprosate, none of which treats withdrawal. Opio…","Discuss the use of the CIWA and the COWS. COWS assesses opioid withdrawal. CIWA assesses sedative-hypnotic and alcohol withdrawal, scoring nausea/vomiting, tremors, anxiety, agitation, paroxysmal sweats, headache, tactile, auditory and visu…","Describe the interdisciplinary approach: SBIRT, recovery supports and collaborative care. The care continuum runs detoxification, rehabilitation, halfway houses, partial hospitalization, intensive outpatient programs, outpatient treatment, …","Apply the clinical judgment measurement model to individuals with substance use and addictive disorders: recognize and analyze cues through screening and assessment, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action, evaluate outcomes.…"]},{"s":"Personality Disorders","a":"personality","k":["Personality Disorders","Key Terms","Cluster A — Odd or eccentric. Paranoid · schizoid · schizotypal. Mnemonic: \"PaSS on the party.\"","Cluster B — Dramatic, emotional, antagonistic and erratic. Borderline · antisocial · histrionic · narcissistic. Mnemonic: \"BAHNed from the party.\"","Cluster C — Anxious or fearful. Dependent · obsessive-compulsive · avoidant. Mnemonic: \"the party is DOA.\"","Learning Outcomes","Define personality disorder: an enduring pattern of deviant inner experience and behavior that differs from cultural expectations, is pervasive, inflexible and stable, and leads to distress or impairment, with dysfunction in cognition, impu…","Identify the clusters: ten disorders in three clusters: A eccentric, B erratic, C anxious.","Identify the differentiating characteristics of each type. Paranoid: longstanding distrust, grudges, vindictiveness. Schizoid: detached loner who does not want company. Schizotypal: magical thinking and ideas of reference they can be made a…","Discuss etiological theories: genetic: traits are inherited but the disorders are not. Neurobiological: neurotransmitters influence temperament. Psychosocial and environmental: learned maladaptive responses, neglect, rejection, divorce, tra…","Describe treatment and nursing care: restructuring personality takes years of psychotherapy, so short-term work on problem-solving, social skills and coping is used. Psychopharmacology, individual therapy, DBT, family education and therapy,…","Discuss the factors behind variable prognosis: personality disorders are treatable and people keep growing and changing, but prognosis depends on the degree of impairment and the person's motivation. Many do not believe a problem exists, or…","Describe splitting: a defense mechanism, seen commonly in borderline personality disorder, in which the person cannot integrate and accept both positive and negative feelings, so people, including themselves, and situations are either all g…"]},{"s":"Eating & Feeding Disorders","a":"eating","k":["Eating & Feeding Disorders","Key Terms","Eating disorders — Eating consistently below or above the caloric need to maintain a healthy weight, occurring without hunger or failing to produce satiety, and resulting in physiologic imbalances or medical complications.","Anorexia nervosa (AN) — Life-threatening. Intense fear of weight gain, a severely distorted body image, and restriction of calories relative to requirements with a significantly low BMI.","Bulimia nervosa (BN) — Recurrent episodes of uncontrollable binge eating followed by compensatory behavior to avoid weight gain through purging, laxatives, diuretics or excessive exercise.","Binge-eating disorder (BED) — Recurrent binge eating with marked distress and impaired control, and no recurrent compensatory behavior. The most common eating disorder.","Binge eating — Eating, within a 2-hour period, an amount definitely larger than most people would eat in that time, with a sense of lack of control.","Purging — Compensatory behavior to avoid weight gain: self-induced vomiting, or misuse of laxatives, diuretics or enemas.","Satiety — Comfortable fullness.","Body mass index (BMI) — The gauge used to determine the severity of anorexia nervosa. Mild 17 or more · moderate 16 to 17 · severe 15 to 16 · extreme under 15.","Amenorrhea — Loss of menstrual periods in girls and women past puberty.","Lanugo — Downy growth of body hair on the face and back, seen in anorexia nervosa.","Russell's Sign — Calluses or scars on the back of the hands and knuckles caused by self-induced vomiting.","Refeeding syndrome — A serious and potentially fatal condition that can occur during refeeding, caused by sudden shifts in the electrolytes that help the body metabolize food.","Pica — Ingestion of substances that have no nutritional value, such as dirt or paint.","Rumination disorder — Undigested food returned to the mouth, then rechewed, reswallowed or spit out.","Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder — Food avoidance causing significant weight loss, nutritional deficiency, dependence on supplements or enteral feeding, and marked interference with functioning. Often tied to the sensory qualities …","Learning Outcomes","Compare and contrast the physical characteristics of the three eating disorders. Anorexia: significantly low BMI, amenorrhea, lanugo, mottled cool skin, peripheral edema, low blood pressure, pulse and temperature, decreased bone density. Bu…","Compare and contrast the epidemiology of the three. Anorexia: lifetime prevalence 0.5%, ratio 3:1 female to male, onset in adolescence or young adulthood. Bulimia: 2.3% of women and 0.5% of men, ratio 10:1, onset later adolescence with a me…","Compare and contrast the signs, symptoms and risk factors. All three share dieting, body dissatisfaction, low self-esteem, perfectionism, impulsivity, a history of abuse and comorbid anxiety or depression. What separates them is whether com…","Distinguish the two types of anorexia nervosa, assigned on the last 3 months of behavior. Restricting type: weight loss through dieting, fasting and excessive exercise, with no regular binge eating or purging. Binge-eating and purging type:…","State the DSM-5 criteria for each disorder. Bulimia and binge-eating disorder both require episodes at least once a week for 3 months. Binge-eating disorder additionally requires three or more of: eating much more rapidly than normal, eatin…","Describe the biological factors. Genetic and familial predisposition in both anorexia and bulimia, with genetic correlations to major depressive disorder, anxiety disorders, OCD and schizophrenia. Altered brain serotonin function dysregulat…","Describe the cognitive and environmental factors. Cognitively, anorexia is ego-syntonic: the person knows the actions are harmful but believes the benefits outweigh the harm. Both involve poor emotional regulation and low distress tolerance…","Describe the criteria for hospitalization. For anorexia: extreme electrolyte imbalance or weight below 75% of ideal body weight, less than 10% body fat, daytime heart rate under 50, systolic blood pressure under 90, temperature under 96 deg…","Describe the biological treatments. Anorexia has no FDA-approved medication, and fluoxetine helps obsessive-compulsive behavior only after maintenance weight is reached. Fluoxetine is the only FDA-approved medication for bulimia in adults. …","Describe the psychological therapies. CBT is first-line for bulimia and is most effective combined with fluoxetine. Binge-eating disorder uses individual or group CBT, DBT and support groups. Anorexia has no empirical support for any specif…","Describe the three feeding disorders. Pica: ingestion of non-nutritive substances, usually beginning in early childhood, treated by monitoring eating behavior and rewarding appropriate eating. Rumination disorder: food returned to the mouth…"]},{"s":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","a":"schizophrenia","k":["Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","Key Terms","Psychosis — Experiencing hallucinations, delusions, or disorganized thoughts, speech or behavior.","Paranoia — Irrational fear ranging from mild to profound, with deep mistrust or suspicion of others.","Delusions — False beliefs held despite a lack of evidence, not corrected by reasoning.","Persecutory — Belief that one is being watched, plotted against or ridiculed.","Referential — Belief that unconnected events are somehow related to you.","Grandiose — Belief that one is a powerful or important person.","Erotomanic — Belief that another person desires you romantically.","Nihilistic — The delusion of nonexistence: the mind, body or world no longer exists.","Somatic — Belief that the body is changing in unusual ways.","Control — Belief that an external force controls your thoughts, feelings, impulses or behavior.","Hallucinations — Perceiving a sensory experience for which no external source exists. Can involve all five senses.","Auditory — Hearing voices or sounds. The most common type.","Command — Auditory hallucinations that instruct the patient to act in a specific way, from innocuous to dangerous. Suicidal or homicidal commands require immediate safety measures.","Visual · olfactory · gustatory · tactile — Seeing people or things · smelling odors · experiencing tastes · feeling body sensations.","Circumstantiality — Extremely detailed, lengthy conversation that eventually reaches the point.","Tangentiality — Wandering off topic and never reaching the point.","Looseness of association — Absence of the normal connectedness of thoughts, ideas and topics.","Flight of ideas — Topic changes repeatedly and rapidly with only superficial connections.","Echolalia — Pathological repetition of another's words.","Clang association — Choosing words by sound rather than meaning, often a rhyme.","Pressured speech — Urgent or intense speech, with reluctance to allow comments from others.","Word salad — A string of totally unconnected words, meaningless to a listener.","Neologisms — Made-up words with meaning for the patient only.","Thought blocking — Reduction or stoppage of thought, losing the thread mid-sentence.","Thought insertion — Belief that someone else has inserted thoughts into the patient's brain.","Thought deletion — Belief that thoughts have been taken or are missing.","Catatonia — Pronounced increase or decrease in the rate and amount of movement. Excessive motor activity is purposeless.","Echopraxia — Involuntary imitation of another's movements and gestures.","Motor retardation — Pronounced slowing of movement.","Motor agitation — Excited behavior such as running or pacing rapidly, often in response to stimuli.","Negativism — Tendency to resist or oppose the requests or wishes of others.","Stereotyped behaviors — Repetitive, purposeless movements peculiar to the person.","Gesturing or posturing — Posture held in a fixed position.","Impaired impulse control — Reduced ability to resist impulses.","Boundary impairment — Impaired ability to sense where one's body or influence ends and another's begins.","Affective blunting — Reduced or constricted affect.","Alogia — Reduction in speech, or poverty of speech.","Anhedonia — Reduced ability or inability to experience pleasure or joy.","Apathy — Decreased interest in activities or beliefs that would otherwise matter.","Asociality — Decreased desire for social interaction, or discomfort during it.","Avolition — Reduced or lost motivation or goal-directed behavior.","Anosognosia — Inability to realize one is ill, caused by the illness itself. Leads to resistance to or cessation of treatment, often combined with paranoia.","Concrete thinking — Impaired ability to think abstractly, interpreting things literally.","Impaired memory — Impacts short-term memory and the ability to learn.","Impaired information processing — Delayed responses, misperceptions or difficulty understanding others.","Impaired executive functioning — Difficulty reasoning, setting priorities, comparing options and planning.","Affective symptoms — Altered experience and expression of emotions. Mood may be unstable, erratic, labile or incongruent.","Extrapyramidal side effects (EPS) — Serious side effects affecting motor control and coordination. The first three begin within a few weeks of starting or increasing a medication.","Acute dystonic reactions — Sudden, sustained contraction of muscle groups, usually head and neck. Airway involvement is an emergency.","Akathisia — Motor restlessness: pacing, rocking, inability to stay still. Easily mistaken for anxiety or agitation.","Pseudoparkinsonism — Temporary symptoms resembling Parkinson's disease: stiff stooped posture, shuffling gait, bradykinesia, pill-rolling, resting tremor.","Tardive dyskinesia (TD) — Involuntary rhythmic movements with long-term antipsychotic treatment, usually oral and facial. Assess with the AIMS at least every 3 months.","Anticholinergic side effects — Dry mouth, blurred vision, dry eyes, constipation, urinary retention, drowsiness, confusion, tachycardia, skin flushing. Can progress to toxicity.","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) — Rare, serious and potentially fatal, usually early in therapy. Severe muscle rigidity, altered mental status, temperature over 103 degrees Fahrenheit, hypertension, tachycardia, tachypnea, diaphoresis,…","Metabolic syndrome — Weight gain, dyslipidemia, increased blood glucose and insulin resistance. Of special concern with atypical antipsychotics.","Learning Outcomes","★ Know which symptoms each classification treats. Typical antipsychotics primarily treat the positive symptoms and have little effect on the negative symptoms. Atypical antipsychotics treat both positive and negative symptoms.","★ Know the medications that fall under each classification, and the side effects and adverse reactions typical of that classification. Typical: chlorpromazine, haloperidol, and haloperidol decanoate as a long-acting injection. Atypical: clo…","★ Know how to treat or manage the medication side effects and adverse reactions. Acute dystonia: monitor and act emergently if the airway is involved. Akathisia: reduce the dose or change medication, add benztropine, and propranolol, loraze…","★ Agranulocytosis is a side effect of taking clozapine. A reduction in circulating granulocytes that limits the ability to fight infection. Life-threatening, greatest risk in the first months, monitor WBC weekly for the first 18 weeks, and …","Discuss the proposed etiologies of schizophrenia: genetics, environmental factors, brain chemistry and substance use, understood through the vulnerability-stress model. Prevalence is 1% to 1.1% worldwide, about 3.5 million in the United Sta…","Differentiate the positive symptoms, which exist but should not be there: alterations in perception (paranoia, delusions, hallucinations), speech, thought and behavior.","Differentiate the negative symptoms, which should be there but are not: affective blunting, alogia, anhedonia, apathy, asociality and avolition. They are a serious problem because they amount to the absence of essential human qualities.","Differentiate the cognitive and affective symptoms. Cognitive: anosognosia, concrete thinking, impaired memory, impaired information processing and impaired executive functioning. Affective: an altered experience and expression of emotion, …","State the DSM-5 criteria for schizophrenia: two or more characteristic symptoms for a significant portion of a 1-month period, at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech, with functioning markedly below the previous…","Name the phases: prodromal, with mild changes appearing 1 to 12 months before the first full episode · acute, which can last several months even with treatment · stabilization, moving toward the previous level of functioning · maintenance o…","Discuss schizoaffective disorder symptoms and treatments. Symptoms of schizophrenia plus a mood disorder, with lifetime prevalence 0.3%. Bipolar type has manic and depressive episodes, depressive type has depression only. The distinguishing…","Discuss care considerations. Priority problems are led by safety: risk for violence toward others, risk for suicide or self-harm, disturbed thought processes and sensory perception, risk for non-adherence. Outcomes are specific and measurab…","Explain why non-adherence matters. 80% to 90% relapse during the course of the illness, each relapse takes longer to recover from, and stopping medication almost certainly leads to relapse, with 80% relapsing within 2 years. Combining medic…","Intervene with delusions and hallucinations. Never debate, argue or try to disprove a delusion, which intensifies it and destroys trust. Focus on the underlying fear and on helping the patient feel safe, validate what is real and present re…"]},{"s":"Week 1","a":"not-tested-week1","k":["Week 1","Prevalence and incidence statistics, unless a specific one is called out.","How to score the Brøset or the DASA, or what is on either tool. Know only that structured tools exist and are used.","The specific monitoring intervals for a patient in seclusion or restraint.","The history of psychiatric mental health nursing: Dorothea Dix, 1837, 1882, 1946, 1973.","Yalom's therapeutic factors.","The phases of group development.","Rational emotive therapy.","The ten cognitive distortions. The term itself still appears as one of the concepts CBT works on.","The cerebral lobes and what damage to each produces. The core structures (thalamus, hypothalamus, cerebellum, brainstem, amygdala, hippocampus) are still tested."]},{"s":"Week 2","a":"not-tested-week2","k":["Week 2","★ Cyclothymic disorder. Focus on bipolar I and bipolar II. Cyclothymia will not be tested. The term and its definition still stand on the topic page.","Table 14.1, the medical conditions and substances associated with major depressive disorder. The chart does not have to be memorized."]},{"s":"Week 3","a":"not-tested-week3","k":["Week 3","★ Scoring the COWS or the CIWA. The withdrawal symptoms on both tools are tested. Producing a score is not.","★ The full DSM-5 criteria list for alcohol use disorder. The chart does not have to be memorized, but the diagnosis still requires at least 2 of 11 criteria in a 12-month period, and the severity bands (mild 2–3, moderate 4–5, severe 6 or m…","The national substance use prevalence figures: the 2023 National Survey on Drug Use and Health totals for substance use disorder, alcohol use disorder, drug use disorder and opioid misuse. The slide carrying them is marked not exam material…"]},{"s":"Week 4","a":"not-tested-week4","k":["Week 4","★ What reality testing is. The interventions that improve it are still taught.","★ That typical antipsychotics are dopamine antagonists. What is tested is that they primarily treat positive symptoms and have little effect on negative symptoms.","★ That atypical antipsychotics are serotonin and dopamine antagonists. What is tested is that they treat both positive and negative symptoms.","★ The first, second and third generation framing. Focus on the classification, typical and atypical, not the generations."]}]},{"f":"medications.html","t":"Medications","secs":[{"s":"Medications","a":"","k":["Exam Prep"]},{"s":"Antidepressant Principles","a":"w2-antidepressant-principles","k":["Antidepressant Principles","All antidepressants increase the availability of neurotransmitters and have similar efficacy in trials. They differ in adverse effects, cost, and safety. Psychotherapy combined with an antidepressant is superior to either alone.","Physiological symptoms improve before psychological symptoms. Energy returns while mood is still depressed, and that is exactly when the risk of acting on suicidal thoughts is highest. Teach patients, caregivers, and family members this.","General Education","Symptom improvement may not appear for 4–6 weeks.","Look first for improved sleep, less daytime fatigue, less crying and irritability, and increased frustration tolerance.","Side effects are usually handled by adjusting the dose or switching to another drug in the same class.","Do not stop when feeling better. That causes relapse. Medication is needed 6–9 months past symptom relief, so most patients take it 12–24 months before tapering.","Antidepressants are not addictive, but stopping abruptly causes withdrawal: nausea, anxiety, insomnia, flu-like symptoms.","No alcohol. Alcohol is a stronger depressant than any available antidepressant.","In Children & Teens","SSRIs carry a black box warning for increased risk of suicidality: suicidal thinking or attempts.","After the warning, prescribing fell and suicide attempts increased: the risk of suicide is greater in depressed children and adolescents who do not take antidepressants.","fluoxetine is first-line. Venlafaxine is used off label. Both have low side effects: mild nausea, headache, stomachache.","Full effect can take up to 12 weeks. Usually given for 6–24 months.","TCAs are avoided: more side effects, potential for dysrhythmias, lethal in overdose, and children and adolescents can be more impulsive.","Order of Drug Classes Tried","SSRIs / SRIs","First-line. If one fails, a second drug in the same class is tried before moving on.","Tricyclics (TCAs)","Second choice. Cheaper, but anticholinergic, cardiotoxic, and lethal in overdose.","MAOIs","Third choice. Reserved for treatment-resistant depression because of the food and drug restrictions."]},{"s":"SSRIs & SNRIs","a":"w2-ssri","k":["SSRIs & SNRIs","Drugs","SSRIs, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors: fluoxetine · paroxetine · sertraline · citalopram · escitalopram","SNaRIs, serotonin noradrenergic reuptake inhibitors: venlafaxine · duloxetine","SNRI, serotonin norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor: desvenlafaxine","Indication — First-line therapy for depression. Block the neuronal uptake of serotonin, leaving more in the synaptic cleft. Good for depression with anxiety or with psychomotor agitation. Also treat anxiety and obsessive-compulsive disorder…","Side Effects & What to Do — Dry mouth: increase fluids, sugar-free gum. Sexual problems: reduced desire, difficulty reaching orgasm, or inability to maintain an erection, in up to 80%. Headache and blurred vision: symptomatic care. These fo…","Adverse Events — Serotonin syndrome: rare but potentially life-threatening. See below. Abrupt discontinuation may be mild or severe: \"electrical surges\" or \"brain shivers\" in the head or body, pins-and-needles sensations on the skin, blacko…","Monitoring & Teaching — Effectiveness takes 4–8 weeks. Most side effects diminish in 4–6 weeks. Monitor for suicidal ideation, extreme agitation, fever, increased blood pressure, and manic symptoms. Teach sleep hygiene and relaxation techni…","Discontinuation syndrome and why psychotropics are tapered rather than stopped are taught in Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Serotonin Syndrome","Rare but potentially life-threatening. 60% of patients who develop it do so within 6 hours of a first dose, a change in dosing, or an intentional overdose. If the serotonergic medication is not discontinued it progresses to worsening myoclo…","Mental status — Agitation, confusion, restlessness, lethargy, delirium, irritability, dizziness, hallucinations.","Autonomic — Diaphoresis, flushing, fever, tachycardia, mydriasis.","Neuromuscular — Myoclonus (muscle twitching or jerks), hyperreflexia, tremors.","Gastrointestinal — Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.","Treatment","Stop or reduce the contributing medications, depending on symptom severity and as directed by the prescriber. This is the first action.","Benzodiazepines (diazepam or lorazepam) for agitation, seizures, and muscle stiffness.","Avoid antipsychotics such as haloperidol for the agitation. They can worsen hyperthermia and contribute to a neuroleptic malignant syndrome–like reaction. The agitation here is an internal restlessness, not outward aggression.","Oxygen to maintain normal levels. IV fluids for dehydration and fever. Symptomatic and supportive care.","Tachycardia or hypertension: esmolol or nitroprusside. Hypotension: phenylephrine or epinephrine.","If other treatments are not working, a serotonin-production blocker: cyproheptadine HCl.","Symptoms usually resolve within 24 hours, but can take several weeks to clear completely."]},{"s":"Tricyclic Antidepressants","a":"w2-tca","k":["Tricyclic Antidepressants","Deadly in overdose, through fatal CNS depression, and equally so combined with alcohol or other CNS depressants. The cardiovascular effects are the serious ones: dysrhythmias, tachycardia, MI, and heart block.","Drugs","imipramine","doxepin","amitriptyline","Indication — Second choice for MDD after the SSRIs. Block the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin at the presynaptic neuron in the CNS, which is thought to elevate mood. Much less expensive than SRIs, and returning to use after several…","Side Effects & What to Do — Dry mouth: sugar-free hard candy or gum. GI upset: take with food. Diarrhea: frequent small meals. Constipation: increase fiber, fluids, exercise. Insomnia: sleep hygiene, take in the morning. Orthostasis: stay h…","Adverse Events — Dysrhythmias, tachycardia, MI, and heart block. Alpha-adrenergic blockade produces orthostatic hypotension and tachycardia. Dizziness and falls are the worry in older adults. Urinary retention or severe constipation needs i…","Contraindications — After an MI or with other cardiovascular disease · narrow-angle glaucoma · any history of seizures · pregnancy. Considered risky in older adults and in anyone with cardiac disease. Avoided in children and adolescents.","Monitoring & Teaching — Check an ECG for cardiac disorder before initiating, and check for a history of seizure disorder. Results take 10–14 days to appear and full effects 4–8 weeks. Start low and go slow. Often given at night. The sedatio…","Anticholinergic side effects. Hot as a hare: increased temperature · Dry as a bone: dry mouth and eyes, urinary retention, constipation · Blind as a bat: mydriasis, dilated pupils, blurred vision · Red as a beet: flushed face · Mad as a hat…"]},{"s":"MAOIs","a":"w2-maoi","k":["MAOIs","Drugs","phenelzine","tranylcypromine","selegiline (Emsam), a transdermal patch, with far fewer dietary restrictions and drug interactions","Indication — Usually the third choice for depression, reserved for treatment-resistant cases. Block monoamine oxidase, the enzyme that metabolizes serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, and tyramine, so the mood-elevating neurotransmitters in…","Side Effects & What to Do — Common: dry mouth · nausea · diarrhea or constipation · headache · drowsiness · insomnia · dizziness or lightheadedness · skin reaction at the patch site. Less common: involuntary muscle jerks · low blood pressur…","Adverse Events — Hypertensive crisis after a contraindicated substance. See below. Serotonin syndrome when combined with an SSRI.","Contraindications — Tyramine-rich foods. See the callout below. Avoid Demerol, and be very careful with OTC cold medications. Rarely used with an SSRI. The combination commonly causes serotonin syndrome. Limit caffeine, chocolate, and avoca…","Monitoring & Teaching — Monitor blood pressure routinely, especially during the first 6 weeks. Crisis occurs 15–90 minutes after ingesting a contraindicated substance.","Hypertensive Crisis","Tyramine-rich foods to avoid. Aged, smoked, fermented, marinated, and processed meats (pepperoni, bologna, salami, pickled herring, liver, frankfurters, bacon, ham) · aged cheeses (blue, cheddar, provolone, brie, Romano, parmesan, swiss) · …","Early — Irritability, anxiety, flushing, sweating, and a severe headache.","Established — Occipital headache, palpitations, nausea and vomiting, hypertension but with orthostatic hypotension on standing, neck stiffness or soreness, dilated pupils, photophobia, blurred vision, chest pain, tachycardia or bradycardia,…","Life-threatening — Severe fever, seizures, then coma or death. Can also produce intracranial hemorrhage.","Treatment","phentolamine, an alpha-adrenergic blocker and vasodilator.","Sublingual nifedipine, a calcium channel blocker that relaxes cardiac muscle.","Symptomatic and supportive care."]},{"s":"Other Antidepressants","a":"w2-other-antidepressants","k":["Other Antidepressants","bupropion(NDRI) — Contraindicated in eating disorders and any history of seizures. Fewer sexual side effects, and considered energizing. Used when fatigue is the prominent symptom. Also used for smoking cessation, as Zyban.","trazodone — Chemically similar to the TCAs. So sedating that it is now used mainly at bedtime for sleep, typically 50–200 mg PO at bedtime PRN insomnia. 300–500 mg is needed for an antidepressant effect. Not addicting, which is why it is pr…","mirtazapine(NaSSA) — Noradrenergic and specific serotonergic. Given at bedtime: good for sleep."]},{"s":"Lithium Carbonate","a":"w2-lithium","k":["Lithium Carbonate","Coarse hand tremor, slurred speech, ataxia, confusion, or GI upset in a patient on lithium: hold the next dose and request a lithium level. Do not treat these as an expected early reaction that will subside.","Indication — Acute mania and maintenance treatment. The most widely used mood stabilizer. Effective in about 40% of patients with bipolar disorder. Action uncertain: crosses cell membranes, alters sodium transport, and is not protein-bound.","Levels & Dosing — Therapeutic 0.6 to 1.4 mEq/L. Toxic 1.5 mEq/L and above. 1200–3000 mg/day. Start low and titrate up. Onset 5 to 7 days, and may take as long as 2 weeks, faster than the antidepressants.","Adverse Events — Kidney damage is a real risk when levels go unmonitored. Thyroid function may decrease, usually after 6–18 months. Watch for dry skin, hair loss, constipation, bradycardia, and cold intolerance. Increased risk of suicidal i…","Contraindications — Cardiovascular disease · renal disease · brain damage · thyroid disease · pregnant or breastfeeding mothers.","Monitoring & Teaching — Labs 1–2 times per week at first, then creatinine, thyroid hormones, and CBC every 6 months. Do not restrict sodium. Maintain a normal, steady intake. Losing fluid means losing sodium, which increases the potential f…","Used With","Acute, severe mania: lithium plus an antipsychotic: for example lithium with risperidone. Once the mania is controlled the lithium continues and the antipsychotic is stopped.","Less severe mania: bordering on hypomania, lithium alone may be enough.","Suicidal ideation: low-dose lithium, about 25 mg PO daily for a few weeks.","Under 1.5 mEq/Lexpected side effects — Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, thirst, polyuria, lethargy, sedation, and fine hand tremor. Renal toxicity may occur with long-term use. Goiter and hypothyroidism. — Symptoms often subside during treatment…","1.5–2.0 mEq/Learly toxicity — Gastrointestinal upset, coarse hand tremor, confusion, hyperirritability of muscles, electroencephalographic changes, sedation, incoordination. — Withhold the medication, measure the blood lithium level, and re…","2.0–2.5 mEq/Ladvanced toxicity — Ataxia, giddiness, serious EEG changes, blurred vision, clonic movements, large output of dilute urine, seizures, stupor, severe hypotension, coma. Death is usually secondary to pulmonary complications. — Ho…","Over 2.5 mEq/Lsevere toxicity — Convulsions, oliguria, and death can occur. — All of the above, plus hemodialysis in severe cases.","Why diet and fluid intake change how lithium behaves is taught with the psychopharmacology principles in Neurobiology & Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Anticonvulsant Mood Stabilizers","a":"w2-anticonvulsants","k":["Anticonvulsant Mood Stabilizers","Any rash on lamotrigine: stop the medication immediately and call the provider. It usually appears first on the chest or trunk. It is an allergic reaction that can advance to Stevens-Johnson syndrome (flu-like symptoms, sore throat, fever, …","valproate / divalproex(sodium valproate, divalproex sodium, valproic acid) — Check serum levels. Broader spectrum of efficacy against bipolar symptoms and longer periods of mood stabilization than the alternatives. All three names appear in…","carbamazepine — Check serum levels. Risk of low white blood cell counts. Check a CBC at the start and every 6 months, and monitor hepatic and renal function. Used when there has been no response to lithium, or with secondary breakthrough ma…","lamotrigine — For rapid cycling and the depressed phase of bipolar illness. Used as add-on therapy in refractory mood disorders. Usually tried after lithium fails. Rash may be life-threatening.","gabapentin — Effective for acute mania, mood stabilization, and rapid cycling."]},{"s":"Antipsychotics & Adjuncts for Mania","a":"w2-mania-adjuncts","k":["Antipsychotics & Adjuncts for Mania","Do not give an antidepressant alone to treat the depressive symptoms of bipolar disorder. It can throw the patient into mania. Mood stabilizers are the first medications used.","Acute, severe mania — A combination: lithium plus an antipsychotic, or divalproex/valproate plus an antipsychotic: for example lithium with risperidone, or divalproex with olanzapine. Once the mania is controlled, the lithium continues and …","Less severe mania — Bordering on hypomania, lithium or divalproex alone may be enough.","Severe agitation — Benzodiazepines: diazepam, lorazepam. They calm agitation and reduce aggression, panic, and insomnia, but they are sedative-hypnotics and addictive, so they are used only when necessary and only short-term.","Predominantly depressed — Symbyax, a combination of the second-generation antipsychotic olanzapine and the SSRI fluoxetine. Used, for example, in bipolar II where full mania does not occur.","Second-generation antipsychotics — olanzapine · risperidone · aripiprazole (for use over age 10) · quetiapine."]},{"s":"Medications in Suicide Care","a":"w2-suicide-meds","k":["Medications in Suicide Care","Low-dose lithium — About 25 mg PO daily for a few weeks. The mechanism is not established, but it reduces suicidal ideation quickly.","Where these sit among the other suicide interventions is covered in Suicide"]},{"s":"At a Glance","a":"w2-glance","k":["At a Glance","fluoxetine — SSRI — First-line for MDD, and in children and teens.","paroxetine sertraline citalopram escitalopram — SSRI — First-line for MDD.","venlafaxine — SNaRI — MDD. Off label in children and teens.","duloxetine — SNaRI — MDD.","desvenlafaxine — SNRI — MDD. A metabolite of venlafaxine.","imipramine doxepin amitriptyline — TCA — MDD, second choice after SSRIs.","phenelzine tranylcypromine — MAOI — Treatment-resistant depression, third choice.","selegiline (Emsam) — MAOI — Transdermal patch: far fewer dietary and drug restrictions.","bupropion — NDRI — Depression where fatigue is prominent. Smoking cessation as Zyban.","trazodone — Other antidepressant — Mainly at bedtime for sleep. Antidepressant effect at higher doses.","mirtazapine — NaSSA — Depression. Given at bedtime, good for sleep.","lithium carbonate — Mood stabilizer — Acute mania and maintenance. Low dose for suicidal ideation.","valproate / divalproex — Anticonvulsant mood stabilizer — Mania. Longer periods of mood stabilization.","carbamazepine — Anticonvulsant mood stabilizer — When lithium fails, or breakthrough mania.","lamotrigine — Anticonvulsant mood stabilizer — Rapid cycling and the depressed phase.","gabapentin — Anticonvulsant mood stabilizer — Acute mania, mood stabilization, rapid cycling.","olanzapine risperidone — Second-generation antipsychotic — Acute severe mania, with a mood stabilizer.","aripiprazole — Second-generation antipsychotic — Acute severe mania, for use over age 10.","quetiapine — Second-generation antipsychotic — Acute severe mania.","Symbyax — Combination: olanzapine + fluoxetine — Predominantly depressed bipolar, e.g. bipolar II.","diazepam lorazepam — Benzodiazepine — Severe agitation, short-term. Serotonin syndrome."]},{"s":"Opioid Overdose Reversal","a":"w3-opioid-overdose","k":["Opioid Overdose Reversal","Drugs","naloxone","Indication — Reverses an opioid overdose: an opioid antagonist that displaces the opioid from its receptor. Also used with buprenorphine as a combination product for opioid use disorder. Do not confuse it with naltrexone, the recovery-phase…","Adverse Events — Sends the patient into instant withdrawal by throwing the opioid off the receptor.","Monitoring & Teaching — Available as a nasal spray or injection: intranasal, IM, sub-q or IV. Its half-life is shorter than that of opioids, so keep watching and repeat the dose if signs return. Naloxone kits go home with patients at risk o…","Opioid overdose is an emergency. Outside hospital, call 9-1-1 and give naloxone if available. In hospital, promote breathing first: aspirate secretions, insert an airway, ventilate. Then give naloxone. The cardinal signs are coma, pinpoint …"]},{"s":"Medication-Assisted Treatment: Opioid Use Disorder","a":"w3-oud","k":["Medication-Assisted Treatment: Opioid Use Disorder","Drugs","Opioid agonists: methadone · buprenorphine · buprenorphine & naloxone","Antagonist: naltrexone","Alpha agonist: clonidine","Indication — Methadone: the first medication marketed for opioid withdrawal. Acts like heroin, morphine and fentanyl but far more slowly, flattening the highs and the withdrawal lows. Buprenorphine: long-acting and slow, reducing cravings a…","Adverse Events — Methadone carries significant overdose potential and dependence is still possible. Buprenorphine does not carry methadone's overdose potential.","Contraindications — Naltrexone requires 10 to 14 days opioid-free before the first dose. Sooner precipitates a rapid, severe withdrawal.","Monitoring & Teaching — Methadone must be monitored closely, with daily clinic visits. For replacement therapy, stabilize the patient first, then taper slowly until off methadone to prevent more severe withdrawal. Naltrexone does not treat …","Buprenorphine Alone","Preferred for induction: starting treatment under the supervision of a healthcare provider.","Buprenorphine & Naloxone","The usual switch after initial treatment, once the medication is no longer taken under direct supervision.","Naloxone is a safety feature that discourages dissolving and injecting the medication.","Naloxone is absorbed only if the medication is injected instead of dissolved in the mouth as directed, and then it causes uncomfortable withdrawal symptoms.","In pregnancy: maintenance decreases dose variability and limits exposure to dirty needles and unknown additives. Dosage may be titrated upward as pregnancy progresses, with twice-daily dosing as an option, titrated against opioid withdrawal…"]},{"s":"Benzodiazepines for Alcohol Withdrawal","a":"w3-alcohol-withdrawal","k":["Benzodiazepines for Alcohol Withdrawal","Drugs","Benzodiazepines: diazepam · lorazepam","In DTs: seizures → IV diazepam · acute agitation, tremors, impending or acute DTs, hallucinosis → oral diazepam · delirium appears → IV lorazepam · elevated BP → clonidine · dehydration → oral or IV fluids.","Indication — Alcohol and sedative-hypnotic withdrawal, given at a CIWA-Ar score of 8 to 10 or more. Goals: control agitation, decrease the risk of seizures, and reduce morbidity and mortality.","Monitoring & Teaching — The patient may be on a fixed, scheduled regimen with PRN doses for breakthrough autonomic symptoms: for example diazepam 10 mg every 6 hours for four doses, then 5 mg every 6 hours for eight doses. Diazepam is the c…","Delirium tremens is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal and can result in death. Prevention is the goal: assess and monitor symptoms, labs, electrolytes and vital signs, hydrate, medicate adequately, and provide life-sustaining measu…"]},{"s":"Medication-Assisted Treatment: Alcohol Use Disorder","a":"w3-aud","k":["Medication-Assisted Treatment: Alcohol Use Disorder","Drugs","naltrexone: also used in opioid use disorder","disulfiram","acamprosate","A combination of medication with individual and behavioral therapies is what sustains recovery.","Indication — Naltrexone: blocks the euphoric effects of opioids and alcohol, so relapse produces no high, and reduces alcohol cravings. Long-acting, given IM. Disulfiram: prevents the breakdown of acetaldehyde, so drinking while taking it p…","Adverse Events — Disulfiram plus any alcohol produces severe flu-like illness.","Monitoring & Teaching — None of the three treats withdrawal symptoms. All are recovery-phase medications. Disulfiram teaching: alcohol is not only in drinks. Patients must avoid being near, touching or consuming alcohol-based products — per…"]},{"s":"Thiamine & Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","a":"w3-thiamine","k":["Thiamine & Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","Drugs","thiamine: vitamin B1","Indication — Replaced daily in alcohol use disorder: the standard regimen is a multivitamin, thiamine and folic acid. Treats Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a neurological disorder caused by lack of thiamine (B1). Chronic alcohol use decreases…","Monitoring & Teaching — Wernicke's encephalopathy: large doses, IV, 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks, typically about 10 days. Acute and reversible. May clear in a few weeks or progress. Korsakoff syndrome: thiamine replacement for 3–12 months…","The two stages and their symptoms are covered in Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"]},{"s":"At a Glance","a":"w3-glance","k":["At a Glance","naloxone — Opioid antagonist — Reverses opioid overdose.","methadone — Synthetic narcotic opioid — Opioid withdrawal and maintenance.","buprenorphine — Partial opioid agonist — Reduces opioid cravings and withdrawal symptoms.","buprenorphine & naloxone — Combination — Reduce or quit opioids. Relieves cravings and reduces mortality.","naltrexone — Opioid antagonist — Recovery in both opioid and alcohol use disorder: blocks the high, reduces cravings.","clonidine — Antihypertensive, alpha agonist — Withdrawal symptoms, especially HR and BP in opioid, alcohol or nicotine withdrawal. Elevated BP in DTs.","diazepam — Benzodiazepine — Alcohol withdrawal, DTs, withdrawal seizures.","lorazepam — Benzodiazepine — Alcohol withdrawal. IV if delirium appears.","disulfiram — Aversive agent — Deters drinking by causing severe illness if alcohol is taken.","acamprosate — Anti-craving agent — Reduces alcohol cravings, maintains abstinence.","thiamine — Vitamin B1 — Replaced daily in alcohol use disorder. Treats Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome."]},{"s":"Typical Antipsychotics","a":"w4-typical","k":["Typical Antipsychotics","Drugs","chlorpromazine","haloperidol","haloperidol decanoate, the long-acting injectable form","Indication — Schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder. Primarily treat the positive symptoms, with little effect on the negative symptoms. Used less often than atypicals for that reason, but as effective as newer agents for positive sympt…","Side Effects & What to Do — Extrapyramidal side effects are the most common and distressing problem, and the leading driver of non-adherence. Also anticholinergic effects, sedation, weight gain, sexual dysfunction, endocrine disturbance and…","Adverse Events — Neuroleptic malignant syndrome · tardive dyskinesia · metabolic syndrome · cardiac arrhythmias · an increased risk of seizures.","Monitoring & Teaching — Assess with the AIMS at least every 3 months for tardive dyskinesia. Monotherapy is recommended, with patient-specific dose adjustment. Teach that the class is unlikely to be lethal in overdose and is not addictive, …"]},{"s":"Atypical Antipsychotics","a":"w4-atypical","k":["Atypical Antipsychotics","Drugs","clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","risperidone, also a long-acting injectable","aripiprazole","paliperidone palmitate, a long-acting injectable","Indication — Schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder. Treat both positive and negative symptoms, which is why they are used more often than typicals. Risperidone and aripiprazole are the agents used in children and adolescents. 2 to 6 we…","Side Effects & What to Do — Sedation and sexual dysfunction. Less likely than typicals to cause anticholinergic effects, orthostatic hypotension, seizures and EPS, and side effects overall are fewer, milder and better tolerated. They are mo…","Adverse Events — Metabolic syndrome, the signature risk of the class: significant weight gain, especially abdominal, dyslipidemia, increased blood glucose and insulin resistance, raising the risk of diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular di…","Monitoring & Teaching — For metabolic syndrome: monitor weight and girth, order an initial glucose tolerance test, monitor blood glucose regularly, and provide nutrition and activity support while considering the patient's lifestyle. Assess…","All atypical antipsychotics carry a risk of metabolic syndrome. It is the reason weight, girth and blood glucose are monitored from the start of therapy rather than after a problem appears."]},{"s":"Clozapine","a":"w4-clozapine","k":["Clozapine","Drugs","clozapine","Indication — Treatment-resistant or refractory schizophrenia, and schizoaffective disorder, where the patient has had an inadequate response to two other antipsychotics or could not tolerate them. A clozapine trial is justified after the fa…","Side Effects & What to Do — The atypical class effects above, including metabolic syndrome and sedation.","Adverse Events — Agranulocytosis, a potentially fatal reduction in circulating granulocytes that limits the ability to fight off infection. Greatest risk during the first months of treatment. Reversible if treated early.","Monitoring & Teaching — Monitor WBC weekly for the first 18 weeks of treatment, then as directed by the provider. Discontinue if the patient develops leucopenia or neutropenia. Teach the patient to report any sign of infection, since a fall…","Agranulocytosis is the reason clozapine is not first-line. It is the one fact to carry about this drug: a life-threatening drop in white blood cells, monitored weekly for the first 18 weeks."]},{"s":"Anticholinergics for Extrapyramidal Side Effects","a":"w4-anticholinergics","k":["Anticholinergics for Extrapyramidal Side Effects","Drugs","benztropine","trihexyphenidyl","Indication — Treating the extrapyramidal side effects of antipsychotic medication. Benztropine is given for akathisia and pseudoparkinsonism. Trihexyphenidyl is given for pseudoparkinsonism. They treat the side effect, not the schizophrenia…","Side Effects & What to Do — Anticholinergic effects, the thing the drug itself produces: dry mouth, blurred vision, dry eyes, constipation, urinary retention or hesitancy, drowsiness, dizziness, confusion, tachycardia and skin flushing from…","Adverse Events — Anticholinergic toxicity, which the side effects can progress to.","Monitoring & Teaching — Teach the mnemonic pattern so the patient can recognise the effects: mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, hot as a hare, dry as a bone. Adding an anticholinergic to an antipsychotic compounds the anticholi…"]},{"s":"Treating Akathisia, Tardive Dyskinesia & NMS","a":"w4-adverse-treatment","k":["Treating Akathisia, Tardive Dyskinesia & NMS","Drugs","propranolol · lorazepam · diazepam, for akathisia","valbenazine · deutetrabenazine, for tardive dyskinesia","dantrolene sodium · bromocriptine mesylate · lorazepam, for NMS","Indication — Akathisia: propranolol, or lorazepam or diazepam, added by the provider alongside a dose reduction and benztropine. Tardive dyskinesia: valbenazine and deutetrabenazine, the two FDA-approved agents for managing symptoms. Neurol…","Side Effects & What to Do — Benzodiazepines are for short-term use only in akathisia. Longer use risks dependence and adds sedation to a patient already at risk of it.","Monitoring & Teaching — Akathisia is easily mistaken for anxiety or agitation, and treating it as such means giving more of the antipsychotic that caused it, making it worse. Assess before medicating. In tardive dyskinesia, symptoms usually…","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a medical emergency with a 10% fatality rate. Stop all antipsychotics immediately. Severe muscle rigidity, altered mental status, temperature over 103 degrees Fahrenheit, hypertension, tachycardia, tachypne…"]},{"s":"At a Glance","a":"w4-glance","k":["At a Glance","chlorpromazine haloperidol — Typical antipsychotic — Schizophrenia. Treat positive symptoms, little effect on negative.","haloperidol decanoate — Typical antipsychotic, long-acting injection — Schizophrenia where non-adherence is a risk. Given every 2 to 4 weeks or longer.","olanzapine quetiapine aripiprazole — Atypical antipsychotic — Schizophrenia. Treat both positive and negative symptoms.","risperidone — Atypical antipsychotic, also a long-acting injection — Schizophrenia, including in children and adolescents.","paliperidone palmitate — Atypical antipsychotic, long-acting injection — Schizophrenia where non-adherence is a risk.","clozapine — Atypical antipsychotic — Treatment-resistant schizophrenia, after two failed monotherapy trials. Causes agranulocytosis.","benztropine trihexyphenidyl — Anticholinergic — Treat extrapyramidal side effects: akathisia and pseudoparkinsonism.","propranolol — Beta blocker — Added for akathisia.","lorazepam diazepam — Benzodiazepine — Akathisia, short-term only. Lorazepam also for agitation in NMS.","valbenazine deutetrabenazine — VMAT2 inhibitor — Manage the symptoms of tardive dyskinesia.","dantrolene sodium bromocriptine mesylate — NMS treatment — Relieve muscle rigidity and reduce fever in neuroleptic malignant syndrome."]}]},{"f":"week1-intro.html","t":"Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing","secs":[{"s":"Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Defining Mental Health & Mental Illness","a":"defining","k":["Defining Mental Health & Mental Illness","Health (WHO) — Complete physical, mental, and social well-being: not merely the absence of disease or infirmity. Includes satisfaction with, and enjoyment of, the self and the environment.","Mental health (WHO) — Well-being in which the individual can realize their own potential, cope with the normal stresses of life, work productively, and contribute to the community.","Mental illness (APA) — Health conditions involving changes in emotion, thinking, or behavior, associated with distress and/or problems functioning in social, work, or family activities.","DSM-5 — Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition: the official medical guidelines of the American Psychiatric Association for diagnosing psychiatric disorders. Criteria influenced by multiprofessional clinical fiel…","Characteristics of Mental Health","Rational thinking","Resiliency","Self-esteem","Self-awareness","Emotional growth","Self-care","Learning and productivity","Communication skills","Meaningful relationships","Spiritual satisfaction","What Shapes Mental Health","Prenatal, perinatal, and neonatal events — Interactions — Family stability","Physical health status — Intelligence quotient — Ethnicity","Nutrition — Self-concept — Housing","History of injuries — Skills — Child-rearing patterns","Neuroanatomy — Creativity — Economic level","Physiology — Emotional developmental level — Religion, values, and beliefs","The Mental Health–Illness Continuum","Health is fluid, whether mental or physical. Mental health and mental illness sit on a continuum, and people move along it in both directions.","Well-Being","Adequate to high functioning. Everyday stress and discomfort do not impact daily functioning.","Emotional Problems or Concerns","Mild to moderate distress, mild impairment in functioning: trouble with concentration and appetite. Insomnia and other sleep issues.","Mental Illness","Marked distress, with altered thinking, mood, and behavior.","Theories of Causation","Early 19th-Century Theories","Inheritance theory: \"insanity\" transmitted from one generation to another.","Moral degeneracy theory: mentally ill by virtue of a bad character.","Germ theory: \"insanity\" believed contagious, so people were segregated.","Septic foci theory: infection causes insanity, so remove the infection by surgery = cure. The era of bleeding and leeches.","What Is Believed Now","No single common cause: different mental illnesses have different theories of causation.","Diathesis-stress model: the most accepted explanation for mental illness. Diathesis = a biological predisposition for an illness. Stress = any emotional or environmental stress or trauma. Many carry the predisposition, but it does not turn …"]},{"s":"History: Early Treatment & the Shift to Community Care","a":"history","k":["History: Early Treatment & the Shift to Community Care","1800s–1900s: symptoms believed to be behavioral, under the person's control. Treatment followed from that belief.","Early Treatments","Physical restraint: padded helmets, hand mittens, chain restraints, straitjackets.","Chemical restraint: heavy medication that left people sitting around.","Lobotomy: stopped acting-out behaviors but drastically changed personality.","Insulin therapy and hydrotherapy (sedative cold wet packs, warm baths, and tanks that sprayed water at the person as a calming measure).","ECT, introduced later.","ECT is taught as a current treatment — full detail in Group & Physiologic Therapies","1845 — Lunacy Act: created asylums and institutionalization. Families dropped people off and often never saw them again.","1930 — Mental Health Treatment Act: institutions became Mental Hospitals. People began to be treated more humanely.","1963 — President John F. Kennedy's Community Mental Health Act accelerated deinstitutionalization: the mass movement of severely mentally ill people from state hospitals to outpatient care, closing many hospitals. Expanded the nurse's role:…","1970s — Mental hospitals shutting down. Mental health units opened inside general hospitals, making mental health care more like acute medical care.","1990s — Strong push for less time in the hospital and more management in the community.","Late 2000s — Integration of primary care and mental health care in outpatient settings.","What Went Wrong After Deinstitutionalization","People had been institutionalized: no coping skills, no jobs, nowhere to live, no ability to cook or care for themselves.","Many with good family support or assisted living transitioned successfully, especially with the new medications. Many others did not.","Community mental health centers grew slowly and were understaffed: no job training, psychiatric counseling, or life skills training.","Incarceration — About 2 million people with mental illness are booked into jails each year. Most get no treatment, so the illness worsens and victimization risk is high. Once out, at least 83% no longer have access to health care and benefi…","Revolving-door treatment — Began in the 1960s with the community mental health movement: psychotropic drug development plus deinstitutionalization into communities that lacked services, leaving patients without the skills to function indepe…","Managed care — 1990s: inpatient a few weeks to stabilize. For the past two decades managed care companies have dictated the terms of admission: length-of-stay reductions and more stringent admission criteria.","Victimization — People assume mental illness means dangerous, but people with mental illness are far more often the victims. Serious mental illness (SMI) carries a 12 times higher rate of victimization than the general population: verbal ab…"]},{"s":"Advocacy, Legislation & the Courts","a":"advocacy-legislation","k":["Advocacy, Legislation & the Courts","Consumers — The term for individuals who have mental illness. The consumer (or consumer recovery) movement advocated for their rights and fought discrimination and forced treatment. Consumers resisted treatment arranged for them by provider…","NAMI (1979) — National Alliance on Mental Illness: the nation's largest grassroots mental health organization.","What NAMI Does","Builds better services and treatment. Supports research.","Communicates that mental illnesses are brain disorders: raising awareness and eliminating stigma and discrimination.","Advocates for people with mental illness and improves access to treatment services.","Integrates mental illness into community life.","Advocates for the language we use: the person is not the disease. Not \"a schizophrenic.\" A person who has schizophrenia.","Landmark Reports and Legislation","Mental Health Parity Act — 1996 — Insurers must give equal coverage for psychiatric illness as for physical health, including deductibles, number of covered visits, and out-of-pocket expense. Also covers substance-related disorders.","Report of the Surgeon General — 1999 — First national report on mental health in the US. Finding: mental health is fundamental to a healthy life · mental health disorders are real health conditions with enormous consequences · effective tre…","New Freedom Commission on Mental Health — 2003 — Finding: mental health care in the US was \"in shambles.\" Called for a streamlined system with less fragmented care, early diagnosis and treatment, adoption of the principles of recovery, and …","Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act — 2010 — Coverage for most uninsured Americans through expanded Medicaid eligibility. Created health insurance exchanges for more choices. Carried the insurance mandate.","Tim's Law (Kentucky) — 2017 — Kentucky's first assisted outpatient treatment (AOT) statute. Other states call this assertive community treatment (ACT). Lets families or others in the community go to court to require an individual to attend …","The Six Goals of the New Freedom Commission","Americans understand the link between mental health and overall health.","Mental health care is family- and consumer-driven.","Disparities in mental health care are eliminated.","Screening, assessment, and referral become common practice, so problems are caught early.","Principles of recovery guide care.","Greater use of technology, and more housing for individuals with mental illness.","Fayette County Mental Health Court","October 2012: hosted by NAMI, attended by mental health and criminal justice stakeholders.","Multidisciplinary team: judges, prosecutors, providers, case workers, and peer support specialists (consumers), delivering treatment-oriented, person-centered care.","Built on therapeutic jurisprudence and restorative justice principles, to reduce re-offending.","Peer support specialists act as \"systems navigators,\" matching participants to housing and support services. Independent housing with access to community services is a primary goal.","Referral by order of diversion, probation, or contempt. Staff then screen candidates for eligibility: verifying a diagnosis of serious mental illness and evaluating which services the person can use. The candidate decides whether to accept …","Lets people with SMI obtain professional treatment and medications instead of a jail sentence."]},{"s":"Barriers to Care & Stigma","a":"barriers-stigma","k":["Barriers to Care & Stigma","Access and provider limitations — Not as many providers, and limits on their current knowledge.","Transportation — A real barrier for many individuals.","Wait times — Two to three months for an outpatient follow-up appointment is common.","Income, cost, and insurance parity — Low income, the cost of care, and the limits of what insurance parity actually covers.","Stigma — Seeing someone in a negative way because of a particular characteristic, attribute, or personal trait (Mayo Clinic). Widespread fear and misunderstanding of mental illness, heightened by the focus on extraordinary symptoms in film …","Why Stigma Matters Clinically","A major clinical and public health issue in its own right.","Worsens the course and outcomes of mental illness. A heavy burden on the individual.","Many people with mental illness will not go to an ER because of stigma from health care providers.","How to Fight Stigma","Talk openly about mental health: break the cultural barrier of not talking about it.","Stop criminalizing these individuals (Mental Health Court is one way).","Push back on media portrayals of mental illness.","Educate yourself and others: education decreases stigma.","Be conscious of the language you use.","Advocate for mental health reform as a nurse.","Encourage equality in physical and mental illness.","See the person, not the illness. Show empathy and compassion."]},{"s":"Key Terms","a":"key-terms","k":["Key Terms","Resilience — The ability and capacity to secure the resources needed to support personal well-being: optimism, competence, and a sense of mastery over obstacles. It determines the incidence, severity, and prognosis of mental illness and is …","Recovery — A process of change through which an individual improves their health and wellness, lives a self-directed life, and reaches their full potential. Recovery is possible even where cure is not: we cannot cure schizophrenia, and a pe…","Dual diagnosis — A co-occurring mental illness and a substance use disorder (e.g. alcohol use disorder with bipolar disorder). One of the two must be a substance use disorder.","Comorbidity — The broader term for two co-occurring chronic illnesses: two mental health disorders (depression and anxiety) or a mental and a medical one (depression and diabetes). Dual diagnosis is the specific case that involves substance…","Adherence (not compliance) — Following the plan of care, and the term that replaced compliance. \"Compliance\" carries a strongly negative connotation, so the language change is itself a way of breaking down stigma.","Anosognosia — An individual with a severe mental illness does not believe they have it: a common reason a stabilized patient stops their medications at discharge."]},{"s":"Psychiatric Nursing Practice & the Treatment Team","a":"nursing-practice","k":["Psychiatric Nursing Practice & the Treatment Team","Basic Level: PMH-RN","Psychiatric mental health registered nurse.","Requires 2 years of full-time work, 2,000 clinical hours, 30 hours of continuing education.","Then sit the certification exam and add BC to the RN title (RN-BC).","Advanced Practice: PMH-APRN","Master of Science or doctorate.","Health promotion, screening, and evaluation. Health assessment and examination. Formulates differential diagnoses.","Orders, conducts, and interprets laboratory and diagnostic studies and procedures.","Conducts individual, family, and group psychotherapy: outside the basic-level scope.","Prescribes, monitors, manages, and evaluates medications.","Provides or directs home health services. Also works in general health settings.","Nursing Skills Rooted in Psychiatric Mental Health Care","Safety: awareness of the client's environment and the client's thoughts.","Therapeutic communication and the nurse-patient relationship.","Milieu management.","Relaxation therapy.","Motivational interviewing and the concept of self-efficacy.","Suicide assessment and prevention.","Management of aggressive behavior, including with medication.","Crisis intervention and disaster mental health.","Instillation of hope: without hope, clients do not do well.","Instillation of hope is also one of Yalom's therapeutic factors — see Group & Physiologic Therapies","The Interdisciplinary Treatment Team","Each discipline gathers data and participates in the plan of care. The treatment plan (some places call it a clinical pathway) guides care during the stay, detailing the steps that deliver evidence-based care. Nurses frequently lead these m…","Who Is on the Team","Always: the patient, family, nurse, and provider.","May also include: CNS or APRN, psychologist, social worker (SW/LCSW), pharmacist, recreational (activity) therapist, and mental health associates or techs.","Certified drug and alcohol counselor when there is a substance use diagnosis. A Fayette County teacher on the child and adolescent units.","The patient is the most important member: they have self-efficacy and need to help design the plan. If they don't agree with it, they won't follow it.","Treatment Settings","Acute care hospital unit — Highly structured, 24-hour locked setting that optimizes safety and addresses crisis intervention. Get them in, get them stable, get them somewhere else. — Average stay 3–7 days, and about 3–5 days on an adult uni…","Residential treatment for substance use — The person lives there and focuses on recovery. — Usually 30–60 days. Can run 6–12 months.","Partial hospitalization (PHP) — Transitional step between inpatient and fully independent home life, for people needing some supervision but not hospitalization: group therapy, occupational or therapeutic activities, recreation, then home. …","Day treatment — One step below PHP. Structured activities and group for ongoing, usually chronic mental illness (e.g. severe autism spectrum disorder), so family members can work. Focus: coping skills, strengths for a more functional, indep…","Specialty treatment settings — Pediatric psychiatric care · geriatric psychiatric care and geriatric units · veteran centers · forensic psychiatric care (clients in jails) · alcohol and drug use disorder treatment · self-help options · eati…","What an Acute Unit Is Actually Like","Admission criteria: danger to self (suicidal ideation or behaviors) · danger to others (homicidal ideation) · unable to care for basic needs and/or gross impairment of judgment, placing the individual at imminent risk through an inability t…","Some units also handle detoxification from drugs or alcohol.","Top 5 inpatient diagnoses: mood disorders (depressive and bipolar) · substance-related disorders · delirium or dementia · anxiety disorders · schizophrenia."]},{"s":"Admissions, Patient Rights & Legal Issues","a":"admissions-rights","k":["Admissions, Patient Rights & Legal Issues","Voluntary Admission","The patient or guardian seeks hospitalization.","Both the individual and the health care professional must agree treatment is needed.","The client signs themselves in and signs a consent for treatment.","Involuntary Commitment","An emergency, very temporary admission typically involving people who are confused or psychotic.","The physician, PA, or NP believes admission is needed but the client does not want to be admitted, and is admitted against their will on a 72-hour hold, not counting weekends and holidays.","During the 72 hours the patient may improve and sign themselves in, becoming a voluntary admission.","If treatment is still needed when the 72 hours expire, form 202A is completed by the APRN or MD and taken to the judge to file for longer commitment.","Patients admitted to a psychiatric hospital have the same rights as any other hospitalized patient.","Rights guaranteed under the law — To have or refuse visitors, to receive or refuse treatment, and rights regarding psychiatric advance directives.","Informed consent — Patient informed of the risks, benefits, and alternatives, then voluntarily accepts treatment.","Implied consent — A patient is approached with a medication and indicates they are willing to take it.","Capacity — A person's ability to make an informed decision.","Competency — A legal term for the degree of mental soundness a person has to make decisions or to carry out specific acts.","Restraint and seclusion — There must be an order and documentation. In an emergency a nurse may place a patient in seclusion or restraint, then obtain a written or verbal order as soon as possible thereafter.","Confidentiality — Only those who need to know may know, unless the client gives permission. Two exceptions: a specific threat to kill someone (you have a duty to warn and protect third parties), and suspected child or elder abuse, which mus…","There is never a PRN or standing order for restraint. A physician must come, lay eyes on the patient, and write the order every time.","Least Restrictive Means","Assess what is going on — Find what is actually driving the behavior before intervening.","Verbally intervene — Talk to the client. Use active listening.","Reduce stimulation — Encourage a client in the day room to go to their room. Removing the client removes the audience, and things often calm down.","Provide diversion — Sometimes enough on its own to avoid restraint.","Offer PRN medication by mouth — Always offered before an injection. If refused, give the PRN IM (typically haloperidol, diphenhydramine, and lorazepam). Chemical restraints (medications ordered specifically for agitation) are less restricti…","Escort to seclusion, if agreed — Walk with them if they agree to go.","Apply restraints (last resort) — Only if the client is at significant danger to themselves or others.","Seclusion and restraint procedure, monitoring, and debriefing are taught in full in Anger, Aggression & Violence"]},{"s":"The Therapeutic Milieu","a":"milieu","k":["The Therapeutic Milieu","The therapeutic milieu is the surroundings and environment of the inpatient unit, including peer and staff interactions.","Containment — Basic needs (food, shelter, safety, security) are provided on a locked unit.","Support — Encouragement, praise, and positive feedback help clients achieve autonomy, improve coping skills, and manage symptoms.","Validation — Respecting privacy, cultural needs, and feelings for holistic health.","Structure — Control and limitation of maladaptive behaviors. Setting limits.","Involvement — Promoting the self-efficacy of the client through self-care and stress management strategies.","Community meetings are the milieu's core group — covered in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]}]},{"f":"week1-neurobiology.html","t":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Visualizing the Brain","a":"imaging","k":["Visualizing the Brain","Brain imaging began only in the 1990s, so the physiology of mental illness is still a relatively new field.","Structured Imaging","Shows anatomy. Locates tumors, atrophy, and other abnormalities.","Computed tomography (CT)","Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","Functional Imaging","Shows brain function and physiological activity: the changes that correlate with behavioral or cognitive alterations.","Positron emission tomography (PET)","Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)","Functional MRI (fMRI)","Brains with mental illness look different from brains without it. PET shows reduced brain activity (darker colors) in depression, especially in the prefrontal cortex. After treatment and recovery the entire brain, and particularly the left …"]},{"s":"Major Brain Functions","a":"brain-functions","k":["Major Brain Functions","Maintaining homeostasis — Keeping the internal environment stable despite the external one. The brain is the control center, regulating through the nervous and endocrine systems.","Negative feedback loops — A change is detected (low blood sugar) → the brain initiates a response (hunger) → the person eats → balance is restored.","Regulating the ANS and hormones — The autonomic nervous system runs involuntary functions: sympathetic = fight or flight, speeds body functions up. Parasympathetic = rest and digest, slows them down. The endocrine system links the brain to …","Biological drives and behavior — Sex, hunger, the sleep-wakefulness cycle, and circadian rhythms.","Conscious mental activity — Thinking, memory, problem-solving, and interpretation of the external world.","Why a mental disturbance produces physical signs: anxiety activates the sympathetic nervous system, so the person doesn't just feel anxious. They get increased heart rate, increased blood pressure, sweating, and shortness of breath."]},{"s":"Core CNS Structures","a":"cns-structures","k":["Core CNS Structures","Thalamus — The main relay station of the CNS: sensory pathways from the spinal cord, cerebellum, and brainstem form synapses here on their way to the cerebral cortex.","Hypothalamus — The major respiratory center, holding the basic vital functions: temperature, appetite, sleep, sex drive, heart rate, and blood pressure.","Cerebellum — Motor coordination. The coiled structure under the occipital lobe, coordinating voluntary movement, equilibrium, and muscle tone.","Brainstem — The central core of the brain, made mostly of nerve fibers, in three areas: midbrain, pons, and medulla. Provides the main motor and sensory innervation to the face and neck through the cranial nerves. 10 of the 12 pairs come fr…"]},{"s":"The Cerebral Lobes & What Damage Looks Like","a":"lobes","k":["The Cerebral Lobes & What Damage Looks Like","Frontal — Where thought processes occur. Executive function: distinguishing good from bad, judging consequences, goal-directed behavior. Planning, organizing, control of emotions and behavior, and part of personality. — Inability to plan se…","Parietal — Perception, spelling, arithmetic, and reading: making sense of the world. — Problems with math, reading, drawing, and naming objects. Difficulty distinguishing left from right. Lack of awareness of certain body parts or surroundi…","Temporal — Receiving and identifying sensory information: memory, understanding, auditory, language. — Difficulty recognizing faces (prosopagnosia). Difficulty understanding spoken words (Wernicke's aphasia). Difficulty identifying and verb…","Occipital — The vision center. — Defects in vision (visual field cuts). Hallucinations. Inaccurately seeing objects (visual illusions). Inability to recognize words and drawn objects. Inability to recognize movement (movement agnosia).","Illusion vs. hallucination. An illusion: something really is there but is seen incorrectly, such as a coat tree across the room perceived as a person. A hallucination: nothing is there.","Where These Show Up Clinically","Frontal: perseveration plays a role in OCD. Mood and lability in bipolar disorder.","Parietal: lack of awareness of body parts or surrounding space is seen a lot in schizophrenia and in nihilistic delusions. Plaque builds up here in Alzheimer's, and people lose the ability to draw.","Temporal: persistent talking is what you see in mania."]},{"s":"Emotion, Memory & Movement Structures","a":"emotion-structures","k":["Emotion, Memory & Movement Structures","Corpus callosum — Relays information between the two hemispheres. — Difficulty integrating logical information with emotion. Left (logical) side dominant: less ability to feel emotion. Right (emotional) side dominant: difficulty controlling…","Limbic system — Learning, memories, and emotions. Coordinates visceral responses: gut feelings, the hair rising on the back of your neck. — Excessive emotional responses.","Hippocampus — Both long- and short-term memory. Also controls the endocrine and immune systems. — Cannot form new memories despite intact intelligence. Heavily damaged in chronic alcoholism, which leads to Korsakoff syndrome and Wernicke's.","Amygdala — The emotional center of the brain: emotion, anxiety, fear, and pleasure. Part of the fight-or-flight system: alerts us to danger with enough fear and anxiety to protect us. — Damage in animals can cause rage. Plays a huge role in…","Basal ganglia — Coordinates and assists in moving the skeletal muscles. — —","Putting It Together: How a Touch Is Processed","1. Peripheral nervous system — The touch signal travels to the sensory cortex.","2. Sensory cortex — Evaluates it: what does that feel like?","3. Hippocampus — Searches for a memory of being touched like this. Good or bad? What do I expect next?","4. Amygdala — Attaches the emotional connection of that earlier remembered touch.","5. Basal ganglia — Causes you to act: withdraw in fear, return the touch with similar gentleness, or strike back with similar force."]},{"s":"The Neurotransmitters","a":"neurotransmitters","k":["The Neurotransmitters","Neurotransmitter: a substance released when a presynaptic neuron is excited, acting by either inhibiting or exciting a target cell. Released from vesicles at the end of the axons across the synaptic cleft, to receptors on the other side.","Too much or too little of these neurotransmitters is believed to cause neurological and mental illness. Drugs act on them by enhancing release, inhibiting release, or blocking synthesis. Many also block reuptake, so a higher concentration s…","There are hundreds of neurotransmitters. This course covers eight. Inhibitory: serotonin · GABA Excitatory: norepinephrine · epinephrine · dopamine · glutamate Other: acetylcholine · histamine","Serotonin — Inhibitory — Modulates mood, emotion, sleep, pain, and appetite as related to mood. Temperature regulation. Some cognitive function. — Decreased in depressive and anxiety disorders. Blocking its reuptake treats both very success…","GABA(gamma-aminobutyric acid) — Inhibitory — The major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Helps neurons recover after transmission, reduces anxiety, aggression, and stress, and regulates norepinephrine, adrenaline, dopamine, and seroto…","Norepinephrine — Excitatory — Affects mood, attention, and arousal. Stimulates the sympathetic branch of the ANS for the fight-or-flight stress response. — Decreased in depression. Increased in mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia.","Epinephrine(adrenaline) — Excitatory — Works with norepinephrine in fight-or-flight to boost strength, heart rate, blood pressure, and energy. Also decreases the ability to feel pain. — —","Dopamine — Excitatory — Pleasurable feelings and complex motor activity. Fine muscle movement. Integration of emotions and thoughts. Decision-making. — Too much can lead to auditory hallucinations. Plays a role in schizophrenia, in both the…","Glutamate — Excitatory — Used at most fast excitatory synapses in the brain and spinal cord. Stimulates the hypothalamus to release thyroid, adrenal, and sex hormones. — Excess over-stimulates the brain and causes seizures. Excessive releas…","Acetylcholine — Excitatory or inhibitory — Learning, memory, cognitive functioning. Sleep-wake cycles. Regulates mood: mania and sexual aggression. — Decreased in Alzheimer's and in sleep disorders. Increased in depression.","Histamine — Other — Listed among the eight. No functions covered."]},{"s":"Neuroplasticity & Synaptic Pruning","a":"plasticity","k":["Neuroplasticity & Synaptic Pruning","Neuroplasticity","The brain's lifelong ability to change with learning, reorganizing neural pathways based on new experiences.","As we learn and acquire knowledge and skills, new pathways are laid down. Learning a fact or skill requires persistent functional changes in the brain.","This is why repetition of a skill or a behavior change matters.","Synaptic Pruning","The neurological process that removes unnecessary or damaged neuronal structures.","Weeds out the weaker, seldom-used synapses, improving the brain's \"networking\" capacity.","The simpler associations formed in childhood are thought to be replaced by learning and more complex structures.","Ongoing, but it speeds up in prenatal development through age two, the preteen years, and midlife.","The link to schizophrenia: one biological theory is over-pruning, which can result in hallucinations. The timing fits the pruning surge in late adolescence and young adulthood."]},{"s":"Psychopharmacology Principles","a":"psychopharm","k":["Psychopharmacology Principles","Start low and titrate up slowly — Titrate to the patient's response. If the response is there, no increase may be needed.","Hold or switch for significant side effects — The medication may be held temporarily, or changed.","Start only one new medication at a time — So you can identify which medication caused a side effect or reaction.","Weigh the risks against the benefits — Especially in older adults and in polypharmacy (many prescribed medications).","Use the lowest effective dose — Enough for the benefit, with fewer side effects. Especially in older adults, who may be more sensitive, and in children.","Taper, never stop abruptly — Gradually reduce to prevent withdrawal, rebound, or worsening of symptoms.","What Changes How a Medication Behaves","Other medications: increase or decrease a drug's metabolism.","Diet: affects how medications are absorbed and metabolized, including half-life (the time the body takes to halve the drug's concentration). Matters most with a narrow therapeutic index or easy toxicity. Lithium, where sodium and fluid inta…","Genetic differences: fast versus slow metabolizers, affecting both drug levels and side effects. Genetic variation differs across populations, so psychiatric prescribing has to be individualized.","Discontinuation Syndrome","Antidepressants are not addicting (people do not abuse them or crave them), but the body does get used to them. A sudden decrease in dosage, or a sudden stop of certain psychotropic medications, causes discontinuation syndrome. Teach patien…","Rebound or relapse — The original symptoms come back.","New symptoms appear — Physical or psychological symptoms that were not there before."]}]},{"f":"week1-anger-aggression-violence.html","t":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","secs":[{"s":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Definitions","a":"definitions","k":["Definitions","Anger — An emotional response to frustration of desires, a threat to one's needs, or a challenge, from mild irritation to intense fury and rage. A normal human emotion, like anxiety, and capable of being under personal control: expressed as…","Aggression — Verbal or physical attack intended to threaten or injure the victim's security or self-esteem: words, fists, kicks, weapons. Virtually always designed to punish, but not always inappropriate: sometimes necessary for self-protec…","Violence — Intentional use of force that results in, or could result in, injury to another. Always an objectionable act. That is what separates it from aggression.","Workplace Violence","Why It Matters to Nurses","Health care and social services workers are at higher risk of workplace violence injury than workers overall.","Nurses are frequent targets because they have the most direct patient care contact.","Workplace violence is underreported, and it impairs your ability to provide effective patient care.","Most frequent settings: emergency departments · psychiatric units · geriatric units · intensive care units, but it can happen anywhere."]},{"s":"What Precipitates Anger & Violence","a":"precipitants","k":["What Precipitates Anger & Violence","Feelings That May Precipitate Anger","Anxiety — Discounted — Embarrassed — Frightened","Fear — Humiliated — Hurt — Ignored","Inadequate — Insecure — Unheard — Out of control of the situation","Rejected — Threatened — Tired — Vulnerable","Anxiety is the one to catch early. Anxious patients, visitors, and families grasp at anything in the environment they can control. Intervene early, and communicate. Communication is how we give them back a little control.","Situations That May Precipitate Anger","Withdrawal and substance use — Very common. Patients frequently carry a co-occurring substance use disorder alongside a psychiatric disorder (dual diagnosis).","Symptom burden — Not every patient with a psychiatric diagnosis becomes angry, aggressive, or violent, but auditory hallucinations and psychosis make it more likely.","Sleep deprivation — Huge factor: staff in and out of the room, symptoms keeping them awake.","Pain, extreme stress, trauma, attention-seeking behaviors — Each precipitates anger.","Feeling out of control — Loss of personal power.","Difference in expectations — Their goals differ from ours and they feel we are not meeting them.","Difference in knowledge — Heavy medical jargon, used as though the patient understands it.","Poor communication — A major contributor, along with inaccurate or incomplete information.","Milieu Characteristics Conducive to Violence","Too hot, too cold, too loud, too quiet","Overcrowding","Staff perceived as inattentive or inexperienced","Staff perceived as too controlling","Poor limit setting: the sense that other patients are allowed to do things they are not","Revocation of privileges","The therapeutic milieu and its five components are taught in Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing"]},{"s":"Warning Signs of Potential Violence","a":"warning-signs","k":["Warning Signs of Potential Violence","Hyperactivity is the most important predictor of imminent violence: pacing, restlessness.","These signs usually (but not always) precede violence.","Motor — Hyperactivity, pacing, restlessness, fidgeting.","Tension — Clenched jaws or fists, rigid posture, fixed or tensed facial expressions. Mumbling.","Speech — Changes in rate, rhythm, volume: a loud or very soft voice, change of pitch, or stone silence.","Verbal content — Verbal abuse, profanity, argumentativeness.","Eye contact — Intense eye contact or glaring, or avoiding it completely.","History and objects — Recent acts of violence. A weapon, or an object usable as one.","Withdrawal — Isolation that is uncharacteristic for that individual."]},{"s":"Assessing the Risk of Violence","a":"risk-assessment","k":["Assessing the Risk of Violence","The best predictor of future behavior is past behavior. Use structured tools rather than unstructured clinical judgment alone, to monitor and reduce incidents and to build a risk management plan.","History of violence — The single best predictor of future violence, including any assaultive or threatening behavior. Reactions to stimuli come from previous experience, so identify past and current triggers.","Symptom burden — Delusions, hyperactivity, impulsivity, predisposition to irritability.","Cognitive changes — Confusion, disorientation, delirium, psychosis, acute hallucinations, delusions. They may misinterpret the environment or staff care activities.","Agitation and escalating anxiety — Restlessness, and especially escalation (mild to severe).","Non-adherence or resistance to treatment — Some do not believe they have a mental illness.","Known history of substance use — Raises risk on its own.","Aggression management needed at transfer — Required aggression management on arrival. They did not come to the unit willingly.","Limit setting by the nurse — Aggression occurs most often in the context of limit setting.","Limited coping or assertiveness — Limited coping skills. Lack of assertiveness, or use of intimidation.","Risk Assessment Tools","BVC — Brøset Violence Checklist","DASA — Dynamic Appraisal of Situational Aggression","ABRAT — Aggressive Behaviour Risk Assessment Tool","MOAS — Modified Overt Aggression Scale","COVR — Classification of Violence Risk","HCR-20 — Historical Clinical Risk-20","START — Short-Term Assessment of Risk and Treatability","How to score a Brøset or DASA is not exam material. What matters is that structured tools exist and are used to identify patients at risk. The COVR is the one used to assess risk at discharge.","Brøset Violence Checklist (BVC)","Developed in Norway. The most common tool in the literature.","A shift assessment, scored on behaviors present versus the individual's own baseline.","Dynamic Appraisal of Situational Aggression (DASA)","Based on the Brøset. Used at Eastern State Hospital.","Completed every 24 hours rather than every 8-hour shift, covering seven areas.","More reliable than the other tools at predicting verbal and physical aggression.","UK HealthCare Patient Violence Risk Assessment (in EPIC)","When it is done — Every patient screened on admission (ED or floor), then, whatever their risk level, reassessed every 12 hours.","How it communicates — Populates to the demographic pane of the chart as an FYI flag, so it travels with the patient across the hospital. A sign is also placed on the door.","History of violence — Verbal or physical aggression at any point in the health care setting. Remains on the banner for the life of the EHR.","Potential for violence — Falls off the banner at discharge."]},{"s":"Interventions & Personal Safety","a":"interventions","k":["Interventions & Personal Safety","Self-Awareness","Nurses bring their own personal and professional history, and it affects the ability to intervene effectively and appropriately.","Be self-aware of your strengths, needs, concerns, and vulnerability.","Without self-awareness, nursing interventions become impulsive or emotion-based.","Watch your choice of words, tone of voice, nonverbal communication, and facial expressions.","Intervention ideally begins before any sign of escalation. Determining what the patient is feeling is already intervening, and often it is just saying you are concerned and want to listen.","Interventions","Acknowledge that the patient has needs, rational or not, possible to meet or not.","State your expectations clearly, usually through limit setting.","Have numerous brief, non-threatening, non-directive interactions to build the therapeutic relationship.","Speak slowly, in shortened sentences, with a low, calm voice: as anger escalates, the ability to process decreases drastically.","Never yell. Model controlled behavior: you are the reference point for the behavior you want.","Use open-ended statements: \"You feel people are treating you unfairly?\" not the challenging, \"What is going on right now?\"","Avoid asking \"Okay?\": it implies choices exist where they don't.","Offer two options: \"Your room, or the quiet room?\" This decreases the sense of powerlessness that often precipitates violence and moves the choice to the patient.","Give feedback on what you see: \"You seem to be very upset\" opens the feelings up and may de-escalate.","Personal space and escape route. Patients at risk for violence need more personal space: stay about one foot further than they can reach with arms or legs, so you read as less of a threat. Always leave yourself an escape route, and never le…","Staff Safety Considerations","Maintain eye contact but don't glare.","No dangling earrings, necklaces, or scarves. A patient may grab them and cause serious injury.","Enough staff for backup, but only one person talks. The rest stay unobtrusive.","Know the layout: furniture, obstacles, hazards that could injure during a physical intervention.","Don't stand directly in front of the patient or the doorway. Stand to the side or at an angle, and encourage the patient to sit.","Talk somewhere quiet but visible to other staff, and tell staff you are with the patient.","Individualized Interventions: Coping Skills","Patients arrive with their own coping skills, and the hospital takes many away: no smoking, no alcohol, a restricted diet, no caffeine.","Assess current coping strategies — What do they use when upset, anxious, stressed, or angry? Which have not worked?","Substitute what is unavailable — A smoker gets a nicotine patch, a gamer gets a TV show, a favorite food gets an alternative.","Help them adopt new strategies — Skills they can use here and afterward.","Pharmacological Interventions","It is the nurse's role to assess the appropriateness of PRN medication. A PRN is sometimes helpful, even essential, as a patient escalates. Used in conjunction with psychosocial interventions and de-escalation, never instead of them.","Long-term treatment of anger, aggression, and violence is treatment of the underlying psychiatric disorder. The PRN handles the moment. It is not the plan."]},{"s":"Matching the Response to the Behavior","a":"matching-response","k":["Matching the Response to the Behavior","Intervene early, and make the staff response fit the level the patient is at. Laying hands on a patient who is merely anxious will escalate the situation.","Level 1: Anxiety","Patient Behavior","Anxiety, frustration, impatience, irritability. Highly animated, repetitive movements.","A change in behavior in either direction: calm to loud, or loud to calm.","The patient still has some level of rationality.","Staff Response: Offer Support","Stay alert to changes in anxiety, anger, frustration, and nonverbal cues.","Approach as soon as you suspect a problem. Open a dialogue and get them talking.","Acknowledge their feelings and needs. It is OK to guess wrong. Trying to understand is the point.","Stay calm and in control. Calm, even tone.","Reflect their emotion. Be personable, a real human being, not cold or uncaring.","Avoid institutional or systems jargon.","Don't take the bait: don't get defensive, counterattack, or be easily offended.","Level 2: Loss of Rationality (Defensive)","Challenging questions: \"What are you going to do if I don't go to my room?\"","Standing in your personal space. Intimidation tactics (staring, glaring).","Refusing: loudly and adamantly refusing requests, care, or policy.","Releasing: table pounding, loud sighing, throwing things but not at you, not meant to cause harm.","Staff Response","Isolate the interaction: move the patient, or move others away.","Reduce stimulation. Remove people staring, laughing, or talking loudly.","Speak clearly and slowly, use few words, and show non-verbally what you want them to do.","Be firm but empathetic.","Allow loud verbal expressions of anger as long as they are not threatening.","Watch your paraverbals (tone, volume, and rhythm of speech): how you say what you say.","It is not a win-lose situation.","Again: keep an exit, and never place the patient between you and it.","Level 3: Hostility, Abuse, or Aggression","May begin as accusations, comments about your competence, or irrelevant personal remarks.","Danger signs: persistent swearing, sexist or racist comments, personal or specific threats of harm, intimidating comments, terroristic-type threats, any physical behavior directed at a person.","Call for help: coworkers, security, or law enforcement.","Protect yourself. Remove yourself from the situation if you can.","Remove objects within reach that could be used as weapons.","Never let the patient be between you and the exit.","Watch your nonverbals: don't sigh, roll your eyes, or show frustration. These escalate.","Keep directions to the patient short (\"please stay seated,\" \"stop, please\").","Don't agree just to agree.","If they can hear you, explain that aggression will not achieve the desired goal.","Level 4: Tension Reduction","Every Situation Eventually Reaches Tension Reduction","Energy decreases and rationality returns: the reachable, teachable moment.","Goal: re-establish therapeutic rapport.","Assess what the triggers were, whether coping strategies were used, and why they didn't work.","Help them identify coping strategies for the future.","Revise the plan of care to include the behaviors that may result in repeat seclusion or restraint."]},{"s":"Seclusion & Restraint","a":"seclusion-restraint","k":["Seclusion & Restraint","Seclusion and restraint are always a last resort. Try everything else first: verbal de-escalation, then pharmacologic interventions. Seclusion is typically used before restraints.","Seclusion — Involuntary confinement of a patient alone in a room or area they are physically prevented from leaving. May be a quiet area on the unit. Less restrictive than restraints and never punitive: the goal is safety. Only for violent …","Restraint — Any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material, or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body, or head freely. Risks staff injury and physical or emotional harm to the patient.","90–95% of patients in the psychiatric setting have a history of trauma or abuse, so holding someone down or mechanically restraining them can be traumatizing or re-traumatizing.","When It May Be Used: All Must Be True","As a last resort","Patient is a danger to self or others","Less restrictive interventions determined ineffective to protect the patient or others from harm","With a written modification to the plan of care","Only by safe, appropriate techniques per hospital policy and state law","What Counts","Physically holding a patient against their will is a restraint.","Four side rails up is a restraint, except for seizure precautions.","Tucking sheets so tightly the patient cannot move is a restraint.","Keeping a patient in their room by physical intervention is seclusion.","Guidelines for the Use of Mechanical Restraints","Mandated by the Joint Commission.","Indications for use — To protect the patient and others from harm.","Legal requirements — Multidisciplinary involvement · provider order per state law, within one hour of initiation, with a face-to-face assessment by someone external to the situation · patient advocate or relative notified · discontinued as …","Clinical assessment — Mental state at the time · physical examination for medical problems possibly causing the behavior change · the need for restraint.","Documentation — Behaviors leading to it · least restrictive measures tried first · interventions used and the response · the plan of care implemented · ongoing nursing evaluation.","Release procedure — Patient able to follow instructions and stay in control · termination of restraints · debrief with the patient.","Never restrain a patient in the prone position.","Types of Restraints","Nonviolent (soft) — The blue soft restraints. Monitoring and documentation frequency is lower.","Violent — Secure cuffs or leather restraints, much sturdier. Monitoring and documentation frequency is much higher.","The wrap — Like a security blanket. Very effective for calming children and adolescents, usable for adults too.","Chairs and boards — Many facilities have moved to restraint chairs for children as safer than the restraint bed.","Monitoring a Patient in Restraints","The specific monitoring intervals are not exam material.","A patient in restraints is never left alone. Someone sits outside or inside the room with the door open, and the patient is directly observed and formally assessed at frequent intervals.","Level of awareness — Do they know what is going on, and where they are?","Level of activity — Thrashing in the restraints, or calm?","Vital signs — Part of the ongoing assessment.","Range of motion — Remove the restraints and do range of motion on all limbs, one at a time.","Circulation — Two fingers between the restraint and the limb. Check capillary refill.","Hydration, nutrition, toileting — Fluids, food, and the opportunity for elimination.","Safety and comfort — Within the restraints.","A chaotic restraint event is extremely dangerous. Act as a team with a designated team leader who tells people what to do.","Organizing a Restraint Event","Assign every role first: who leads · who talks to the patient · who checks the seclusion area · who clears the path and moves other patients out · who prepares and gives the medication · who does the 1:1 in seclusion · who documents · who l…","Always place a wedge pillow under the head of anyone restrained in a bed. Lying flat makes breathing harder, especially with a history of breathing difficulty. An extra-elevated wedge pillow exists for patients with COPD.","Restraining a child is more dangerous: the trachea, intercostal muscles, and diaphragm are underdeveloped and more pliable, so a restraint device restricts them more easily. Nonviolent Crisis Intervention (CPI) teaches holding techniques fo…","Contraindications for Seclusion & Restraint","The Six Contraindications","Extremely unstable medical and psychiatric conditions","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Spinal injury","Seizure disorders","Pregnancy","Delirium or dementia: seclusion may be intolerable because of the absence of stimulation"]},{"s":"Critical Incident Debriefing","a":"debriefing","k":["Critical Incident Debriefing","When It Happens","Immediate and mandatory after every restraint episode, with the patient and with staff who took part in or witnessed it.","If it cannot happen immediately, it must occur within the first couple of hours, or at least within the shift.","What It Addresses","Could anything have been done that would have prevented the episode? If yes, what, and why was it not done in this situation?","Did the team respond as a team?","Was safety maintained?","Did we follow hospital policies?","What could we have done differently, and what lessons were learned?","Areas of staff education that would help in future restraint episodes."]},{"s":"Teaching & Health Promotion","a":"teaching","k":["Teaching & Health Promotion","Role Model and Educator","Model appropriate responses and ways to cope with anger","Teach a variety of methods to appropriately express anger","Educate about coping skills, de-escalation techniques, and self-soothing skills to manage behavior","Assist in identifying triggers for anger and aggression","Above all, be proactive and respond early"]}]},{"f":"week1-therapies.html","t":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","secs":[{"s":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"The Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing Assessment","a":"assessment","k":["The Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing Assessment","Do a complete assessment on every patient, in any practice setting, and understand the role the mind-body connection plays in mental health disorders.","Purpose of the Assessment","Establish rapport: the foundation of everything that follows.","Understand the current problem, including the chief complaint.","Review physical status: obtain baseline vital signs.","Assess risk and safety: risk factors, and anything affecting the safety of the patient and others on the unit.","Assess psychosocial status: the dimensions of the person's life.","Identify mutual goals: set treatment goals with the client.","Formulate a plan of care: document the data in a retrievable format.","Gathering the Data","Review of systems: vital signs and all systems.","Labs: bring abnormal results to the NP, PA, or MD.","Mental status: current cognitive processes, including behavior, speech, appearance, and mood.","Validation: previous records on the unit, police reports, emergency department records.","Rating scales: the PHQ-9 and the suicide risk screening.","Psychosocial assessment: occupation, sexuality, hobbies, spirituality, coping, relationships, lifestyle."]},{"s":"Analyzing & Recognizing Cues","a":"cues","k":["Analyzing & Recognizing Cues","What You Assess, and What You Are Looking For","Affect: what you see.","Thought processes and content: circumstantial or loose? Delusional? Any hallucinations? Any suicidal thoughts?","Judgment and insight: assessed as its own area.","Mood and feelings: in the person's own words.","Physical behavior and activity: eye contact, posture, agitation, psychomotor retardation.","Movement: tics, tremors, akathisia.","Communication: speech rate (rapid, slow, pressured), volume, and fluency.","If a person is depressed, they must be assessed for suicidal tendencies. Be aware of symptoms that can mask depression. If a patient seems depressed or down, do not be afraid to ask directly: \"Are you depressed?\" and, if yes, \"Do you feel t…","Affect","Affect is the observed responsiveness of a person's emotional state: the physical manifestations and observable behavior. Gauged two ways: range and congruency.","Range","Full / broad: normal expression of feeling, sad or happy. What most people without mental illness show.","Constricted: a little less expression than normal.","Blunted: limited expression, but some is still there. May smile a little at times.","Flat: no emotion on the face whatsoever, like the masking seen in Parkinson's. A blank canvas.","Congruency","Congruent: mood and affect match.","Incongruent: \"I'm extremely happy today,\" said flatly. Crying while saying \"I'm doing great.\"","Sad also describes affect: eye contact downcast, stooped posture, and the facial expression.","Mood","Document the patient's own description of their mood. A psychiatric term can be added alongside it.","Euthymic — Normal mood.","Elated / euphoric — Abnormally elevated mood.","Dysthymic — Chronically low mood.","Labile — Mood goes up and down.","Cyclothymic — Cycling mood.","Everyday descriptors — Calm, anxious, agitated, overwhelmed, helpless, and hopeless, which is very important to document.","Screening Questions for Mood","\"How are you feeling?\"","\"Have you been discouraged / depressed / low / blue lately?\"","\"Have you been energized / elated / high / out of control lately?\""]},{"s":"Psychosocial Interventions","a":"psychosocial","k":["Psychosocial Interventions","Psychosocial interventions are non-pharmacological therapeutic techniques that address the psychological aspects (societal, familial, and cultural) through individual or group therapy, and promote mental wellness.","Group or individual therapy — Delivered one-to-one or in a group.","Music therapy — Strongly evidence-based, and not just psychiatric. Now used in NICUs and other ICUs.","Education — On the illness, its symptoms, or its treatment.","Positive self-talk — Cognitive behavioral technique to modify negative thought patterns. Improves self-esteem, resilience, and emotional regulation.","Self-monitoring — Journaling mood, feelings, or sleep. Strong because the client can keep doing it after discharge.","Enhancing communication skills — Building healthy relationships, navigating social situations, expressing needs and emotions.","Healthy relationships — Reduce isolation. Provides emotional and practical support.","Play therapy — For younger children.","Family consultations and sessions — Help relatives understand what is going on, help parents develop more effective parenting skills, and teach suicide risk and how to monitor for it. Also used where a client has a guardian, or a spouse.","Limit setting — For sexual preoccupation or agitation. Be matter-of-fact: \"What you're saying is inappropriate, and I will come back when we can interact better.\" Plan your response in advance.","Increasing coping skills and self-esteem — Recreational activities and therapies: a craft can become a coping skill and boost self-esteem. Group exercise: list three positive things about yourself.","Stress reduction and relaxation — Taught as strategies the client can use.","Spiritual support — Not necessarily about religion, but a sense of connection to something bigger than ourselves, including nature or art. Cognitive, experiential, and behavioral. Gives a person strength.","Psychological safety — Everyone contributes in groups. No answer is wrong and there are no wrong questions.","Unconditional positive regard — Given to the client regardless.","Recognition of relapse symptoms — Nurses watch for them, and clients are taught to recognize their own.","Discharge planning — Counts as a psychosocial intervention in its own right.","Group therapy (the types, the therapeutic factors, and the roles) is taught in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]},{"s":"Psychoanalytic Theory & Therapy","a":"psychoanalytic","k":["Psychoanalytic Theory & Therapy","Freud's theory, rarely used now in the acute setting. Freud believed mental disorders resulted from unresolved issues from childhood.","Three Levels of Awareness","Consciousness: everything one is aware of at a given time.","Preconsciousness: easily retrievable.","Unconscious: memories and emotions retrievable only with the help of a trained therapist. This is where a lot of clients' traumatic memories live.","Personality Structures","Id: present at birth. Unconscious, impulsive, illogical.","Ego: begins around age 2–3. The problem-solver and reality-tester.","Superego: the moral part of personality.","Freud thought all three should work together, but that the ego is in charge.","Free association — The patient shares whatever is on their mind.","Dream analysis — Analyzing dream content for specific meanings. Dreaming of falling may mean being unable to control situations.","Defense mechanism recognition — Defense mechanisms decrease anxiety and internal stress. Help patients recognize and change the overuse of mechanisms like denial and rationalization. More in Chapter 15.","Purpose: uncovering unconscious conflicts. Not seen inpatient. Runs two to three times a week, for several years. What carries forward: childhood experience shapes personality development, and traumatic memories are unconscious."]},{"s":"Behavioral Theories & the Five Behavioral Therapies","a":"behavioral","k":["Behavioral Theories & the Five Behavioral Therapies","Personality consists of learned behavior, influenced by conditioning: pairing a behavior with a condition that reinforces or decreases it (Pavlov's dog and the bell). Three textbook theories gave rise to behavioral therapy: classical condit…","Modeling — Demonstrating behaviors more effective than the current ones. A lot like role-playing.","Operant conditioning — Using positive reinforcement to increase the behaviors you want to see: tokens earned for going to groups, completing ADLs, and asking for meds, rising through levels that unlock privileges.","Systematic desensitization — Evidence-based. Combines relaxation techniques with gradual exposure to overcome a phobia. Can be virtual, in vivo (real world), or imaginal.","Aversion therapy — The opposite of operant conditioning: a negative stimulus to decrease unwanted behavior. Disulfiram (Antabuse) in alcohol dependence: drinking on it causes nausea, dizziness, and flushing, so drinking decreases.","Biofeedback — The patient learns to control normally involuntary bodily responses (blood pressure, brain waves, heart rate) using electrical sensors that feed back information about the body. Used for stress, pain, anxiety, headaches, and A…","Modeling = role playing · operant conditioning = tokens / positive reinforcement · systematic desensitization = exposure · aversion therapy = negative stimulus decreases behavior · biofeedback = control of body."]},{"s":"Talk Therapies & Interpersonal Therapy","a":"talk-therapies","k":["Talk Therapies & Interpersonal Therapy","Medications and talk therapy are the two main treatments for mental illness.","Interpersonal therapy targets relationships by improving functioning and communication patterns. Very short-term: 12 to 16 sessions.","Identification of emotion — Identify what the emotion is and where it is coming from.","Expression of emotion — Express it in a healthy way: calmly telling a partner how you feel, instead of acting out of anger and sarcasm.","Dealing with emotional baggage — Unresolved issues from past relationships are carried into present ones. Examining them shows how they affect current mood and behavior."]},{"s":"Cognitive Therapy & CBT","a":"cbt","k":["Cognitive Therapy & CBT","Cognitive therapy is the umbrella term for therapies focused on thoughts (cognitions), with a dynamic interplay between the individual and the environment.","The core CBT idea: our thoughts cause our feelings and behaviors, not external things like people or situations. So if we change the way we think, we can change the way we feel, and act better even if the situation does not change.","Cognitive Distortions: \"Stinkin' Thinkin'\"","Part of CBT: taking the irrational internal commentary we run all day and making it more rational and more positive. Developed at Hazelden, then taken further by David Burns in The Feeling Good Handbook. The set is his \"Top 10 Stinkin' Thin…","1. All-or-nothing thinking — Seeing things in black or white.","2. Overgeneralization — A single negative event becomes always or never: \"I'm never going to get a promotion.\"","3. Mental filter — Dwelling excessively on one negative and ignoring all the positive feedback.","4. Discounting the positive — Rejecting positive experiences: \"that doesn't count,\" \"that was just a fluke.\"","5. Jumping to conclusions — Interpreting things negatively with no facts: mind reading and fortune telling.","6. Magnification — Blowing something out of proportion.","7. Emotional reasoning — Treating a feeling as evidence of fact.","8. Should statements — \"I should have done this, I should have done that.\"","9. Labeling — All-or-nothing thinking taken to an extreme: instead of \"I made a mistake,\" you say \"I'm a loser.\"","10. Personalization and blame — Holding yourself personally responsible for something that was not even under your control."]},{"s":"Rational Emotive Therapy (RET)","a":"ret","k":["Rational Emotive Therapy (RET)","RET explores irrational thinking and beliefs: identifying activating situations and the negative emotions that lead to irrational beliefs, then teaching the client to delete the \"must,\" \"should,\" \"ought,\" and \"have to.\"","The ABCs of RET","Activating event: \"I receive an invitation to a party.\"","Beliefs: \"I hate parties, I don't like hanging out with people.\"","Consequences, emotional: \"I feel miserable.\"","The therapy works on B, because changing beliefs changes the emotional consequences.","Desired outcome: the client controls their behavior and thinking, and changed thinking produces changed behavior."]},{"s":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)","a":"dbt","k":["Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)","DBT involves the integration of opposites. Originally developed to treat chronically suicidal individuals, many of whom had borderline personality disorder. It also helps with other self-destructive or self-injurious behaviors such as eatin…","What it combines — Cognitive and behavioral techniques combined with mindfulness.","Setting — Both inpatient and outpatient, but more often outpatient, because it takes one to two years before you see the best results.","Core interventions — Problem-solving · exposure technique · skill training · contingency management · cognitive modification.","The four primary modes of treatment — Group skills training · individual psychotherapy · telephone contact · therapist consultation and team meetings.","The team — Several people, including an MD psychiatrist, not just a therapist or social worker. You have one primary therapist you check in with regularly.","The Four Skill Sets","Emotional regulation — Managing and labeling intense moods and emotions.","Interpersonal effectiveness — Problem-solving skills and assertiveness, including being able to say no, which many people find very hard.","Distress tolerance — Also appears as self-management: \"learning to control, manage, or change your behaviors, thoughts, and emotions.\"","Mindfulness — Being present in the moment. Meditation skills.","Mindfulness: indulging a wandering mind reinforces the emotions that make us suffer, and most negative thoughts are about the past or the future. The past is over. The future is unreal until it happens. The present moment is the only one yo…"]}]},{"f":"week1-group-physiologic-therapies.html","t":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","secs":[{"s":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topics 5 & 6"]},{"s":"Group Dynamics","a":"what-is-a-group","k":["Group Dynamics","Group work: individuals with a common purpose come together and benefit by mutually giving and receiving feedback. A group is two or more people pursuing common goals or interests, so patient and family education counts as leading a group.","Characteristics of a Group","Size","Defined purpose","Degree of similarity: members share the problem the group is built around, even when the similarity isn't visible.","Rules: one person speaks at a time. No phone calls.","Boundaries","Content and process: what is talked about, and what goes on inside the group.","Who Can Lead What","A registered nurse: activity, educational, task, and support groups.","Advanced degree required: group psychotherapy, aimed at bringing about personality change."]},{"s":"Types of Groups","a":"group-types","k":["Types of Groups","Psychoeducational groups increase knowledge about a specific psychological or somatic subject. The group types and Yalom's factors are also the categories the peer support paper has to cover.","Medication or health education — Symptom management or disease education, such as a diabetes group on a med-surg floor.","Dual diagnosis — Both a substance use disorder and another mental health disorder, such as major depressive disorder.","Symptom management — Managing anxiety. Stress management.","Recreational activity — Ping pong, the art room, cards. Not recreational therapy, which requires a recreational therapist or someone with an advanced degree.","Interpersonal skills — Enhances knowledge and improves skills so clients can solve their problems.","Therapeutic Community Meeting","Essential group on the inpatient unit, usually in the morning.","Covers disruptions and disagreements on the unit and reinforces the rules: ten minutes at a time on the phone, because others need it.","Where the unit works out how to make things better.","Support / Self-Help Groups","Very structured.","Provide the opportunity to maintain or enhance personal and social functioning through cooperation and shared understanding of life's challenges.","AA, cancer survivors, bereavement, substance abuse, surviving suicide. Can be very empowering.","The therapeutic milieu and its five components are taught in Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing"]},{"s":"Yalom's Therapeutic Factors","a":"yalom","k":["Yalom's Therapeutic Factors","Yalom identified 11 factors that make group therapy work.","Instillation of hope — Leader and members share optimism about improvement: \"you got better, so maybe I can too.\"","Universality — Clients realize they are not alone: \"I'm not the only one who hears voices,\" \"I'm not the only one with thoughts of suicide.\"","Imparting of information — Participants receive formal teaching from the leader, or advice from peers.","Altruism — Members gain from giving support to others, which improves self-value. Doing it for someone else, not for yourself.","Corrective recapitulation — Members repeat behavior patterns learned in their families, get feedback, and work out how to correct the unhealthy ones.","Development of socializing techniques — New social skills learned from feedback and modeling. The group is a safe place to try them.","Imitative behavior — Members copy behavior from leaders or peers and adopt healthier habits.","Interpersonal learning — Insight into themselves from others' feedback, with the choice to change. Happens in later phases, in an established group.","Group cohesiveness — In a mature group, each member feels connected to the others, the leader, and the group as a whole, and can accept both positive feedback and constructive criticism.","Catharsis — A therapeutic discharge of emotion through experiencing and expressing feelings: \"this has really helped me get in touch with my sadness.\"","Existential resolution — Members examine what affects everyone (loneliness, mortality, responsibility) and construct meaning."]},{"s":"Advantages & Disadvantages of Group Therapy","a":"advantages","k":["Advantages & Disadvantages of Group Therapy","Advantages","Engages multiple clients in treatment at the same time, which is why group is the main treatment on the units.","Participants benefit from the feedback, knowledge, and life experience of others.","A safe setting to try out new behaviors and communication skills.","Promotes a feeling of belonging.","Disadvantages","Time constraints: a unit group runs about 45 minutes. With 15 clients, participation time feels short, especially if someone monopolizes it.","Confidentiality cannot be guaranteed: no promise that every member keeps what is said in the group.","Disruptive members. Someone acutely psychotic, manic, or intoxicated cannot sit through a group or interact effectively and does not benefit from group treatment. Leaders will try to get them out of it."]},{"s":"Group Leadership & Member Roles","a":"leadership","k":["Group Leadership & Member Roles","Leadership Styles","Autocratic — Exerts control, runs the group, and does not encourage much interaction between members, as in an educational group heavy on information.","Democratic — Supports extensive group interaction in the process of problem-solving: psychotherapy groups, and AA.","Laissez-faire — Allows members to behave however they choose and does not control the group's direction. Starts it and lets it go where it goes. AA groups are usually democratic or laissez-faire.","Task Roles","Initiator-contributor — Suggests new ideas, or different ways of seeing the problem or goal.","Information seeker — Tries to clarify the group's goals.","Information giver — Provides facts and shares experiences as an authority figure.","Coordinator — Clarifies how different ideas can work together.","Orienteer — Notes the group's progress toward its goals.","Recorder — Keeps notes and acts as the group's memory.","Building & Maintenance Roles","Oriented toward the group's function: they strengthen, regulate, and keep it going.","Encourager — Praises, and seeks input.","Harmonizer — Reconciles differences among members.","Gatekeeper — Facilitates the contributions of others, to keep communication open.","Group observer — Notes and reports what is occurring in the group process.","Follower — Goes along with others, an audience role.","Individual Roles","Meet the need of only one member and hamper rather than enhance group functioning.","Aggressor — Criticizes or attacks others' ideas or feelings.","Blocker — Oppositional: resists progress. Argues or disagrees beyond reason.","Recognition seeker — Calls attention to themselves. Boasts about their achievements.","Play person — Horses around, plays on their phone, tells jokes.","Dominator — Asserts authority and superiority. Manipulates or interrupts others, sometimes with flattery.","Handling Challenging Members","Monopolizing member — Address it as the group goes: everyone has ten minutes, or an equal chance to contribute. Sometimes take the member aside privately after the meeting.","Demoralizing member — Listen objectively, then ask in private what is going on with their anger. Be matter-of-fact.","Silent member — Determine the cause. Provide extra time to think about a response and come back to them.","The member who needs extra time — Slowed psychomotor functioning in severe depression means a longer wait for an answer. Silence is not always resistance. Offer to come back to them at the end of group."]},{"s":"Phototherapy (Light Therapy)","a":"phototherapy","k":["Phototherapy (Light Therapy)","The device — A fluorescent light box with a strong UV filter, about a 10,000 lux light source.","How it is used — Set it off to the side: do not look directly into it. 30 to 45 minutes a day, two to three times a week, usually in the morning, most often during fall and winter months.","Indications — Depression occurring during pregnancy, so medications can be avoided. Also major depressive disorder with a seasonal pattern, where it is first-line treatment.","Terminology change — Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) was removed in DSM-5 and reclassified as major depressive disorder with seasonal onset / seasonal pattern.","Mechanism — Efficacy is credited to the influence of light on melatonin. It stimulates the pineal gland, increasing melatonin and serotonin.","How well it works — As effective as medication for SAD.","Side effects — Headache and jitteriness. Also eye strain, and rarely nausea, sweating, or sedation. Most people have none at all.","Light therapy can trigger mania or hypomania. Before prescribing it for depression, investigate thoroughly to make sure the diagnosis is not actually bipolar disorder. Otherwise it is very safe."]},{"s":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","a":"ect","k":["Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","ECT is the most effective treatment for depression, and it is non-invasive.","Indications","Severe long-term depression: primary treatment with malnutrition, exhaustion, or dehydration. Rehydrate before ECT.","Certain medical conditions: safer than medications in some conditions. Avoids polypharmacy in the elderly.","Delusional depression: works very well.","Psychotic illnesses: the second most common indication for ECT.","Refractory depression: has not responded to multiple medication trials, or keeps recurring despite treatment. Not usually first-line. ECT follows failed trials.","Catatonia: works especially well in schizophrenia with catatonia.","The Procedure","Anesthesia: done under anesthesia with a muscle relaxant.","Electrode placement: electricity applied to the temporal regions: unilateral (one side) or bilateral (an electrode on each temple).","Course: two to three treatments a week, for a total of 6 to 12 treatments.","The stimulus: an electrical current lasting 1 second or less.","The seizure: a brief seizure of 30 to 60 seconds, monitored throughout. One extremity is left un-paralyzed so the convulsion can be observed.","With or without medication: can be used either way. Sometimes it helps boost SSRIs. Improves energy, appetite, sleep, and mood.","How it works: not exactly known. Theories: alters neurotransmitter receptors and can increase serotonin. Regulates stress hormones. Increases cerebral blood flow, possibly neurogenesis.","Cardiac: the heart is heavily stressed at the onset of the seizure and for up to ten minutes after. Assess and manage in hypertension, congestive heart failure, cardiac arrhythmias, and other cardiac conditions.Neurologic: the brain is stre…","Waking — About 15 minutes after the procedure.","Confusion and disorientation — Very common, lasting several hours. Reorienting afterward is part of the procedure, not just a reaction to it. Tell the family of an outpatient they may need to do the same.","Retrograde amnesia — Loss of the events leading up to and including the treatment itself, usually just the morning before and shortly after. Most patients experience it."]},{"s":"TMS, Vagus Nerve Stimulation & Deep Brain Stimulation","a":"brain-stimulation","k":["TMS, Vagus Nerve Stimulation & Deep Brain Stimulation","Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS): Non-Invasive","What it is — MRI-strength magnetic pulses that stimulate focal areas of the cerebral cortex. FDA approved for treatment-resistant depression.","The course — Outpatient, 30 minutes, five days a week for four to six weeks.","What happens — Patient is awake and alert. An electromagnet on the scalp sends short pulses through the prefrontal cortex, inducing neurons to fire. Feels like a slight tapping, sometimes with scalp contractions and jaw tightening.","Safety profile — No neurological deficits, no memory problems, no loss of consciousness. Seizures rarely. Most reactions mild: scalp tingling and discomfort at the site. Patients can continue their medications.","Adverse reactions — Headache and lightheadedness.","The one contraindication for TMS is metal in the area of stimulation: brain stimulators, cochlear implants, medication pumps, bullet fragments. Also not used in anyone with a pacemaker or a history of seizures.","Vagus Nerve Stimulation (VNS): Invasive","Origin — Originally for epilepsy. It decreases seizures and improves mood, so it is FDA approved for partial onset seizures and depression.","The device — A pulse generator the size of a pacemaker in the chest, wired up and wrapped around the vagus nerve in the neck.","How it fires — Stimulates the left cervical vagus nerve, pulsing 30 seconds every 5 minutes, 24 hours a day.","Mechanism — Boosts neurotransmitter levels by activating the limbic system.","Used for — Invasive, so generally reserved for treatment-resistant depression.","Side effects — Voice alteration in nearly 60% of patients, plus hoarseness, headache, neck pain, cough, paresthesia, and dyspnea, all decreasing with time. Implanting a device also carries infection risk.","Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS): Most Invasive","Very seldom used. Electrodes implanted in the chest, threaded up the neck and into the scalp, with probes placed into the brain.","A treatment for Parkinson's disease, now investigated in depression, stimulating regions identified as underactive in depression.","Risk is not only chest infection. Something is placed inside the brain."]},{"s":"Exercise & Herbal Treatments","a":"exercise-integrative","k":["Exercise & Herbal Treatments","Exercise","Combats the harmful effects of stress, anxiety, and depression. Any exercise is good.","Regular exercisers with depression and anxiety report elevated mood and greater happiness.","Alters serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine, and dampens the HPA axis, thought to be overly active in depression.","Has biological, social, and psychological effects.","Less expensive than ECT, fewer side effects than medication.","Herbal treatments are one of the seven key terms to know.","St. John's Wort — Mild-to-moderate depression. A flower processed into tea or tablets, thought to increase serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine.","Ginseng — Stress or fatigue.","Valerian root · Chamomile — Depression, insomnia, stress, or anxiety.","SAM-e — Depression.","Herbals are not regulated by the FDA. The concentration of active ingredients varies between brands and formulas, and much more research is needed."]}]},{"f":"week2-depressive-disorders.html","t":"Depressive Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Depressive Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Background & Epidemiology","a":"background","k":["Background & Epidemiology","Scope — About 20 million people in the US affected. Roughly half the population is affected over a lifetime.","Treatability — Responds very well to treatment: 80%–90% of adults and 70%–80% of children respond.","Comorbidity — Rarely occurs alone. Frequently accompanies substance use, eating disorders, and personality disorders. Anxiety with depression is the most common presentation.","Sex — Women twice as often as men.","Race — Occurs more often in Caucasians, but is more severe in African Americans.","Seasonality — Increased incidence where sunlight is decreased: depression with a seasonal onset, formerly called seasonal affective disorder.","Socioeconomic status — More frequent in lower-income areas. The direction of causation is unclear: low status may lead to depression, or depression may limit the ability to reach a higher status."]},{"s":"Etiology & Contributing Factors","a":"etiology","k":["Etiology & Contributing Factors","No single cause. Identifying the causal factors is what directs treatment toward therapy, medication, or both.","Biological Factors","Genetic: runs in families.","Neurotransmitters: serotonin and norepinephrine, sometimes dopamine, glutamate, and acetylcholine.","Electrolyte disturbances, and cortisol is higher in depressed individuals.","Hormonal imbalance: the estrogen-to-progesterone ratio. Thyroid plays a huge role. About 25% of people with depression have a hypoactive thyroid, so T3, T4, and TSH are checked on admission.","Inflammatory process.","Psychological Factors","Cognitive theory: thoughts lead to emotions. Negative or unrealistic expectations of the environment, the self, and the future lead to dissatisfaction.","Learning theory, learned helplessness: repeated exposure to uncontrollable stressors, where nothing the person tries helps, leads to failing to use any coping skills at all.","The diathesis-stress model also applies here — the predisposition-plus-stress explanation is taught in Introduction to PMH Nursing","The eight neurotransmitters and what each one does are taught in Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Contributing Factors Across the Lifespan","Children — Detachment from a primary caregiver, physical or emotional. A secure attachment with one primary caregiver is protective. Parental separation, divorce, or foster placement. Death of a loved one, including a pet. Relocation. Acade…","Teens — Adult and child factors apply, plus conflict between independence and maturation, and role confusion. Grief and loss, including a relationship breakup, is a frequent precursor.","Older adults — Societal attitudes that value money, youth, and productivity over wisdom. Financial problems. Job loss or retirement, which also severs social connections. Relocation. Chronic illness and chronic pain. Bereavement overload fr…"]},{"s":"Major Depressive Disorder","a":"mdd","k":["Major Depressive Disorder","All the depressive disorders share sadness, emptiness, irritability, somatic concerns, and impaired thinking, and all impair the ability to function.","Adults","Five or more symptoms present daily in the same 2-week period, and at least one must be depressed mood or anhedonia (loss of interest or pleasure in almost all activities).","Weight or appetite change · sleep disturbance · fatigue · psychomotor agitation or retardation · worthlessness or guilt · loss of ability to concentrate · recurrent thoughts of death.","Symptoms cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning.","Not attributable to a substance, medication, or medical condition.","No manic or hypomanic episode. If one ever occurs, the MDD diagnosis is dropped and replaced by a bipolar diagnosis.","Children & Teens","The two sources give different counts. The pre-lecture material requires five or more symptoms in a 2-week period. The live review requires three or more of the top criteria. Both agree that BOTH depressed or irritable, cranky mood AND anhe…","Plus any three of: significant weight loss or decreased appetite (more than 5% of body weight in a month, or failure to meet expected weight gain) · insomnia or hypersomnia · psychomotor agitation or retardation · fatigue · worthlessness or…","Also: frequent vague physical complaints, school absence and poor performance, boredom (which may be anhedonia), alcohol or substance use, increased anger or hostility, reckless behavior.","Assessment relies on behaviors and statements, plus collateral from parents and teachers. Children may not have the words for how they feel.","About 20% of MDD becomes chronic, lasting more than two years. Recurrent episodes are the norm rather than the exception, and most recurrences happen within the first year. Specifiers carry that detail in the chart: most often chronic, epis…","Depression Symptoms in Children by Age","Up to age 3 — Failure to thrive, feeding problems, lack of playfulness, lack of emotional expression, delay in speech or motor development.","Ages 3–5 — Prone to accidents, phobias, aggressiveness, excessive self-reproach for minor infractions.","Ages 6–8 — Vague physical complaints, aggressive behavior, clinging to parents, avoiding new people and challenges, behind in social skills and academic performance.","Ages 9–12 — Morbid thoughts, excessive worrying, lack of social interest, thinking they have disappointed their parents."]},{"s":"The Other Depressive Disorders","a":"other-disorders","k":["The Other Depressive Disorders","Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) — Diagnosed only in children 6–18, with onset before age 10. Core feature is chronic, severe, persistent irritability: verbal or behavioral temper outbursts at least 3× weekly for at least a yea…","Persistent Depressive Disorder — Formerly dysthymia. Low-level depressed feelings most of the day, the majority of days, for at least 2 years in adults: an underlying depressed feeling, less severe than MDD but ongoing. Requires two or more…","Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder — Affects about 5% of women. Severe mood changes in the week before menstruation: mood swings, irritability, depression, anxiety, feeling overwhelmed, difficulty concentrating, plus low energy, overeating, hy…","Substance-Induced Depressive Disorder — The person does not experience depressive symptoms in the absence of drug or alcohol use or withdrawal.","Depressive Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition — Symptoms result from a medical diagnosis or its medications (kidney failure, Parkinson's disease, and Alzheimer's disease, for example) and are not counted as major depressive disorder.…"]},{"s":"Assessment & the Clinical Judgment Model","a":"assessment","k":["Assessment & the Clinical Judgment Model","Recognize cues — Assess affect, mood, thought process and content, judgment and insight, communication, and physical behavior and activity.","Analyze cues — Risk for suicide. Safety is always the highest priority. Also hopelessness, ineffective coping, social isolation, and self-care deficit. Hopelessness and loss of hope make self-harm more likely.","Prioritize hypotheses — Rank the problems by urgency, complexity, and time.","Generate solutions — Use the recovery model: healing is possible and attainable, focus on the patient's strengths, develop goals mutually from their needs and values, and make outcomes measurable: specific, patient-centered, and with a time…","Take action — Therapeutic communication. Teach challenging and restructuring negative thinking. Administer medications and provide education. Observe for safety. Help the patient list three people they can call with phone numbers, kept some…","Screening Tools","PHQ-9 (Patient Health Questionnaire): the one used in clinical assessment.","Zung Depression Scale · Hamilton Depression Rating Scale · Geriatric Depression Scale · Beck Depression Inventory · Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale.","Why screening matters: people do not seek help for depression. They present to primary care with the somatic complaints that go with it, such as insomnia, fatigue, or weight loss. Hence the push for depression screening at every visit. The …","The full PMH nursing assessment, including affect versus mood, is taught in Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Cognitive distortions and the restructuring techniques taught to patients are covered in Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Psychomotor Retardation vs. Agitation","Psychomotor Retardation","Visible slowing of physical activity: movement or speech.","Slow talking, long pauses before speaking, taking a long time to cross a room, slow chewing with long waits between bites.","Associated with severe depression.","Psychomotor Agitation","An increase in activity brought on by mental tension.","Restlessness, pacing, tapping fingers or feet, abruptly starting and stopping tasks, meaninglessly moving objects around.","Associated with agitated depression."]},{"s":"Antidepressant Principles","a":"antidepressant-principles","k":["Antidepressant Principles","The antidepressants on the Exam Focus slides are also gathered by class, with the other Week 2 drugs, in Medications","Antidepressant therapy is effective for most cases of MDD, especially severe ones, but psychotherapy combined with an antidepressant is superior to either alone. All antidepressants increase the availability of neurotransmitters and have si…","Physiological symptoms improve before psychological symptoms. Energy returns while mood is still depressed, and that is exactly when the risk of acting on suicidal thoughts is highest. Teach patients, caregivers, and family members this.","General Antidepressant Education","Symptom improvement may not appear for 4–6 weeks.","Look first for improved sleep, less daytime fatigue, less crying and irritability, and increased frustration tolerance.","Side effects are usually handled by adjusting the dose or switching to another drug in the same class.","Do not stop when feeling better. That causes relapse. Medication is needed 6–9 months past symptom relief, so most patients take it 12–24 months before tapering.","Antidepressants are not addictive, but stopping abruptly causes withdrawal: nausea, anxiety, insomnia, flu-like symptoms.","No alcohol. Alcohol is a stronger depressant than any available antidepressant.","Watch for emerging mania. A shift to elevated mood or markedly increased energy soon after starting an antidepressant is a warning sign, not improvement.","Order of Drug Classes Tried","SSRIs / SRIs","First-line. If one fails, a second drug in the same class is tried before moving on.","Tricyclics (TCAs)","Second choice. Cheaper, but anticholinergic, cardiotoxic, and lethal in overdose.","MAOIs","Third choice. Reserved for treatment-resistant depression because of the food and drug restrictions."]},{"s":"SSRIs, SNRIs & Serotonin Syndrome","a":"sris","k":["SSRIs, SNRIs & Serotonin Syndrome","SSRIsselective serotonin reuptake inhibitors — fluoxetine · paroxetine · sertraline · citalopram · escitalopram · vortioxetine · vilazodone — First-line therapy. Block the neuronal uptake of serotonin, leaving more in the synaptic cleft. Fe…","SNaRIsserotonin noradrenergic reuptake inhibitors — venlafaxine · duloxetine — Act on both serotonin and norepinephrine. Usually a few more side effects than SSRIs.","SNRIserotonin norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor — desvenlafaxine — A metabolite of venlafaxine.","Dry mouth * — Increase fluids. Sugar-free gum.","Sexual problems * — Reduced desire, difficulty reaching orgasm, or inability to maintain an erection. Reported in up to 80% of people taking these medications.","Headache * — Symptomatic care.","Blurred vision * — Symptomatic care.","Drowsiness — Take the dose at night.","Nausea — Take with food, or at bedtime.","Insomnia — Take the dose in the morning.","Diarrhea — Increase fiber.","Nervousness, agitation, restlessness, dizziness — Teach relaxation techniques. Avoid caffeine if anxious.","* The most common side effects.","Nursing Implications","Effectiveness takes 4–8 weeks. Most side effects diminish in 4–6 weeks.","Monitor for suicidal ideation, extreme agitation, fever, increased blood pressure, and manic symptoms.","Teach sleep hygiene and relaxation techniques.","Abrupt discontinuation may be mild or severe: \"electrical surges\" or \"brain shivers\" in the head or body, pins-and-needles sensations on the skin, blackouts, short-term memory loss, and feeling on the verge of unconsciousness.","Discontinuation syndrome and why psychotropics are tapered rather than stopped are taught in Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Serotonin Syndrome","Rare but potentially life-threatening. 60% of patients who develop it do so within 6 hours of a first dose, a change in dosing, or an intentional overdose. If the serotonergic medication is not discontinued it progresses to worsening myoclo…","Mental status — Agitation, confusion, restlessness, lethargy, delirium, irritability, dizziness, hallucinations.","Autonomic — Diaphoresis, flushing, fever, tachycardia, mydriasis.","Neuromuscular — Myoclonus (muscle twitching or jerks), hyperreflexia, tremors.","Gastrointestinal — Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.","Treating Serotonin Syndrome","Stop or reduce the contributing medications, depending on symptom severity and as directed by the prescriber. This is the first action.","Benzodiazepines (diazepam or lorazepam) for agitation, seizures, and muscle stiffness.","Avoid antipsychotics such as haloperidol for the agitation. They can worsen hyperthermia and contribute to a neuroleptic malignant syndrome–like reaction. The agitation here is an internal restlessness, not outward aggression.","Oxygen to maintain normal levels. IV fluids for dehydration and fever. Symptomatic and supportive care.","For tachycardia or hypertension: esmolol or nitroprusside. For hypotension: phenylephrine or epinephrine.","If other treatments are not working, a serotonin-production blocker: cyproheptadine HCl.","Symptoms usually resolve within 24 hours, but can take several weeks to clear completely."]},{"s":"Tricyclic Antidepressants","a":"tcas","k":["Tricyclic Antidepressants","imipramine · desipramine · doxepin · amitriptyline. Block the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin at the presynaptic neuron in the CNS, which is thought to elevate mood. Results take 10–14 days to appear and full effects 4–8 weeks. Sta…","Deadly in overdose, through fatal CNS depression, and equally so combined with alcohol or other CNS depressants. The cardiovascular effects are the serious ones: dysrhythmias, tachycardia, MI, and heart block. Contraindicated after an MI or…","Before and During Treatment","Check an ECG for cardiac disorder before initiating.","Check for a history of seizure disorder.","Alpha-adrenergic blockade produces orthostatic hypotension and tachycardia. Dizziness and falls are the worry in older adults. TCA use is considered risky in older adults and in anyone with cardiac disease.","Often given at night. The sedation aids sleep, and the side effects fall during sleeping hours.","Warn about early morning awakening, feeling worse in the morning, worry and anxiety early in treatment. These ease as the day goes on.","Managing Anticholinergic Effects","Dry mouth: sugar-free hard candy or gum.","GI upset: take the medication with food.","Diarrhea: frequent small meals.","Constipation: increase fiber, fluids, and exercise.","Insomnia: sleep hygiene. Change the dosing time: take in the morning if it causes insomnia.","Orthostasis: stay hydrated. Get up slowly.","Sexual side effects: erectile dysfunction medications.","Urinary hesitancy: run water while voiding. Measure the amount.","Anticholinergic side effects. Hot as a hare: increased temperature · Dry as a bone: dry mouth and eyes, urinary retention, constipation · Blind as a bat: mydriasis, dilated pupils, blurred vision · Red as a beet: flushed face · Mad as a hat…"]},{"s":"MAOIs & Hypertensive Crisis","a":"maois","k":["MAOIs & Hypertensive Crisis","phenelzine · isocarboxazid · tranylcypromine · selegiline (Emsam), a transdermal patch. Block monoamine oxidase, the enzyme that metabolizes serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, and tyramine, so the mood-elevating neurotransmitters increase…","Early — Irritability, anxiety, flushing, sweating, and a severe headache.","Established — Occipital headache, palpitations, nausea and vomiting, hypertension but with orthostatic hypotension on standing, neck stiffness or soreness, dilated pupils, photophobia, blurred vision, chest pain, tachycardia or bradycardia,…","Life-threatening — Severe fever, seizures, then coma or death. Can also produce intracranial hemorrhage.","Prevention & Treatment","Monitor blood pressure routinely, especially during the first 6 weeks. Crisis occurs 15–90 minutes after ingesting a contraindicated substance.","Avoid Demerol, and be very careful with OTC cold medications.","Rarely used with an SSRI. The combination commonly causes serotonin syndrome.","Limit caffeine, chocolate, and avocados.","Treatment: phentolamine, an alpha-adrenergic blocker and vasodilator. Sublingual nifedipine, a calcium channel blocker that relaxes cardiac muscle. Plus symptomatic and supportive care.","Tyramine-rich foods to avoid. Aged, smoked, fermented, marinated, and processed meats (pepperoni, bologna, salami, pickled herring, liver, frankfurters, bacon, ham) · aged cheeses (blue, cheddar, provolone, brie, Romano, parmesan, swiss) · …","Dry mouth · nausea · diarrhea or constipation · headache · drowsiness · insomnia · dizziness or lightheadedness · skin reaction at the patch site. — Involuntary muscle jerks · low blood pressure · reduced sexual desire or orgasm · weight ga…"]},{"s":"Other Antidepressants & Other Treatments","a":"other-antidepressants","k":["Other Antidepressants & Other Treatments","bupropion(NDRI) — Contraindicated in eating disorders and any history of seizures. Fewer sexual side effects, and considered energizing. Used when fatigue is the prominent symptom. Also used for smoking cessation, as Zyban.","trazodone — Chemically similar to the TCAs. So sedating that it is now used mainly at bedtime for sleep, typically 50–200 mg PO at bedtime PRN insomnia. 300–500 mg is needed for an antidepressant effect. Not addicting, which is why it is pr…","mirtazapine(NaSSA) — Noradrenergic and specific serotonergic. Given at bedtime: good for sleep.","Antidepressants in Children & Teens","SSRIs carry a black box warning for increased risk of suicidality (suicidal thinking or attempts) in children and adolescents.","After the warning, prescribing fell and suicide attempts increased: the risk of suicide is greater in depressed children and adolescents who do not take antidepressants. Part of the reason is the same energy-before-mood gap: physiological s…","fluoxetine is first-line. Venlafaxine is used off label. Both have low side effects: mild nausea, headache, stomachache.","Full effect can take up to 12 weeks, and improvement is gradual enough that patients often only notice after one to two months. Usually given for 6–24 months.","TCAs are avoided: more side effects, potential for dysrhythmias, lethal in overdose, and children and adolescents can be more impulsive.","Other Treatments & Advanced Practice","Other Treatments","Phototherapy · ECT · transcranial magnetic stimulation · vagus nerve stimulation · very seldom deep brain stimulation.","St. John's wort, SAMe, omega-3 fatty acids.","Exercise. Decreasing caffeine and alcohol.","Acupuncture, massage, relaxation techniques, meditation, guided imagery.","Advanced Practice Interventions","Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT).","Interpersonal therapy (IPT).","Time-limited focused psychotherapy.","Behavior therapy.","Group therapy.","Phototherapy, ECT, TMS, VNS, and DBS are each taught in full in Group & Physiologic Therapies","CBT, interpersonal therapy, and the behavioral therapies are taught in Assessment, Theories & Therapies"]}]},{"f":"week2-bipolar-disorders.html","t":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Epidemiology & Risk Factors","a":"epidemiology","k":["Epidemiology & Risk Factors","Commonly misdiagnosed — Up to 21% of patients diagnosed with major depression have an undiagnosed bipolar disorder. First episodes are depressive, and more time is spent in the depressive phase than in mania or hypomania.","Bipolar I — More common in males. Average age of onset 18.","Bipolar II — More common in females. Usually starts around age 20.","Cyclothymia — Usually begins in adolescence or early adulthood. Occurs about equally in both sexes.","Comorbidity — Up to 75% also have an anxiety disorder. Up to 50% have a substance use disorder.","Genetic — Strong link: runs in families. Several genes contribute to the expression of the disorder.","Neurotransmitters — Norepinephrine, dopamine, GABA, and serotonin. One view: too few in depression, too many in mania. The problem may also be at the receptor site.","Neurobiological — Changes in brain structure and function: dysfunction in the prefrontal cortical region, hippocampus, and amygdala, which may produce the emotional lability and mood dysregulation.","Neuroendocrine — The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid-adrenal (HPTA) axis. Hypothyroidism is the most common physical abnormality found in people who also have a bipolar disorder.","Environmental — Stressful family life and adverse events produce a more severe course of illness, and stress is a common trigger for both mania and depression.","The hippocampus and amygdala and what each does are taught in Neurobiology & Pharmacology"]},{"s":"The Three Bipolar Disorders","a":"types","k":["The Three Bipolar Disorders","The DSM-5 lists seven bipolar and related disorders: bipolar I, bipolar II, cyclothymic disorder, substance- or medication-induced, due to another medical condition, other specified, and unspecified. The three below are the focus. They diff…","Cyclothymic Disorder","A mild mix of both poles. Fluctuating hypomanic and mild-to-moderate depressive symptoms, but no full episode of either.","Bipolar II","At least one hypomanic episode plus at least one major depressive episode. Never a manic episode.","Bipolar I","The most severe form, with the highest mortality rate of the three. At least one manic episode.","Specifiers","With mixed features — Symptoms of both depression and mania at the same time. Rare. Charted as, for example, \"bipolar I disorder with mixed features.\"","With rapid cycling — Four or more manic episodes for at least 2 weeks in 12 months, with partial or full remission for 2 months at a time or a switch to the opposite episode. High risk of recurrence, resistant to drug therapy, greater sever…","Rapid cycling is not mood lability. Rapid cycling is measured in episodes across a year. Mood lability shifts every one or two hours and is more what you would see in borderline personality disorder."]},{"s":"The Manic Episode & Bipolar I","a":"mania","k":["The Manic Episode & Bipolar I","Mania is a medical emergency. It requires pharmacotherapy, and hospitalization is often necessary.","Diagnostic Criteria for Bipolar I","A. Abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood and increased goal-directed activity or energy, for at least 1 week.","B. Three or more of the seven symptoms below, four if the mood is only irritable.","C. Symptoms cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, or require hospitalization to prevent harm to self or others, or there are psychotic features.","D. Not caused by substance use, medication, withdrawal, or a medical condition.","DIG FAST: the clinical features of mania and hypomania: Distractibility · Indiscretion · Grandiosity · Flight of ideas · Activity increase · Sleep deficit · Talkativeness.","Distractibility — Attention too easily drawn to unimportant external stimuli. Multiple overlapping projects, often at unusual hours.","Indiscretion — Excessive involvement in pleasurable activities with high potential for painful consequences: spending sprees, hypersexuality, gambling, profuse generosity. Also covers impaired judgment and impulse control, and the legal and…","Grandiosity — Inflated self-esteem, often to the point of delusion: a special relationship with a famous person, a special invention such as a cure for cancer, or religious grandiosity.","Flight of ideas — Racing thoughts, or the subjective experience that thoughts are racing.","Activity increase — Increase in goal-directed activity, or psychomotor agitation: purposeless, non-goal-directed activity and pacing.","Sleep deficit — Decreased need for sleep: feeling rested after only three hours, or needing sleep but unable to get there because the mind is racing.","Talkativeness — Pressured speech: rapid, loud, very difficult to interrupt. They will spontaneously approach and talk to strangers, which makes assessment hard.","What Bipolar I Looks Like Over Time","At least one manic episode, usually alternating with major depressive and/or hypomanic episodes.","Classically there are psychotic features: delusions and hallucinations. They may be absent over a lifetime, but that would be very unusual. Psychotic features place the diagnosis in bipolar I: a hypomanic episode with psychosis is by defini…","The more manic episodes occur, the more intense they become, and the highs mirror the lows in depth and intensity, which is why medication adherence matters so much.","Behaviors of Mania","Mood lability: extreme mood swings, with irritability or sudden outbursts of misplaced rage.","Demanding and manipulative. Set limits in a firm but neutral manner.","Low frustration tolerance. Feels misunderstood. A great deal of pacing.","Dramatic mannerisms, jokes and puns, flamboyant or sexually suggestive dress.","May not slow down enough to eat or drink. Watch nutrition and hydration."]},{"s":"Hypomania, Bipolar II & Cyclothymia","a":"hypomania","k":["Hypomania, Bipolar II & Cyclothymia","Manic Episode","At least 1 week of elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased activity or energy.","Marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, or hospitalization required.","Psychotic features may be present.","Hypomanic Episode","At least 4 consecutive days, present most of the day, nearly every day.","An unequivocal change in functioning that is uncharacteristic of the person and observable by others.","Not severe enough to cause marked impairment or to require hospitalization.","No psychotic features. If there are any, the episode is by definition manic.","Bipolar II","Presence or history of at least one major depressive episode and at least one hypomanic episode, and never a manic episode.","Impairment in functioning in at least one area. No psychosis with hypomania, but psychosis may occur during the depressive phase.","Patients present during the depressive phase. They tend to like the hypomanic phase because they get more done, so they do not complain about it. Getting a history of ups and downs, plus collateral from family and friends, is what separates…","Impulsivity is a strong symptom in bipolar disorders and leads to suicide attempts.","Cyclothymic Disorder","Present for at least 2 years in adults, 1 year in children and adolescents.","Multiple hypomanic symptoms but not a hypomanic episode. Depressive symptoms but no major depressive, manic, or mixed episode.","Symptoms present more than half the time, and never absent for more than 2 months at a time.","Not as deep or as high as bipolar I or II, but more persistent. Rapid cycling is possible.","50% risk of subsequently developing bipolar I or bipolar II."]},{"s":"Assessment & Nursing Care","a":"nursing-care","k":["Assessment & Nursing Care","Mood — Mania: expansive, elevated, euphoric, or unrestrained. Hypomania. Depression. Lability, where the mood switches back and forth.","Behavior — Agitated, manipulative, restless, pacing.","Thought processes — Loose associations: thoughts only loosely connected. Flight of ideas: continuous accelerated speech with abrupt changes from topic to topic. Circumstantial: talks off subject but eventually returns to the point. Tangenti…","Thought content — Grandiose, paranoid, and persecutory delusions.","Speech patterns — Pressured, circumstantial, and clang associations: rhyming.","Cognitive functioning — Deficits possible, as in schizophrenia and severe mood disorders.","Assessment Guidelines","Danger to self or others, and the need for protection from uninhibited behaviors. Poor impulse control leads to giving away belongings or money and to gambling debts. Social work or guardianship may be needed to avoid bankruptcy.","Need for hospitalization: are they unstable?","Medical status and coexisting conditions. In mania they do not stop to eat, drink, or sleep, so a thorough medical examination is required.","The patient's and family's understanding of the disorder, the signs of relapse, and available support groups and resources.","Nursing Care for Acute Mania","The primary outcome in acute mania is injury prevention. Through the first few days the focus is the patient’s degree of exhaustion and nutrition. They have been going for days on little sleep, food, or fluid, and that outranks demanding or…","Manage medications — Give them on time, and use PRN medication when agitation is starting.","Decrease physical activity — No gym, treadmill, or exercise bike. Walking with a staff member is the right activity: it allows supervision and redirection with minimal energy expenditure. Puzzles are too frustrating, and games like bingo or…","Increase food and fluid intake — Finger foods and high-protein foods they can eat on the go, in larger amounts and more frequently, with fluids always on hand. They will not slow down to sit and eat.","Ensure sleep — At least 4 to 6 hours per night. Patients in mania may go days without sleeping.","Intervene for self-care and set limits — Prompt and supply what is needed for showering and grooming. Foot care: constant pacing, often without shoes, produces blisters and calluses. Seclusion and restraint are sometimes required to set lim…","Seclusion and restraint — the criteria, monitoring, and debriefing — are taught in full in Anger, Aggression & Violence","Patient & Family Teaching","Bipolar disorder is chronic and episodic and there is no cure, but recovery is possible. Treatment requires one or more mood-stabilizing agents taken for a long time.","Teach the expected side effects and toxic effects of the medication, and whom to call and where to go for an adverse reaction. Emergency phone numbers are kept somewhere easily accessed.","Teach the signs and symptoms of relapse, which may come \"out of the blue,\" and the family's role in preventing a full relapse.","Alcohol, drugs of abuse, caffeine (particularly energy drinks), and OTC medications can cause a relapse.","Good sleep hygiene is critical to stability. Lack of sleep is frequently the earliest symptom of a manic episode, and mania can sometimes be averted by using sleep medication early.","Coping strategies lower stress, enhance a sense of personal control, and increase community functioning.","Group and individual therapy build insight and relapse-prevention skills, provide social support, improve medication adherence, and reduce hospitalizations."]},{"s":"Medications for Mania & Mood Stabilization","a":"medications","k":["Medications for Mania & Mood Stabilization","The mood stabilizers and antipsychotics on the Exam Focus slides are also gathered by class, with the other Week 2 drugs, in Medications","Treating an acute manic episode is not the same as maintenance treatment for bipolar disorder. An acute episode gets something heavier added on top of the maintenance regimen, usually an antipsychotic, and that addition comes back off once …","Do not give an antidepressant alone to treat the depressive symptoms of bipolar disorder. It can throw the patient into mania. Mood stabilizers are the first medications used.","Acute, severe mania — A combination: lithium plus an antipsychotic, or divalproex/valproate plus an antipsychotic: for example lithium with risperidone, or divalproex with olanzapine. Once the mania is controlled, the lithium continues and …","Less severe mania — Bordering on hypomania, lithium or divalproex alone may be enough.","Severe agitation — Benzodiazepines: diazepam, lorazepam, clonazepam. They calm agitation and reduce aggression, panic, and insomnia, but they are sedative-hypnotics and addictive, so they are used only when necessary and only short-term.","Predominantly depressed — Symbyax, a combination of the second-generation antipsychotic olanzapine and the SSRI fluoxetine. Used, for example, in bipolar II where full mania does not occur.","Second-generation antipsychotics — olanzapine · risperidone · aripiprazole (for use over age 10) · quetiapine · ziprasidone · lurasidone."]},{"s":"Lithium Carbonate","a":"lithium","k":["Lithium Carbonate","Indications — Acute mania and maintenance treatment. The most widely used mood stabilizer. Effective in about 40% of patients with bipolar disorder.","Therapeutic level — 0.6 to 1.4 mEq/L.","Toxic level — 1.5 mEq/L and above.","Dosing — 1200–3000 mg/day. Start low and titrate up.","Onset — 5 to 7 days, and may take as long as 2 weeks, faster than the antidepressants.","Action — Uncertain. Crosses cell membranes, alters sodium transport, and is not protein-bound.","Contraindications — Cardiovascular disease · renal disease · brain damage · thyroid disease · pregnant or breastfeeding mothers.","Monitoring — Labs 1–2 times per week at first, then creatinine, thyroid hormones, and CBC every 6 months. Kidney damage is a real risk when levels go unmonitored. Thyroid function may decrease, usually after 6–18 months. Watch for dry skin,…","Teaching Points","Do not restrict sodium. Maintain a normal, steady sodium intake. Losing fluid means losing sodium, which increases the potential for lithium toxicity. Keeping intake steady is also what makes the blood levels interpretable.","Do not pre-load sodium before habitual activity. A daily workout or an outdoor job is already the baseline the monitored levels reflect. Increase sodium ahead of an unaccustomed exertion: a sedentary patient going hiking, or running a marat…","Hydrate. A child may need to take bottled water to school. Hydrate more than usual before strenuous activity that causes sweating.","Contact the provider if illness causes fluid loss, such as vomiting from a stomach bug.","Intermittent blood tests are required. Keep the appointments.","Increased risk of suicidal ideation when lithium is discontinued. Assess for symptoms of depression.","Coarse hand tremor, slurred speech, ataxia, confusion, or GI upset in a patient on lithium: hold the next dose and request a lithium level. Do not treat these as an expected early reaction that will subside.","Under 1.5 mEq/Lexpected side effects — Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, thirst, polyuria, lethargy, sedation, and fine hand tremor. Renal toxicity may occur with long-term use. Goiter and hypothyroidism. — Symptoms often subside during treatment…","1.5–2.0 mEq/Learly toxicity — Gastrointestinal upset, coarse hand tremor, confusion, hyperirritability of muscles, electroencephalographic changes, sedation, incoordination. — Withhold the medication, measure the blood lithium level, and re…","2.0–2.5 mEq/Ladvanced toxicity — Ataxia, giddiness, serious EEG changes, blurred vision, clonic movements, large output of dilute urine, seizures, stupor, severe hypotension, coma. Death is usually secondary to pulmonary complications. — Ho…","Over 2.5 mEq/Lsevere toxicity — Convulsions, oliguria, and death can occur. — All of the above, plus hemodialysis in severe cases.","Why diet and fluid intake change how lithium behaves is taught with the psychopharmacology principles in Neurobiology & Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Anticonvulsant Mood Stabilizers","a":"anticonvulsants","k":["Anticonvulsant Mood Stabilizers","valproate / divalproex(sodium valproate, divalproex sodium, valproic acid) — Check serum levels. Broader spectrum of efficacy against bipolar symptoms and longer periods of mood stabilization than the alternatives. All three names appear in…","carbamazepine — Check serum levels. Risk of low white blood cell counts. Check a CBC at the start and every 6 months, and monitor hepatic and renal function. Used when there has been no response to lithium, or with secondary breakthrough ma…","lamotrigine — For rapid cycling and the depressed phase of bipolar illness. Used as add-on therapy in refractory mood disorders. Usually tried after lithium fails. Rash may be life-threatening.","gabapentin — Effective for acute mania, mood stabilization, and rapid cycling.","topiramate — Also used as an anticonvulsant mood stabilizer.","Any rash on lamotrigine: stop the medication immediately and call the provider. It usually appears first on the chest or trunk. It is an allergic reaction that can advance to Stevens-Johnson syndrome (flu-like symptoms, sore throat, fever, …"]},{"s":"ECT, Safety & Advanced Practice","a":"other-treatments","k":["ECT, Safety & Advanced Practice","Electroconvulsive Therapy","Used to subdue severe manic behavior.","Useful in treatment-resistant mania and in rapid cycling: four or more episodes of illness per year.","More effective than drug-based therapy for treatment-resistant bipolar depression, and indicated for depressive episodes that are severe, catatonic, or treatment-resistant.","Teamwork, Safety & Support","An interprofessional staff working together minimizes staff splitting.","A great deal of limit setting. Seclusion or restraint for hyperactivity and impulsivity.","Support groups through the Depression and Bipolar Support Alliance and NAMI.","Health teaching and health promotion.","Advanced Practice Interventions","Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT).","Interpersonal and social rhythm therapy.","Family-focused therapy.","ECT — the procedure, the pre- and post-care, and its side effects — is taught in full in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]}]},{"f":"week2-suicide.html","t":"Suicide","secs":[{"s":"Suicide","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Epidemiology","a":"epidemiology","k":["Epidemiology","Rank in the US — 10th leading cause of death overall, 2nd for ages 15–34, 4th for ages 35–54, 8th for ages 55–64.","US deaths — 48,824 Americans died by suicide in 2024.","Global deaths — More than 700,000 people worldwide each year, a rate of 8.96 per 100,000. Over 77% occur in low- and middle-income countries.","Attempts — For every 1 person who dies by suicide, 20 more have attempted.","Sex — Over twice as high among men as women.","Military — Rates among active-duty service members have surpassed civilian rates, and veteran rates are increasing more rapidly than the general population.","In hospitals — Ranks among the top five most frequently reported sentinel events.","Why This Matters on Every Unit","Every area of a health care facility admits patients with suicidal thoughts, not just mental health units, and you may not know it on admission.","All RNs care for patients after suicide attempts. Depending on method and severity, many are in ICUs or on medical units.","Warning signs are easy to miss when the focus is physical health, so every patient admitted or seen in the ED is screened."]},{"s":"Key Terms & Concepts","a":"terms","k":["Key Terms & Concepts","Suicidal ideation (SI) — Thinking about, considering, or planning to kill oneself.","Suicidal plan — The organized plan and time frame for killing oneself.","Suicide attempt — A non-fatal self-directed potentially injurious behavior with any intent to die.","Suicide / death by suicide / fatal self-directed violence — The voluntary and intentional act of taking one's own life. All of these describe the same thing.","Suicide intent — At the time of the injury or death, the person had thoughts of killing themselves or wished to die.","Suicide survivor — The family and friends grieving a loved one's suicide. Not the person who attempted.","Suicidal self-directed violence — Behavior that is self-directed and deliberately results in injury or the potential for injury to oneself, with evidence of suicidal intent.","Non-suicidal self-directed violence — The same behavior, but with no evidence of suicidal intent.","Interrupted self-directed violence, by other — The person takes steps to injure themselves but is stopped by another person before the fatal injury, at any point during the behavior or the thought of suicide.","Interrupted self-directed violence, by self — The person takes steps to injure themselves but stops themselves before the fatal injury.","Suicide contagion / cluster — An increase in suicides when stories about suicide increase, are reported at length, are placed on the front page or at the beginning of a broadcast, concern a broadly known person, or carry a particularly dram…","Suicidal Behavior Disorder — A DSM-5 condition for further study. Suicide itself is not classified as a mental disorder. Criteria: an attempt within the last 24 months, not initiated in delirium or confusion, not undertaken solely for a rel…","Nonsuicidal self-injury — the same chapter, and the behavior these terms are defined against — has its own page in Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","Unacceptable Terms","Completed suicide — Implies an achievement, as though it were a good thing, and reinforces stigma. Substitute death by suicide or died by suicide.","Failed attempt — The person already feels bad enough, and it invites thoughts like \"I can't even kill myself. I must really be a loser.\"","Nonfatal suicide — No longer used.","Parasuicide / suicide gesture — An apparent attempt where the intent was to bring attention to a personal problem rather than to die: a sublethal drug overdose, for example.","Suicidality — Too broad. It covers ideation, plans, and attempts all at once."]},{"s":"Risk Factors & Etiology","a":"risk-factors","k":["Risk Factors & Etiology","No single factor causes suicide. It is usually a combination of factors plus a triggering event that tips the balance. Risk is assessed across the biological, psychological, and sociocultural domains.","Previous attempts — About half of those who die by suicide had a history of a previous attempt.","Psychiatric disorders — Approximately 60% of suicides are by individuals who had a psychiatric illness at the time.","Alcohol or substance use — Account for 60%–90% of suicides, thought to be related to the destabilizing, disinhibiting effects of substances.","Male gender — Males complete about 70% of suicides, but women make more attempts. Males use more lethal means such as firearms or hanging, while females predominantly use poisoning. Rural men are at higher risk than urban men, and women's r…","Physical health — Medical illness raises the risk of chronic depression and so raises suicide risk, and early onset of an illness increases it further. About 75% of elderly patients who attempted suicide had seen their primary care provider…","Chronic pain — Any chronic, painful, or disabling illness (chronic headaches, arthritis) and anything that greatly reduces function.","Race — 85%–90% of suicides are in Caucasians, with a higher rate among American Indians and Alaska Natives ages 15–34.","Religion, marriage, profession — Each also affects risk.","Biological Factors","Familial tendency. Risk rises sharply with a family history of suicide, and concordance is higher among identical than fraternal twins.","Lowered SKA2 gene expression. There may also be a familial tendency toward impulsivity or aggressiveness.","Low serotonin levels. Found in the cerebrospinal fluid of those who attempt suicide, and post-mortem.","Psychological Factors","Freud: aggression turned inward.","Menninger's three parts to suicide hostility: the wish to kill (revenge), the wish to be killed (guilt), and the wish to die (hopelessness).","Aaron Beck: the central emotional factor is hopelessness. When people lose hope, screening and intervention have to intensify.","Diathesis-stress model: a combination of suicidal fantasies and a significant loss.","African Americans — Protective: religion, the role of the extended family, and a strong support system.","Hispanic Americans — Protective: Roman Catholic religion and the importance of the extended family. Risk for recent immigrants: not understanding the US health care system, fear of deportation, and language barriers.","Asian Americans — Adherence to religions emphasizing interdependence between the individual and society, so self-destruction is seen as disrespectful or selfish. Belief in reincarnation may make death an honorable solution for the elderly, …","Societal factors. People are becoming increasingly isolated, which sets the stage for loneliness and despair: texting rather than phoning, no longer carpooling, not meeting neighbors, and not making eye contact or greeting others. Many cult…"]},{"s":"Recognizing & Analyzing Cues","a":"assessment","k":["Recognizing & Analyzing Cues","An accurate, complete assessment is the first step to suicide prevention: recognize who is at risk, use evidence-based instruments, and know the warning signs. Two tools: the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS), used by UK Healt…","Recognizing Cues","Overt verbal cues: a direct statement of a plan and the means to carry it out.","Covert verbal cues: vague statements such as \"maybe I could just go to sleep and never wake up.\"","Nonverbal cues: a patient who was participating and smiling on the unit stops interacting with others.","Analyzing Cues","Lethality of the plan and how much preparation has gone into it. If they say they will shoot themselves, ask whether they have access to a gun and whether it is in the home.","Self-assessment: check your own reactions.","Statements — Threatening or talking about hurting or killing oneself: seek professional help immediately. Talking or writing about death or dying. Saying they see no reason for living or no sense of purpose.","Actions — Looking for the means: guns or pills. Giving away prized possessions, including pets. Increasing drug or alcohol use. Withdrawing from friends and family.","Feelings — Guilt, shame, anger, or seeking revenge. Feeling like a burden to others. Feeling anxious, agitated, trapped, or hopeless. Dramatic mood swings.","Continuing a Gentle Inquiry","\"Have you wished you were dead, or wished you could go to sleep and not wake up?\"","\"Have you had any thoughts of killing yourself?\" Ask directly. Asking about suicide does not increase the risk or plant the idea. It opens the door to help.","\"What would you do if you had the urge to kill yourself while you are here in the hospital?\"","\"Share with me what is contributing to your thoughts of suicide.\"","\"What would keep you from hurting yourself?\"","\"If you have had these thoughts in the past, what have you done to keep yourself safe?\"","Suicide Is Often in Disguise","Motor vehicle \"accidents,\" especially single-car crashes.","Suicide risk underlying other health concerns.","Somatic complaints in older patients.","Self-injurious behavior. \"Accidental\" poisonings.","Nurse Self-Assessment","Health professionals experience fear, grief, anger, puzzlement, and even condemnation of suicidal feelings or intent.","Acknowledge those feelings to team members to avoid countertransference.","Nurses are at a much higher risk of suicide than the general public, so resilience and self-care are part of the work."]},{"s":"Outcomes, Safety Planning & Protective Factors","a":"planning","k":["Outcomes, Safety Planning & Protective Factors","Prioritize hypotheses — Prioritize by immediacy. Multiple high risk factors plus current intent needs urgent intervention. Highest priority is risk for suicide. Safety is always number one. Also self-care deficit, sleep pattern disturbance,…","Generate solutions — Decreased or resolved suicidal ideation. Identifies effective coping strategies or social supports. Expresses hope for the future and a will to live. Remains free from injury before discharge.","Plan — Interprofessional collaboration based on risk and protective factors. Mitigate the risks and bring in more protective factors. Involve significant others whenever possible and educate them before discharge.","Do not use no-suicide contracts. They are not evidence-based and must not replace safety planning.","Stanley-Brown Safety Planning Intervention","A written six-step plan completed together with the patient, covering warning signs, internal coping strategies, social settings and people who provide distraction, who to ask for help, and where to go in a crisis.","It helps the patient identify what is worth living for.","In a crisis, thinking is not clear, so the plan, with names and phone numbers, is kept somewhere visible such as the refrigerator or a mirror.","Levels of Intervention","Prevention","Activities that provide support, information, and education to prevent suicide.","Treatment","Treatment of the actual suicidal crisis: the hospitalization itself.","Postvention","Intervention with the circle of survivors left behind, to reduce the traumatic after-effects.","Protective Factors","External","Children or pets to care for in the home, family responsibilities, pregnancy.","A social support system.","Financial incentives or deterrents.","Effective access to and care for mental, physical, and substance use disorders.","Restricted access to highly lethal means, such as guns.","Internal","Meaning in life or a reason to live: a job, loved ones, helping others.","Cultural or religious prohibition or belief, and spirituality.","Hope.","Skills in problem solving and conflict resolution, frustration tolerance, optimism.","Connectedness to others, successful past coping strategies, effective mental health care.","Internal protective factors are used to identify the patient's strengths, build individualized coping strategies, and plan for what comes up after discharge. Document them on the clinical assessment form."]},{"s":"Environmental Safety & Levels of Observation","a":"safety","k":["Environmental Safety & Levels of Observation","Safety is the number one priority. Suicidal patients admitted to general hospitals attempt suicide more rapidly after admission and during shift changes, give fewer threats or warnings, and use more violent means.","Removing the Means","Remove anything usable for hanging. Place the patient in a hospital gown and limit clothing, bedding, and sheets in the room. No shoelaces.","Remove sharps such as razors, plus perfume bottles, makeup mirrors, and anything glass.","Remove medications from home. Lock the windows. Keep the bed locked in the low position.","Count disposable trays and plastic utensils, and dispose of them outside the patient's room at the nurses' station.","Paper trash-can liners, not plastic bags. No soda cans, glass vases, or pens. Pencils only if the metal eraser band is removed, and some units allow crayons only.","Environmental Safety Guidelines","No private room. Always keep the door open. People generally will not attempt suicide if someone else is in the room.","Jump-proof and hang-proof bathrooms: Velcro or paper shower curtains.","Lock doors to non-patient areas. When a fire alarm unlocks the doors, post staff to monitor them.","Assess belongings and search the patient: a full skin assessment on entry to the unit.","Monitor for and remove harmful gifts. Check all packages brought in by visitors. Ensure visitors and non-clinical staff do not bring or leave harmful objects.","Available levels — Constant (1:1), within arm's reach or line of sight, then every 15 minutes, every 30 minutes, or hourly.","Locked inpatient psych unit — Level is assigned by acuity, and is usually higher at the beginning of an admission or when the patient verbalizes strong suicidal ideation.","Acute care unit — Usually 1:1 constant observation by trained staff. It is very hard to monitor for suicide around tubing and equipment. Family members are not allowed to provide 1:1.","Changing the level of observation: it can be decreased only by an MD or NP order. It can be increased by the RN, who then notifies and discusses it with the physician. Patients on precautions are watched even while showering and using the t…","Other Actions","Inform all unit staff of the patient's risk, and note suicide and elopement precautions.","Instruct the sitter or patient safety companion at the start of the shift, monitor the sitter for vigilance, teach them to observe the patient even in the bathroom, and schedule their break times.","RN hourly rounding with the patient. Place the patient close to the nursing station.","Monitor for cheeking of medications. Have the patient open their mouth and lift the tongue.","Identify access to firearms or other means of self-harm at home, and involve the family in removing them."]},{"s":"Interventions, Documentation & Postvention","a":"interventions","k":["Interventions, Documentation & Postvention","Psychosocial Interventions","Therapeutic use of self. Give unconditional positive regard.","Open, accepting, trust-building frequent brief interactions. Make eye contact and lean forward.","Ask the question. Be direct and talk matter-of-factly about suicide.","Be available, spend time, stay non-judgmental, and allow expression. Discuss the current crisis in the patient's life.","Identify protective factors and successful past coping strategies.","At discharge, give the suicide hotline number: 988, or 1-800-273-TALK.","Psychobiological Interventions","Low-dose lithium: about 25 mg PO daily for a few weeks. The mechanism is not established, but it reduces suicidal ideation quickly.","Nasal ketamine: newer research supports its effectiveness for suicidal ideation.","ECT: a fast treatment to lift the depression.","Why suicide risk rises as antidepressant energy returns is covered in Depressive Disorders","\"Pt agrees to a no-suicide contract\"\"Pt denies SI\"\"Pt admits he will not harm self\" — State the behaviors congruent with the denial of SI: cooperative, communicating needs to staff, attending group therapy, actively participating in groups,…","Case Management & Teaching","Involve social work; make follow-up appointments before discharge. Ideally the patient sees their outpatient provider within 48 hours.","Develop agreements and releases of information between providers to make the transition safe, and send patient health information to the referral providers.","Teach the family to take any cue of suicide seriously, to be good listeners, and not to leave the person alone.","Postvention for Survivors","Survivors feel shock, disbelief, numbness, grief, sadness, despair, confusion, guilt, and self-anger.","Allow discussion of these emotions and validate the feelings. Provide support systems and resources.","Warn that holidays, birthdays, and anniversaries can be very painful, and explain their own risk of depression.","Discourage blaming and scapegoating. Help them understand the grieving process.","Evaluation is ongoing throughout hospitalization, staying alert to changes in mood, thinking, and behavior. Positive signs: communicating thoughts and feelings more readily, a widening social network, and engaging in problem-solving. Advanc…"]}]},{"f":"week2-nssi.html","t":"Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","secs":[{"s":"Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"What NSSI Is","a":"definition","k":["What NSSI Is","Deliberate and direct attempts to inflict shallow yet painful injuries to the surface of the body without intending to die. It is most often done to alleviate psychic pain or numbness, not as a means to end life, and not to seek attention.","The Behavior","Most commonly cutting, burning, scraping or scratching the skin, biting, hitting, skin picking, and interfering with wound healing. A person often uses multiple methods.","Usually inflicted with a knife, needle, razor, or other sharp object. Sometimes a fingernail.","Minor behaviors such as picking off a scab or biting nails are not considered self-injury.","Common sites are the frontal thighs and the dorsal forearm: easily covered and easily reached. As it intensifies the person worries less about concealment, so injuries appear in less concealable places.","Diagnostic Features","A desire for relief from negative thoughts or feelings: anxiety, anger, distress.","Self-loathing and a belief that they are bad, defective, or deserving of punishment.","May injure to achieve a short but intense state of euphoria during or after the act.","Impaired interpersonal relationships may be the impetus.","To be considered significant, it typically lasts at least a year and happens repeatedly.","The majority do not seek help. They are usually discovered when they come to hospital for other reasons, or through school counselors, teachers, a primary care provider, family, or friends.","Non-suicidal self-directed violence and the rest of the self-directed violence terminology are defined in Suicide"]},{"s":"DSM-5 Criteria","a":"criteria","k":["DSM-5 Criteria","Recognized in 2013 as a distinct clinical phenomenon and included as a condition requiring further research. These are good guidelines, but the APA has not determined that NSSI is a psychiatric disorder.","NSSI must occur for at least 5 days in the past year. The days need not be consecutive: any 5 days within the 12-month period.","Engaged in for one or more of these reasons: to relieve negative thoughts or feelings, to resolve an interpersonal difficulty, or to induce a positive feeling state.","Preceded by negative thoughts or feelings, conflicts with others, preoccupation with the behavior that is difficult to resist, or recurrent thoughts about the behavior.","Not socially sanctioned behavior. Body piercing, tattooing, or part of a religious or cultural ritual do not count.","The behavior or its consequences cause clinically significant distress or interference in important areas of functioning.","Does not occur solely in the context of another mental disorder."]},{"s":"Epidemiology, Comorbidity & Risk Factors","a":"epidemiology","k":["Epidemiology, Comorbidity & Risk Factors","Prevalence — Lifetime prevalence is difficult to determine accurately because the behavior is concealed. About 6% of the US population has engaged in NSSI at some point, and at least 1 million people in the US each year engage in intentiona…","Sex and age — Female to male ratio 3 or 4:1. An estimated 13%–23% of adolescents engage in NSSI. Globally the problem peaks between ages 20–29, with 17%–35% reporting it, and declines beyond age 29. Fewer people, rather than the behavior di…","Comorbidity — Depression · anxiety · eating disorders · substance use disorders · borderline personality disorder. 70% of females with borderline personality and 30% with eating disorders are affected by NSSI, and eating disorders co-occur …","Biological risk — Inconclusive, but serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine may play a role in mediating NSSI.","Cognitive risk — NSSI may be a form of self-punishment: painful actions used to make up for acts that caused harm or distress to others. Positive reinforcement, in the form of a reward, may support self-injurious acts.","Environmental and societal risk — Cultural differences are under investigation. NSSI may be a social phenomenon: individuals often learn about it from peers who are doing it.","Each year the CDC gathers hospital data on non-fatal self-harm injuries. Of the 500,000 people who visited a hospital for self-harm injuries, the figures suggest about 12 people harm themselves for every reported death by suicide, but the w…"]},{"s":"Recognizing & Responding","a":"recognition","k":["Recognizing & Responding","Signs & Symptoms","Scars from burns or cuts; fresh cuts, scratches, bruises, or other wounds. Broken bones.","Keeping sharp objects on hand.","Wearing long sleeves or long pants even in hot weather. Claiming frequent accidents or mishaps.","Spending a great deal of time alone. Pervasive difficulties in interpersonal relationships.","Persistent questions about personal identity: \"Who am I?\" \"What am I doing here?\"","Behavioral and emotional instability, impulsivity, and unpredictability. Statements of helplessness, hopelessness, or worthlessness.","Take Action","Establish a therapeutic relationship, which may fall to a family member, friend, counselor, teacher, or primary care provider, since these patients are rarely in the ED or on a psychiatric unit for the self-injury.","Care for the wounds or injuries.","Assess along four lines: what kind of self-injury, how often it happens, what triggers the urge, and what has helped in the past. Do not ask how the behavior makes other people feel. It shifts focus off the patient and reads as judgment.","Psychotherapeutic interventions: assess previous coping mechanisms and build healthy coping mechanisms to use instead of self-harm.","CBT and DBT: both successful for this condition, inpatient or outpatient.","Group therapy and psychopharmacology.","NSSI and suicidal ideation are not the same thing, and the nurse has to tell them apart. When a patient shows these signs, is impulsive and unpredictable, and voices hopelessness, ask the questions: Are you having thoughts of suicide? Do yo…","The Self-Harm Inventory is the scale used when self-harm is suspected: in a school, a pediatrician's or primary care office, the ED, or a medical unit. Mental health relies heavily on scales because there are no lab values for conditions li…","DBT and its four skill sets, and CBT, are taught in Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Misconceptions","All of These Are False","Only females self-injure · only teenagers self-injure · anyone who self-injures is part of the \"Goth\" or \"Emo\" subgroup.","Self-injury is a suicide attempt or a failed suicide attempt · people who self-injure only cut themselves.","Self-injury is just attention-seeking · people who self-injure are manipulative.","Anyone who self-injures is crazy and should be locked up · someone who self-injures is a danger to others.","All people who self-injure have borderline personality disorder · all people who self-injure have been abused.","People who self-injure enjoy the pain, or cannot feel it.","Self-injury is untreatable · someone who self-injures can stop if they really want to · there is nothing I can do to help.","Stigma is the biggest roadblock with mental illness, and every statement above is false."]}]},{"f":"week3-substance-use-disorders.html","t":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"What a Substance Use Disorder Is","a":"what-sud-is","k":["What a Substance Use Disorder Is","A complex brain disease marked by craving a substance, seeking it, and using it regardless of consequences: pathological use that leads to a disorder of use. The DSM-5 sets separate criteria for each substance, but all share the same shape.","The Four Symptom Groups","Impaired control: difficulty limiting or cutting down, larger amounts over a longer period than intended, repeated unsuccessful efforts to stop.","Social impairment: failure to meet major responsibilities at home, school or work. Withdrawal from social, occupational and recreational activities.","Risky use: continued use in physically dangerous situations, or despite knowing it causes or worsens physical or psychological problems.","Physical effects: intoxication, tolerance and withdrawal.","What It Is and Is Not","Is: chronic and treatable. Improves with medical treatment.","Is NOT: easy to manage · a personal weakness · a moral failing.","Addiction: a chronic medical condition with a strong craving, cycles of relapse and remission, and the risk of tolerance, withdrawal, disability or premature death. Many cycles may pass before abstinence holds.","Substances covered: alcohol, caffeine, cannabis, hallucinogens, inhalants, opioids, sedatives/hypnotics, anxiolytics, stimulants, tobacco."]},{"s":"Stigma, Language & Countertransference","a":"stigma-language","k":["Stigma, Language & Countertransference","Stigma reflects discriminatory attitudes and interferes with seeking treatment. It touches the individual, family and friends, treatment professionals, and the general public.","Person-first, person-centered language — Describe the illness as one part of a person's life rather than defining the person by the condition. \"A person with alcohol use disorder,\" not \"an alcoholic.\" \"A person with a substance use disorder…","Stigmatizing words to avoid — Alcoholic · abuser · addict · drunk. If a person uses such a term for themselves that is their right. Providers still do not use it.","Countertransference — When a healthcare worker unconsciously displaces feelings about significant figures from their own past onto the patient.","Preventing countertransference — Self-assessment: examine your own attitudes about substance use, and recognize that past experiences shape how you provide care to people with addictions.","Comorbidity — Two or more co-occurring chronic disorders that can interact and worsen each other. About 25% of people have both a mental and a physical disorder.","Dual diagnosis — Co-occurring mental illness and a substance or addictive disorder. Impairment may be mild, moderate or severe."]},{"s":"The Biology of Addiction","a":"biology","k":["The Biology of Addiction","Biological Risk","Genetic predisposition: addiction runs in families, with a higher concordance rate among siblings and children. Genes influence how drugs are metabolized, how intensely the brain responds to dopamine, and the risk of impulsivity.","Neurotransmitter disruption: dopamine is the most central: pleasure, reward, motivation. Serotonin: mood and emotional regulation. GABA and glutamate: drugs disrupt the calming/excitatory balance, causing sedation or anxiety. Endorphins: op…","Brain structures: nucleus accumbens: the main reward center, flooded with dopamine · prefrontal cortex · amygdala: links use to cues and triggers as emotional memory · hippocampus: reinforces cravings.","Immature brain development: the adolescent brain is not fully developed until about age 24: poor judgment and riskier behavior.","Environmental Risk","Chronic stressors and socioeconomic factors: poverty and unfavorable living environments.","Anxiety.","Substance use or addiction in family or peers. Parental factors and lack of supervision matter most in adolescence.","Exposure to physical, sexual or emotional abuse, or trauma. Any trauma exposure is a major risk factor.","Access to an addictive substance.","Ineffective coping strategies. Coping shifts onto the substance, so teaching positive coping is the counter.","The Reward Pathway","Dopamine Surge","The substance floods the brain with dopamine, up to 5 to 10 times the normal level, hijacking the brain's natural reward system.","Reinforcement","With dopamine elevated, the brain remembers and associates the substance with euphoric reward.","Craving & Repeat Use","Cravings develop and the person seeks the substance again to reproduce the feeling. Low dopamine between uses brings anhedonia.","The mesolimbic reward pathway","Different substances act differently: cocaine stops reuptake · amphetamines push more dopamine out · heroin makes dopamine neurons fire more · alcohol triggers more dopamine release.","The eight neurotransmitters and what each one does are taught in Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Alcohol Craving and Acetate","Alcohol breaks down into acetate, a metabolic byproduct, and acetate triggers a craving for more acetate.","In a normal drinker acetate is cleared quickly and exits the body. In alcohol use disorder it lingers, because the liver and pancreas process alcohol at only one-third to one-tenth the normal rate.","The first drink is barely processed out, so it triggers craving for more. The next drink adds to what is left, compounding the craving and driving roughly twice the intake of a normal drinker.","Buildup overwhelms self-control, control is lost, and the craving cycle has begun.","The craving effect never resets. The way acetate triggers cravings does not change over time, even with abstinence. After years without alcohol, one drink biologically reignites the accumulation cycle. This is why abstinence is critical."]},{"s":"Intoxication, Tolerance & Withdrawal","a":"key-terms","k":["Intoxication, Tolerance & Withdrawal","Substance intoxication — Recent or in-process overuse or excessive use of a substance: the height of the substance in the bloodstream. Symptoms are substance-specific and can occur with a single use. Produces impaired judgment, maladaptive …","Tolerance — The person no longer responds to a substance as they did initially, so they use increasing amounts over time to achieve the original level of response. Cocaine causes rapid physiological tolerance. Prescription opioids may take …","Substance withdrawal — Physiological symptoms that occur when a person stops using a substance. Specific to each substance, ranging from mild to life-threatening. The more intense the symptoms, the more likely the person is to resume use to…"]},{"s":"Opioid Use Disorder","a":"oud","k":["Opioid Use Disorder","Common opioids: oxycodone, morphine, codeine. The most common in street use now are heroin, fentanyl and oxycodone. Prescription overuse came first. When pain medications became expensive people turned to cheaper heroin, and fentanyl is now…","Intoxication & Overdose","Opioid Intoxication","Pinpoint pupils (miosis), a major sign.","Bradycardia · hypotension · hypothermia.","Sedation and calmness. Head nodding.","Hypokinesis: slowed movement. Slurred speech.","Altered mood: euphoria.","Recognizing an Overdose","Drowsy, difficult to wake, or unconscious.","Respirations slow, shallow or absent.","Nails and/or lips blue or purple. Skin pale, cool and clammy.","Choking, snoring or gurgling sounds.","Pinpoint pupils.","The three cardinal signs: coma, pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression.","Opioid overdose is an emergency. Opioids suppress the part of the brain that regulates breathing, and it can kill. Outside hospital, call 9-1-1 and give naloxone if available. In hospital, promote breathing first: aspirate secretions, inser…","Withdrawal & the COWS","Vital signs — Tachycardia · hypertension · hyperthermia · increased respiratory rate, the opposite of intoxication.","Eyes, nose, skin — Enlarged pupils · lacrimation (tearing) · rhinorrhea (runny nose) · diaphoresis · piloerection (gooseflesh).","Musculoskeletal — Muscle spasms · bone or joint aches · hyperreflexia · tremor.","GI — Abdominal cramps, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.","Neuro-behavioral — Insomnia · yawning · anxiety or irritability · restlessness.","COWS","Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale. Items scored: resting pulse rate, sweating, restlessness, pupil size, bone or joint aches, runny nose or tearing, GI upset, tremor, yawning, anxiety or irritability, gooseflesh skin.","Scores: 5–12 mild · 13–24 moderate · 25–36 moderately severe · >36 severe.","Technique: assess pupils in good lighting, comparing with another person's if unsure. Check gooseflesh by running fingertips over the inner forearm. Have the patient sit for five minutes first, since activity raises heart rate. A protocol t…","Medication-Assisted Treatment","Methadone — The first medication marketed for opioid withdrawal. Used less now. A synthetic narcotic that acts like heroin, morphine and fentanyl but far more slowly, flattening both the highs and the withdrawal lows. Significant overdose p…","Buprenorphine — Long-acting and slow, much like methadone. Reduces cravings and withdrawal symptoms so the patient can focus on recovery, without methadone's overdose potential.","Buprenorphine & naloxone — Used together to help people reduce or quit opioids. Produces a weaker euphoria than heroin or methadone, relieves cravings well, and reduces the mortality seen in opioid use disorder.","Naltrexone — Blocks the effects of opioids and alcohol, reducing cravings and preventing relapse, and blocking the high if the person relapses. Does not treat withdrawal symptoms.","Clonidine — An antihypertensive and alpha agonist that reduces withdrawal symptoms by blocking the neurotransmitters that trigger sympathetic nervous system activity.","Lofexidine — A newer alpha agonist, like clonidine, used to relieve opioid withdrawal symptoms, especially on abrupt discontinuation.","Naltrexone requires 10 to 14 days opioid-free before the first dose. Starting it sooner precipitates a rapid, severe withdrawal and makes the patient very sick.","Setting: opioid withdrawal is typically managed outpatient. Extremely uncomfortable, but not deadly the way alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal is. Psychological treatments: individual therapy, behavioral therapy, CBT, family therapy, soci…","Maintenance in Pregnancy","Pregnant women are detoxed in hospital, or placed on maintenance: start at a dose comparable to their prior use, then titrate upward as pregnancy progresses, with twice-daily dosing as an option instead of one larger dose. Titration follows…","Maintenance decreases dose variability and limits exposure to shared needles, unknown toxic additives and the intoxication-withdrawal swing, a more predictable outcome for mother and fetus.","The fetus experiences withdrawal symptoms 2–3 times as intense as the mother's, and a fetus can die from withdrawal. Maintenance reduces infant mortality and newborn withdrawal.","Methadone was the standard. Buprenorphine/naloxone is now preferred."]},{"s":"Alcohol Use Disorder","a":"aud","k":["Alcohol Use Disorder","Blood alcohol concentration — Alcohol intoxication is a blood concentration of 80 to 100 mg ethanol per deciliter (mg/dL), also expressed as 0.08 to 0.10 g/dL. No alcohol reads as 0.00. In Kentucky the DUI threshold is 0.08.","Effects at BAC 0.08% — Poor muscle coordination · altered speech and hearing · difficulty detecting danger · impaired judgment · poor self-control · difficulty reasoning. Impaired judgment is why so many drive while intoxicated. They believ…","DSM-5 diagnosis — At least 2 of 11 criteria within a 12-month period.","Severity — Based on the number of symptoms: mild 2–3 · moderate 4–5 · severe 6 or more.","Alcoholic Hallucinosis","The hallucinations of alcohol withdrawal are usually tactile or visual rather than the auditory ones seen in schizophrenia: commonly the sensation of bugs crawling on the skin, or seeing people or things that are not there.","Withdrawal After the Last Drink","6–8 hours — Minor symptoms: agitation, impaired cognition, lack of appetite, nausea, vomiting, insomnia, tremulousness, anxiety, mild perceptual changes, and rising blood pressure, pulse and temperature.","8–10 hours — Psychotic and perceptual symptoms begin. Alcoholic hallucinosis: visual, auditory or tactile hallucinations.","12–24 hours — Withdrawal seizures: generalized tonic-clonic seizures, if withdrawal is untreated.","Anytime within first 72 hours — Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens): autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, diaphoresis, fever, anxiety, insomnia, hypertension), delusions, and visual and tactile hallucinations.","CIWA & Managing Withdrawal","What CIWA is — The Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for alcohol, the tool for sedative-hypnotic and alcohol withdrawal.","Items scored — Nausea/vomiting · tremors · anxiety · agitation · paroxysmal sweats · headache · tactile disturbance · auditory disturbance · visual disturbance · orientation.","Scores — 0–9 minimal withdrawal · 10–19 mild-to-moderate · >20 severe. Most items score 0–7. Orientation scores 0–4.","Frequency — Usually ordered every 4 hours, repeated PRN, sometimes only 30 minutes later.","Goals — Control agitation, decrease the risk of seizures, and reduce morbidity and mortality.","Medication threshold — For a CIWA-Ar score of 8 to 10 or more: chlordiazepoxide, diazepam or lorazepam. Example fixed regimen: diazepam 10 mg every 6 hours for four doses, then 5 mg every 6 hours for eight doses, with PRN dosing for breakth…","Practice Points","Assess tremor by having the patient extend the tongue and hold hands out with fingers spread. Resting a fingertip on theirs makes involuntary movement easy to feel.","Diazepam is the current standard at UK HealthCare, replacing chlordiazepoxide, because its long half-life works better and it is particularly effective at decreasing seizures.","With a history of seizures, gabapentin is typically started as well. Prior seizures raise the risk of withdrawal seizures.","Alcohol withdrawal is usually over in 3 to 5 days and peaks around 48 hours."]},{"s":"Delirium Tremens","a":"dts","k":["Delirium Tremens","DTs are the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal and a medical emergency that can result in death. Hepatitis or pancreatitis increases the likelihood, through impaired toxin filtering. Rarely seen in people in good physical health, which …","Symptoms","Agitation · increased anxiety.","Gross confusion and disorientation.","Coarse tremors · seizures.","Delusions · hallucinations (visual, tactile) · paranoia.","Autonomic hyperactivity: tachycardia, diaphoresis, fever, anxiety, insomnia, hypertension.","Prevention Is the Goal","Assess and monitor closely: symptoms, labs, electrolytes, vital signs. If the patient reports worsening withdrawal, rescore immediately.","Hydrate and medicate adequately. Follow the protocol.","Provide life support and life-sustaining measures as indicated: suctioning, padded bed rails.","Seizures — IV diazepam.","Acute agitation, tremors, impending or acute DTs, hallucinosis — Oral chlordiazepoxide or diazepam.","Delirium appears — IV lorazepam.","Elevated blood pressure — Clonidine.","Dehydration — Oral or IV fluids."]},{"s":"Medication-Assisted Treatment for Alcohol Use Disorder","a":"aud-treatment","k":["Medication-Assisted Treatment for Alcohol Use Disorder","A combination of medication with individual and behavioral therapies is what sustains recovery. Treat withdrawal symptoms symptomatically (nausea/vomiting, anxiety, headache) and replace thiamine daily. The standard is a multivitamin, thiam…","Naltrexone — Blocks the euphoric effects of opioids and alcohol, so relapse produces no high, and reduces cravings for alcohol. Long-acting, given IM. Does not treat withdrawal symptoms.","Disulfiram — Given to stop drinking by causing severely unpleasant flu-like illness if the patient drinks while taking it.","Acamprosate — Reduces cravings, helping maintain abstinence. Does not treat withdrawal symptoms.","Disulfiram teaching: alcohol is not only in drinks. Patients must avoid perfumes, aftershaves, mouthwash, many liquid medications (especially nighttime formulas) and paint thinners. Even splashing on aftershave can make them severely ill.","All three of these are recovery-phase medications. None treats withdrawal.","Aversion therapy, the behavioral therapy disulfiram belongs to, is taught in Assessment, Theories & Therapies"]},{"s":"Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","a":"wernicke-korsakoff","k":["Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","A neurological disorder caused by lack of thiamine (B1) that requires immediate treatment, consisting of two stages. Chronic alcohol use decreases intestinal absorption of thiamine by about 70%. Other causes of thiamine deficiency must be r…","Wernicke's Encephalopathy","Acute and reversible. May clear within a few weeks, or progress to Korsakoff syndrome.","Treatment: thiamine replacement in large doses, IV, 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks, typically about 10 days.","Symptoms: confusion · ataxia: loss of muscle coordination, posture and balance problems, tremors · double vision, droopy eyelid, nystagmus (abnormal eye movements).","Korsakoff Syndrome","Chronic and debilitating, and not typically reversible.","Treatment: thiamine replacement for 3–12 months, varying with symptoms, severity and whether psychosis is present.","Outcome: most never fully recover, with a recovery rate of only about 20%, though cognition may improve with thiamine and nutritional support. Many require residential care.","Symptoms: everything seen in Wernicke's, plus severe, irreversible, persistent memory impairment, including anterograde amnesia, difficulty forming new memories · confabulation, filling memory gaps with invented detail rather than lying · h…","The eleven Exam Focus drugs, gathered by class with their full rubric, are on Medications"]},{"s":"Applying the Clinical Judgment Model","a":"cjm","k":["Applying the Clinical Judgment Model","Recognize Cues: Screening Tools","Be open, kind and non-judgmental. Most patients accept screening, are open to advice on quitting or cutting down, and show more readiness to change than expected. Read the instructions before screening a patient so the protocol is followed …","AUDIT — Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test, a 10-item tool developed by the WHO covering alcohol consumption, drinking behaviors and alcohol-related problems.","CRAFFT — Six key questions for ages 12–21: Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Family or friends, Trouble. A score of 2 or more indicates higher likelihood of a substance use disorder and needs further evaluation. 4 or more raises suspicion of depen…","CAGE — Cut down · Annoyed by criticism · Guilty about drinking · Eye-opener, a drink first thing in the morning. A score of 2 or more is significant. A score of 1 requires further assessment.","CAGE-AID — The same questions Adapted to Include Drugs, not alcohol alone.","SBIRT","Screening — Assesses the severity of substance use and identifies the appropriate level of treatment.","Brief Intervention — Focuses on increasing awareness about substance use and motivation toward behavioral change.","Referral to Treatment — Gives those needing more extensive treatment access to specialty care.","Why It Exists","Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment: a comprehensive, integrated approach to early intervention for people with or at risk for substance use and addictive disorders.","Completed by any healthcare professional in any healthcare setting, screening for alcohol, illicit drugs, and prescription drug misuse.","Goal: reduce and prevent related health consequences, disease, accidents and injuries, and reduce costs and healthcare utilization.","From Cues to Action","Analyze cues — Comprehensive, holistic assessment of background · pattern of substance use · mental health symptoms · trauma history · family history of substance use or mental health problems · strengths and level of willingness to change.","Prioritize hypotheses — Risk for self-harm or suicide · risk for overdose · withdrawal · impaired sleep · impaired coping · social isolation · denial · hopelessness · dysfunctional family process.","Generate solutions — Realistic short- and long-term goals, patient-centered and based on the assessment, weighing the patient's ability to recognize the problem and readiness to change. Example: for withdrawal risk, control symptoms and ret…","Take action — Monitor vital signs and for withdrawal · administer medications as prescribed · develop the therapeutic relationship · gradually reintroduce healthy food and push hydration · support and encourage self-care · promote safety an…","Evaluate outcomes — Reassess whether interventions achieved the intended goals. Outcomes are determined by the patient and mutually supported by the patient and interdisciplinary staff, so feedback from patient, family and support system is…","Discharge, Relapse Prevention & the Care Continuum","Discharge Planning","Promote participation in an outpatient support system. Many outpatient programs will not accept a patient until they have spoken with them personally, so encourage and prompt the patient to make those calls before discharge.","Help identify alternative sources of coping. Support health promotion and maintenance.","Relapse prevention: teach the warning signs and develop a relapse prevention plan. Ask \"How will you handle stress so you don't go back to using?\"","Involving relatives in treatment decreases relapse rates by 20 to 50 percent.","Care Continuum","Detoxification: medically managed.","Rehabilitation.","Halfway houses: residential, substance-free communal living that allows independent growth.","Partial hospitalization programs.","Intensive outpatient programs (IOPs): non-residential, highly structured scheduled treatment groups with at least one regular individual session.","Outpatient treatment · medication management · Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) and Narcotics Anonymous · relapse prevention programs. Smartphone apps can track behavior patterns and relapse cues.","Patient & Family Education","Nature of the illness: effects of the substance on the body, and the ways use affects life.","Management of the illness: activities to substitute for the substance in times of stress, and increasing coping skills.","Problem-solving skills.","Essentials of good nutrition.","Relaxation techniques: progressive relaxation, tense-and-relax, deep breathing, mindful meditation, imagery, yoga.","Recovery is possible. Instill hope. Encourage self-responsibility, setting life goals, improving coping skills, and adherence to the treatment plan. Participation in group, individual and family therapy, peer support through NA/AA 12-step p…"]}]},{"f":"week3-personality-disorders.html","t":"Personality Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Personality Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Personality & Personality Disorder","a":"definitions","k":["Personality & Personality Disorder","Personality — The characteristics and behavior that comprise a person's unique adjustment to life: major traits, interests, drives, values, self-concept, abilities and emotional patterns. Personality helps determine behavior.","Personality traits — Characteristics a person is born with or develops early in life that influence how they perceive and relate to their environment. A complex pattern largely outside the person's awareness: distinctive patterns of perceiv…","Personality disorder — An enduring pattern of deviant inner experience and behavior that differs from cultural expectations, is pervasive, inflexible and stable, and leads to distress or impairment. Involves dysfunctions in cognition, impul…","Among the most challenging disorders to treat, often because the individual does not recognize that they have a problem, or that their life difficulties are of their own making. Traits become a disorder when they deviate markedly from cultu…"]},{"s":"Epidemiology, Etiology & Common Symptoms","a":"etiology","k":["Epidemiology, Etiology & Common Symptoms","Prevalence — About 8% of the global population, about 1 in 10 American adults. Emotional dysregulation is amplified.","Genetic — Personality traits are inherited. The disorders themselves are not.","Neurobiological — Neurotransmitters regulate and influence temperament.","Psychosocial & environmental — Children learn maladaptive responses and copy behaviors from others. Parenting matters: childhood neglect or rejection, and divorce.","Trauma — A major risk factor for personality disorders.","Diathesis-stress model — A genetic or biological predisposition plus a stressor that brings out the actual disorder.","The Four Common Symptoms","Present in almost all personality disorders:","Impairment in interpersonal relationships","Dysfunction in cognition","Dysfunction in affect","Dysfunction in impulse control"]},{"s":"Diagnostic Criteria","a":"criteria","k":["Diagnostic Criteria","A specific and notable pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving with all of the following characteristics.","Persistent: enduring over time.","Pervasive: across a broad range of personal and social situations.","Involves both inner experience and external behavior: how the person perceives, experiences and interprets the world, and the methods they use to achieve outcomes.","Differs significantly from the expectations of the individual's culture.","Involves two or more areas of functioning: thinking: ways of perceiving or interpreting self, others and events · feeling: emotional range, intensity, stability, appropriateness · interpersonal functioning: the style and nature of relations…","Leads to clinically significant distress or impairment in social, legal, occupational, financial, safety and other important areas.","Not better accounted for by another mental health condition, by the direct physiological effects of a substance, or by a medical condition."]},{"s":"Treatment & Prognosis","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment & Prognosis","Treatment","Restructuring personality takes years of psychotherapy, a great deal of time and money, so short-term treatment focused on immediate problem-solving, social skills and coping skills is increasingly used.","Psychopharmacology · individual therapy · dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) · family education and therapy · social skills training groups · group therapy.","Medications are rarely indicated in long-term treatment, but may be used for a short period to keep patients safe and manage anxiety or psychotic symptoms.","Prognosis","Once considered grim. The current view is that personality disorders are treatable: people continue to grow and change throughout life.","Prognosis depends on the degree of impairment and the person's motivation for help. The patient must be motivated for change.","The obstacle: many do not believe a problem exists, do not see themselves as its cause, or are unaware their behavior is unusual.","DBT and its four skill sets are taught in Assessment, Theories & Therapies"]},{"s":"The Three Clusters","a":"clusters","k":["The Three Clusters","The DSM-5 groups 10 personality disorders into three clusters, each sharing common traits. Symptoms overlap across them.","Cluster A — Odd or eccentric — Paranoid · Schizoid · Schizotypal","Cluster B — Dramatic, emotional, antagonistic and erratic — Borderline · Antisocial · Histrionic · Narcissistic","Cluster C — Anxious or fearful: worried, anxious and fearful are the key components — Dependent · Obsessive-compulsive · Avoidant","The three clusters, as a night out. Cluster A, \"PaSS on the party\": Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal. Cluster B, \"BAHNed from the party\": Borderline, Antisocial, Histrionic, Narcissistic. Cluster C, \"the party is DOA\" (dead on arrival): Depe…"]},{"s":"Cluster A: Odd or Eccentric","a":"cluster-a","k":["Cluster A: Odd or Eccentric","These disorders sit on the adaptive end of the schizophrenia spectrum. Negative symptoms appear, as in schizophrenia, but these individuals do not lose contact with reality, their symptoms are less severe, and they can be made aware of thei…","Paranoid Personality Disorder","Core — Longstanding, intense distrust and suspicion. Suspects others are exploiting, harming or deceiving them.","Relationships — Preoccupied with doubts about loyalty and trustworthiness. Suspicious about the fidelity of spouses or partners. Jealous and controlling as adults. Reluctant to confide in others.","Interpretation — Reads hidden or threatening meaning into harmless, benign remarks. Perceives attacks on their character or reputation that are not there.","Grudges — Severe unwillingness to forgive: bears grudges, unforgiving of any slight, insult or injury, and vindictive.","Behavior — Hypervigilant, quick to anger, provokes others or counterattacks. Beneath the guarded surface they are anxious and worried about being harmed.","Distinguishing it — Look at the history: paranoid beliefs must have been present by early adulthood and persist in the absence of substance use or psychotic episodes.","Nursing Care","Counteract mistrust. They reject treatment out of suspicion, and can be very difficult to interview.","Adhere strictly to promises, appointments and schedules. If you say two minutes, come back in two minutes.","Avoid being overly friendly, which deepens suspicion, but acknowledge feelings.","Set firm boundaries, especially with threatening behavior.","Use simple, direct language and project a neutral but kind affect.","Group therapy can feel threatening, but role-play and group feedback on social skills can reduce suspiciousness.","Schizoid Personality Disorder","Characteristics","Loners who do not desire the presence of others and are detached from social relationships. Schizoid: avoid.","Cold, detached, distant. Avoid close relationships.","Poor academic performance. Increased prevalence of disordered family life. Rarely marry.","Solitary jobs or hobbies with no interaction.","Depersonalization and detachment: flat and removed from everything around them.","Avoid being too \"nice\" or \"friendly.\"","Do not try to increase socialization.","Assess for symptoms the patient is reluctant to discuss.","Protect against the group's ridicule. Others on the unit may make fun of them.","Respect personal space while gently encouraging participation.","Treatment: psychotherapy or group therapy. Sometimes antidepressants or second-generation antipsychotics.","Schizotypal Personality Disorder","Core — Unusual thoughts, behaviors and perceptions: less severe than schizophrenia. The difference from schizophrenia: they can be made aware that their magical thinking is not real.","Social deficits — Severe social and interpersonal deficits: acute discomfort and reduced capacity for close relationships. May have a few close friends. Anxiety in social situations, intensified because they do not blend in.","Thinking — Odd beliefs and magical thinking that influence behavior: superstitions, believing they are clairvoyant or telepathic, preoccupation with paranormal phenomena. May be vulnerable to cults and religious groups for this reason.","Ideas of reference — Incorrect interpretations of events as having special personal meaning for them.","Presentation — Cognitive and perceptual distortions · eccentric behavior and dress · constricted or inappropriate affect · rambling conversation · paranoia, suspiciousness, distrust · brief episodes of hallucination or delusion.","Respect the need for social isolation.","Be aware of and intervene appropriately with suspiciousness.","Reinforce socially appropriate behavior and dress.","Perform a careful, in-depth diagnostic assessment for symptoms needing intervention, especially suicidal thoughts.","Be aware of strange religious practices or peculiar thoughts as part of their life, and withhold judgment or ridicule.","Treatment: low-dose antipsychotics and supportive psychological care."]},{"s":"Borderline Personality Disorder","a":"borderline","k":["Borderline Personality Disorder","The most well-known and most dramatic personality disorder. A pervasive pattern of instability in interpersonal relationships, self-image and affect. These individuals are always in a state of crisis, with mood swings that can change hourly…","Self-harm — Recurrent suicidal behavior, gestures or threats, and self-mutilating behavior. About 75% have a history of at least one deliberate act of self-harm.","Affective instability — Rapid, extreme shifts in mood, a core characteristic. Erratic emotional responses to situations, and intense sensitivity to criticism or perceived slights. They come across as moody, irresponsible and intense.","Identity disturbance — No sense of their own identity or direction, shown through feelings of emptiness, dysphoria or boredom. Often direct their actions to fit what others wish.","Anger — Inappropriate, intense anger or difficulty controlling anger.","Under stress — Transient stress-related paranoid ideation or severe dissociation.","Relationships — Frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment. A pattern of unstable, intense relationships and a history of insecure attachment. They idealize others and form intense relationships that violate boundaries, then sabo…","Impulsivity — In at least two areas that are self-damaging: spending, sex, substance abuse.","Responsibility — View themselves as victims and do not accept responsibility for their problems.","Splitting","A defense mechanism in which the person is unable to integrate and accept both positive and negative feelings. People, including themselves, and life situations are either all good or all bad, with no grey area.","Challenging for nurses: the same nurse may be called the best they have ever had, and then, after one perceived slight, the worst.","Patients with borderline personality disorder tend to get worse when admitted. Attention-seeking behavior, boundary-pushing and relationship dynamics intensify inpatient, producing more self-harm behavior and decompensation, even though mos…","Nursing Care","Comprehensive assessment including risk assessment and the emotional triggers that exacerbate symptoms.","Build trust through consistency and reliability. Be predictable and available, to counter the fear of abandonment and anxiety.","Empathy and validation through active listening.","Teach emotional regulation: DBT techniques such as mindfulness, grounding and deep breathing.","Encourage coping skills: journaling, distraction, creative outlets.","Set boundaries and help them recognize when they violate others'. Teach conflict resolution without self-destructive behavior, outbursts or manipulation.","Safety.","Safety Plans","Build the plan with the patient: the steps to take during an emotional crisis or when feeling overwhelmed.","Names and phone numbers of a few people to call right away when self-harm urges or overwhelm hit.","Plus the things that help: journaling, distraction, deep breathing.","Keep it somewhere visible, such as the refrigerator. In crisis mode the brain cannot recall who to reach out to, which is exactly why the plan is written down.","Nonsuicidal self-injury, which co-occurs heavily with this disorder, is taught in Nonsuicidal Self-Injury"]},{"s":"Cluster B: Antisocial, Histrionic & Narcissistic","a":"cluster-b-rest","k":["Cluster B: Antisocial, Histrionic & Narcissistic","Antisocial Personality Disorder","Core — A pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others. Also referred to as psychopath or sociopath. Heavily researched because of its impact on society through criminal activity.","Antagonistic behaviors — Deceitful and manipulative for personal gain, or hostile when their needs are blocked. Lying, false names, conning others, for pleasure or for profit.","Disinhibited behaviors — High risk-taking · disregard for responsibilities · impulsivity. Criminal misconduct and substance abuse are common.","Empathy — Profound lack of empathy, remorse or guilt. Indifferent to, or rationalizing, having hurt, mistreated or stolen from others.","Developmental history — Evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15. Many age out of the pediatric diagnosis into this one.","Self-view — Driven by a need to prove superiority. See themselves as the center of the universe. Irritability and aggressiveness leading to physical fights or assaults. Superficially charming but deceitful, lacking genuine warmth.","Nursing Care","Identify blocks to the therapeutic relationship: bargaining about rules, or breaking them.","Identify dysfunctional thinking patterns and develop new problem-solving behaviors.","Self-responsibility facilitation: hold the patient responsible for their behavior, give positive feedback when they accept added responsibility, monitor how much they assume, and discuss consequences of not meeting responsibilities.","Anger control assistance: meditation, anger management techniques, medications.","Help build a new support system. Theirs is typically poor.","Self-awareness enhancement: encourage recognition of their own thoughts and feelings.","Antisocial vs. Borderline","Antisocial: typically legal involvement, more outwardly destructive, a prior conduct disorder diagnosis, and more common in males.","Borderline: can have some legal involvement, but is more self-destructive, and more common in females.","Histrionic Personality Disorder","Characteristics","Excitable, dramatic, high-functioning: frequently in the spotlight, the life of the party.","Bold, attention-seeking, provocative. Frequently sexually seductive.","Self-centered with a low frustration level.","Excessive emotions. Can appear smothering.","No insight: limited ability to form meaningful relationships, and no recognition that the excessive emotion and attention-seeking is what interferes. Considers relationships more intimate than they are.","Behaviors are motivated by an intense desire to gain nurturance and support. They are intentional.","Distinguishing it from mania: the excessive emotion is constant. Moods do not cycle up and down as in bipolar disorder.","Know that seductive behavior is a response to inner distress.","Keep interactions professional and ignore flirtations. Avoid overreacting to the patient's actions.","Help the patient clarify their real inner feelings.","Model concrete language. Teach and role-model assertiveness.","Assess for suicidal ideation.","Set firm boundaries for interactions with staff and with peers.","Frequently co-occurs with anxiety, substance abuse or other personality disorders.","Narcissistic Personality Disorder","Feelings of entitlement, superiority and exaggerated self-importance. Exaggerate achievements and talents. Come across as arrogant.","Lack of empathy and a tendency to exploit others.","Constant need for admiration.","Underneath: fear of abandonment, weak self-esteem and hypersensitivity to criticism, a fragile sense of self-worth.","Will only interact with other \"special\" people, to demonstrate their superiority.","Less functional impairment than the other personality disorders.","Remain neutral and calm. Do not take criticism or dismissiveness to heart.","Promote a stronger patient self-identity.","Avoid power struggles or becoming defensive, and set clear boundaries.","Role-model empathy.","A complex condition needing a thoughtful, empathetic approach alongside psychiatrists, psychologists and social workers."]},{"s":"Cluster C: Anxious or Fearful","a":"cluster-c","k":["Cluster C: Anxious or Fearful","Dependent Personality Disorder","Core — A pattern of submissive and clinging behavior driven by an overwhelming need to be cared for.","Separation — Intense fears of separation and abandonment. Intense anxiety when left alone, even briefly. Will go to great lengths to stay in a relationship even an unhealthy or abusive one, and urgently seek a new one if it ends.","Decision-making — Difficulty initiating projects, doing things alone, or making everyday decisions. They distrust their own judgment and believe others have better ideas. Complete lack of self-confidence.","Manipulation — Manipulate others into taking on responsibilities they do not feel able to handle.","Nursing Care","Psychotherapy is the treatment of choice.","Help address current stressors and the fear of being alone.","Set limits gently, so the patient does not feel punished, for example when they manipulate others into doing things for them.","Be aware of strong countertransference.","Use the therapeutic relationship for assertiveness training.","Encourage independence and self-efficacy.","Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder","Characteristics","A pervasive pattern of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism and control at the expense of flexibility, openness and efficiency.","Excessively devoted to work and productivity to the exclusion of leisure and friendships: workaholics.","Rigidity and inflexible standards. Stubborn.","Excessive goal-seeking that is self-defeating or relationship-defeating.","Strict standards interfere with project completion, since nothing is ever perfect enough, and they are fearful of delegating in case the result is imperfect.","They seek help when overwhelmed by their inability to keep up with tasks, lists or organization.","The Key Distinction & Care","OCPD does not interfere greatly with daily functioning as obsessive-compulsive disorder does. They are not stuck in a compulsive loop and can go about their lives.","Guard against power struggles.","Remember the patient has difficulty with unexpected changes to routine and structure.","Provide structure, but allow enough time to complete habitual behaviors.","Promote a calm, supportive environment and encourage group participation. Group and behavioral therapy are used.","Medications may be needed for anxiety, depression or obsessions: clomipramine or fluoxetine.","Avoidant Personality Disorder","Low self-esteem: view themselves as inept, unattractive, inadequate or inferior, with a preoccupation with their perceived shortcomings.","Preoccupied with rejection, humiliation and failure. Reluctant to take risks or engage in new activities for fear of embarrassment.","Avoid activities involving interpersonal contact for fear of criticism, rejection or disapproval.","Unwilling to get involved with people unless certain of being liked. Very inhibited in social relationships.","Shyness that increases with age. Timid, lonely, overly sensitive: wallflowers.","Subject to depression, anxiety and anger.","Friendly, accepting, reassuring approach, but do not push too hard.","Show empathy and acceptance of their fears of rejection.","Exercises to enhance new social skills, designed so they succeed and do not feel like failures.","Assertiveness training works well.","Individual therapy for trust building, plus group therapy.","Antidepressants and anti-anxiety medications are used in this cluster."]}]},{"f":"week4-eating-feeding-disorders.html","t":"Eating & Feeding Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Eating & Feeding Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"What an Eating Disorder Is","a":"definitions","k":["What an Eating Disorder Is","Eating disorders — Disorders in which eating is consistently below or above the caloric need to maintain a healthy weight, occurs without hunger or fails to produce satiety, and results in physiologic imbalances or medical complications. Ca…","Satiety — Comfortable fullness. Eating that fails to produce it is one of the defining features above.","Binge eating — Eating, within a 2-hour period, an amount of food definitely larger than most people would eat in that time under similar circumstances, with a sense of lack of control over the eating.","Purging — Compensatory behavior to avoid weight gain: self-induced vomiting, or misuse of laxatives, diuretics or enemas.","Body mass index (BMI) — The gauge used to determine the severity of anorexia nervosa.","Eating disorders have the second highest mortality rate of any mental illness, surpassed only by opioid addiction. About 9% of the population will have one at some point in their lifetime, across every age, race, size, gender identity, sexu…","From Normal Eating to a Full Syndrome","Normal Eating","Risk factors accumulate and move a person off this point: low self-esteem, dieting, parental attitudes, body dissatisfaction and media ideal bodies.","Partial-Syndrome Eating Disorder","Binge eating and serious dieting appear, but not yet at full diagnostic severity.","Full-Syndrome Eating Disorder","An increase in the frequency and severity of binge eating, purging and starvation. Treatment enters here."]},{"s":"Etiology, Risk Factors & Comorbidity","a":"etiology","k":["Etiology, Risk Factors & Comorbidity","Neurobiological — Altered brain serotonin function contributes to the dysregulation of appetite, mood and impulse control.","Environmental — Childhood trauma and sexual abuse are frequently reported. Those with abuse histories have a poorer prognosis.","Cultural — Culture influences the development of self-concept and satisfaction with body size.","Developmental — Onset of puberty, major life changes or stressors, and dieting that becomes uncontrolled.","Personal — A vulnerable personality, perfectionism, impulsivity, being female, and a history of obesity.","Familial — Genetics, and family functioning style.","Sociocultural — Emphasis on slimness.","Comorbidity","Studies show an association between depression, anxiety and eating disorders.","Many with anorexia nervosa have a lifetime history of an anxiety disorder.","Binge and purge behavior carries comorbid alcohol or substance use problems.","Bulimia frequently coexists with major depression, substance abuse and personality disorders.","Depressive disorders are comorbid throughout this topic and are taught in full in Depressive Disorders","Why These Disorders Are Hard to Treat","Individuals with eating disorders rarely seek help.","They are typically not motivated to change.","They often leave treatment.","Some recover spontaneously, others have long-term problems."]},{"s":"Anorexia Nervosa","a":"anorexia","k":["Anorexia Nervosa","A life-threatening eating disorder characterized by an intense fear of weight gain, a severely distorted body image, and restriction of calories relative to requirements with a significantly low BMI.","The Two Types","Restricting Type","Does not regularly engage in binge-eating or purging behavior. Weight loss is accomplished through dieting, fasting and excessive exercise.","Binge-Eating and Purging Type","Regularly engages in binge-eating or purging behavior: self-induced vomiting or misuse of laxatives, diuretics or enemas.","The type is assigned on the last 3 months of behavior.","DSM-5 Criteria","Restriction of energy intake relative to requirements, leading to a significantly low body weight in the context of age, sex, developmental trajectory and physical health.","Intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behavior that interferes with weight gain, even though at a significantly low weight.","Disturbance in the way body weight or shape is experienced, undue influence of weight or shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the current low body weight.","Clinical Signs & Symptoms","Low body weight — 15% or more below what is expected for age, height and activity level.","BMI severity — Mild at a BMI of 17 or more · moderate at 16 to 17 · severe at 15 to 16 · extreme when less than 15.","Amenorrhea — Loss of menstrual periods in girls and women past puberty.","Lanugo — Downy growth of body hair on the face and back.","Skin — Mottled, cool skin on the extremities, and peripheral edema.","Vital signs — Low blood pressure, pulse and temperature.","Energy — Lack of energy, fatigue and muscular weakness.","Other — Constipation, abnormal lab values, impaired renal function, decreased bone density, and anemic pancytopenia.","Epidemiology & Etiology","Prevalence — Lifetime prevalence 0.5%. Less common than bulimia nervosa.","Sex ratio — 3:1 female to male.","Onset — Commonly adolescence or young adulthood. Uncommon before puberty or after age 40.","Higher-risk groups — Athletes in sports emphasizing aesthetics or leanness for competitive advantage, regardless of gender. Prevalence may also be higher among people who identify as LGBTQ+.","Biological — Genetic and familial predisposition. Genetic correlations with major depressive disorder, anxiety disorders, OCD and schizophrenia. Neurobiologically, tryptophan and its impact on serotonin synthesis.","Psychological — An ego-syntonic disorder: the person knows the actions are harmful but believes the benefits outweigh the harm. Significant struggle with emotional identification, regulation and processing, with low distress tolerance.","Environmental — Internalization of a thin body ideal, associated with cultures that value thinness.","Comorbidity — Bipolar disorder, anxiety disorders, OCD, depressive disorders, PTSD and trauma-related disorders, and alcohol or substance use disorders."]},{"s":"Warning Signs & Clinical Course","a":"an-warning","k":["Warning Signs & Clinical Course","Warning Signs","Dramatic weight loss.","Preoccupation with weight, food, calories, fat grams and dieting.","Refusal to eat certain foods, progressing to restriction against whole categories of food.","Frequent comments about feeling fat despite weight loss, and denial of hunger.","Food rituals: eating foods in certain orders, excessive chewing, rearranging food on a plate.","Consistent excuses to avoid mealtimes, and excessive, rigid exercise to burn off calories despite weather, fatigue, illness or injury.","Withdrawal from usual friends and activities.","Clinical Course","A chronic condition with relapses characterized by significant weight loss. The 1-year relapse rate is about 50%.","Patients often continue to be preoccupied with food.","10% to 25% go on to develop bulimia nervosa.","1 in 5 anorexia nervosa deaths is by suicide.","Poor outcome relates to a lower initial minimum weight, the presence of purging, and an earlier age of onset.","Difficult to treat, but recovery can occur.","Suicide screening belongs in every eating disorder assessment and is covered in full in Suicide","Complications of Weight Loss and Starvation","Cardiac — Bradycardia, hypotension, loss of cardiac muscle, small heart, arrhythmias, chest pain, sudden death.","Metabolic — Hypothyroidism producing lack of energy, weakness, cold intolerance and bradycardia. Also hypoglycemia and electrolyte abnormalities.","Musculoskeletal — Loss of muscle mass and fat, and early onset osteoporosis.","Gastrointestinal — Delayed gastric emptying, bloating, constipation, abdominal pain, gas and diarrhea, GERD, hemorrhoids.","Reproductive — Amenorrhea, irregular periods, loss of libido, infertility.","Dermatologic — Dry, cracking skin and brittle nails from dehydration, lanugo, edema, acrocyanosis, hair thinning, yellowish discoloration, poor wound healing.","Hematologic — Leukopenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia, hypercholesterolemia, hypercarotenemia.","Neuropsychiatric — Abnormal taste sensation possibly from zinc deficiency, apathetic depression, mild organic mental symptoms, sleep disturbance, fatigue."]},{"s":"Anorexia: Treatment & Hospitalization","a":"an-treatment","k":["Anorexia: Treatment & Hospitalization","Criteria for Hospitalization in Anorexia Nervosa Extreme electrolyte imbalance, or weight below 75% of ideal body weight Less than 10% body fat Daytime heart rate less than 50 beats per minute Systolic blood pressure less than 90 Temperatur…","Criteria for Hospitalization in Anorexia Nervosa","Extreme electrolyte imbalance, or weight below 75% of ideal body weight","Less than 10% body fat","Daytime heart rate less than 50 beats per minute","Systolic blood pressure less than 90","Temperature less than 96 degrees Fahrenheit","Arrhythmias","Initial Goals","Depend on the acuity of the patient.","Initiating nutritional rehabilitation.","Health teaching and health promotion.","Hospitalization is usually necessary, with intensive therapies, then outpatient partial hospitalization once stabilized.","Later Goals","Resolving conflicts around body image disturbance.","Increasing effective coping.","Addressing underlying conflicts relating to maturity fears and role conflict.","Assisting the family with healthy functioning and communication.","Weight Restoration","Once medically stable, a weight restoration program begins that allows for incremental weight gain, with a treatment goal set at 90% of ideal body weight. It runs on precise mealtimes, adherence to a selected menu, observation during and af…","Refeeding Syndrome A serious and potentially fatal condition that can occur during refeeding, caused by sudden shifts in the electrolytes that help the body metabolize food.","Refeeding Syndrome","A serious and potentially fatal condition that can occur during refeeding, caused by sudden shifts in the electrolytes that help the body metabolize food.","Biological & Psychological Treatments","Pharmacotherapy — No FDA-approved medications, and research does not support pharmacological agents. Fluoxetine has proven helpful for obsessive-compulsive behavior, but only after the patient has reached a maintenance weight.","Integrative therapy — Yoga, massage, acupuncture or bright light therapy.","Psychological therapy — No empirical evidence supports any specific psychotherapy model in adults. In adolescents there is evidence for insight-oriented therapy, family-based treatment, adolescent-focused therapy and CBT.","Long-term — Provided on an outpatient basis to maintain weight: individual, family and group therapy, plus nutrition counseling."]},{"s":"Bulimia Nervosa","a":"bulimia","k":["Bulimia Nervosa","An eating disorder in which the individual engages in recurrent episodes of uncontrollable binge eating followed by compensatory behavior to avoid weight gain through purging methods such as self-induced vomiting, laxatives, diuretics or ex…","DSM-5 Criteria","Recurrent episodes of binge eating, meaning both an amount definitely larger than most would eat in a 2-hour period, and a sense of lack of control over the eating.","Recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors to prevent weight gain: self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives or diuretics, fasting, or excessive exercise.","Both occur, on average, at least once a week for 3 months.","Self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight.","The disturbance does not occur exclusively during episodes of anorexia nervosa.","Epidemiology & Etiology","Prevalence — Lifetime prevalence 2.3% for women and 0.5% for men.","Sex ratio — 10:1 female to male.","Onset — Later adolescence, peaking into young adulthood. Rare under age 12 and over age 40. Incidence is increasing in women between 25 and 45.","Comorbidity — Nearly half have a comorbid mood disorder. More than half have a comorbid anxiety disorder. Nearly 1 in 10 have a substance use disorder, usually alcohol.","Biological — Genetic and familial predisposition. Neuropathological changes in the brain may be due to eating dysregulation. Biochemically, lower brain serotonin.","Psychological — Anxiety disorders or low self-esteem, impulsivity and compulsivity, chaotic non-nurturing family relationships, and difficult interpersonal relationships. Triggers: stress, poor body self-image, food, restrictive dieting or …","Environmental — Childhood sexual or physical abuse, traumatic events and environmental stress. Internalization of a thin body ideal, and weight-based teasing or bullying.","Risk factors — Binge-eating behaviors, a history of anorexia nervosa in a quarter to a third of patients, depressive signs and symptoms, problems with interpersonal relationships, impulsive behaviors, increased anxiety and compulsivity, and…","Clinical Course","Why It Goes Unnoticed","Few outward signs. Patients initially do not appear physically ill.","Often at or close to normal weight.","Binge and purge in secret, so it does not come to the attention of others.","Treatment is often delayed for years, and is usually initiated when control of eating is lost.","Once treatment is complete there is typically complete recovery, except where depression or personality disorders are present.","How Patients Present","Overwhelmed and overly committed \"social butterflies\" who have difficulty setting limits and establishing boundaries.","They hold enormous rules about food and food restriction.","They feel shame, guilt and disgust about their binge eating and purging.","They may be compulsive and impulsive in other areas of their life."]},{"s":"Bulimia: Warning Signs, Complications & Treatment","a":"bn-treatment","k":["Bulimia: Warning Signs, Complications & Treatment","Russell's Sign — Calluses or scars on the back of the hands and knuckles from self-induced vomiting.","Parotid swelling — Unusual swelling of the cheeks or jaw area.","Dental — Erosion of dental enamel, dental caries, and discoloration or staining of the teeth. Also xerostomia and tooth decay.","Evidence of bingeing — Disappearance of large amounts of food in short periods, or wrappers and containers indicating large consumption.","Evidence of purging — Frequent trips to the bathroom after meals, signs or smells of vomiting, packages of laxatives or diuretics.","Behavioral — Excessive, rigid exercise, lifestyle schedules or rituals that make time for binge-and-purge sessions, and withdrawal from friends and activities.","Cardiac — Ipecac cardiomyopathy and arrhythmias.","Neuropsychiatric — Seizures, fatigue, weakness, mild organic mental symptoms.","Treatment","Setting — Usually outpatient, unless the patient is suicidal or past outpatient treatment has failed. Hospitalize for life-threatening complications or suicide risk.","Focus of care — Stabilizing and normalizing eating to interrupt the binge-purge cycle, restructuring dysfunctional thoughts about eating, weight and shape, and teaching healthy boundary setting.","Behavioral technique — Using a diary to record binges and purges along with the precipitating emotions and environmental cues.","Pharmacotherapy — SSRIs are effective in treating binge eating and purging. Fluoxetine is the only FDA-approved medication for bulimia nervosa in adults, and is most effective in conjunction with CBT.","Psychological therapy — CBT is first-line treatment. Also DBT, group psychotherapy and support groups. Family therapy is not usually used, because of the age of the individual.","CBT and DBT are named as treatments throughout this topic and are taught in full in Assessment, Theories & Therapies"]},{"s":"Binge-Eating Disorder","a":"bed","k":["Binge-Eating Disorder","An eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of binge eating, with marked distress and impaired control over the behavior. It is the most common eating disorder.","DSM-5 Criteria","Recurrent episodes of binge eating, with both the large amount in a 2-hour period and the sense of lack of control.","Episodes associated with three or more of the following: eating much more rapidly than normal · eating until uncomfortably full · eating large amounts when not physically hungry · eating alone because of embarrassment over how much one is e…","Marked distress regarding binge eating is present.","Occurs, on average, at least once a week for 3 months.","Not associated with recurrent compensatory behavior as in bulimia nervosa, and does not occur exclusively during bulimia or anorexia nervosa.","Epidemiology & Comorbidity","Lifetime incidence 3.6% for women and 2.1% for men, and it is prevalent among females.","All racial and ethnic groups are represented fairly equally.","Occurs in normal-weight, overweight and obese individuals.","About half the risk is genetic.","79% have another psychiatric disorder, most often specific phobia, social phobia, PTSD and alcohol abuse or dependence.","History of trauma, adverse childhood events and food insecurity.","Health Consequences","Hypertension","High cholesterol levels","Heart disease","Diabetes mellitus","Gastrointestinal diseases","Gallbladder disease","Musculoskeletal problems","Treatment","Hospitalization is not usually required. Treatment is outpatient.","Health teaching and health promotion: healthy eating and exercise.","Pharmacotherapy: SSRIs and lisdexamfetamine dimesylate.","Psychological: individual or group CBT, DBT, and support groups.","Assessment includes the nutritional pattern, the history of weight cycling, and a careful history of binge-eating triggers, foods and frequency."]},{"s":"Comparing the Three Eating Disorders","a":"comparison","k":["Comparing the Three Eating Disorders","Weight — Significantly low BMI — Typically normal — Normal, overweight or obese","Compensatory behavior — Only in the binge-purge type — Always present — Absent","Lifetime prevalence — 0.5% — 2.3% women, 0.5% men — 3.6% women, 2.1% men","Sex ratio — 3:1 — 10:1 — Prevalent among females","Onset — Adolescence or young adulthood — Later adolescence, mean onset 18 — Adulthood","Severity — Life-threatening — Generally not life-threatening — Medical consequences of obesity","Usual setting — Hospitalization usually necessary — Usually outpatient — Outpatient","Medication — None FDA-approved — Fluoxetine, FDA-approved — SSRIs, lisdexamfetamine dimesylate","Outcome — Outcomes poorer, mortality higher — Outcomes better, mortality lower — Responds to CBT"]},{"s":"The Three Feeding Disorders","a":"feeding","k":["The Three Feeding Disorders","Pica — Ingestion of substances that have no nutritional value, such as dirt or paint. — Usually begins in early childhood and lasts a few months, but can appear in adolescence or adulthood. Males and females affected equally. Among institut…","Rumination disorder — Undigested food is returned to the mouth, then rechewed, reswallowed or spit out. — May be diagnosed after 1 month of symptoms. Can begin at any age, but in infants onset is usually between 3 and 12 months. More freque…","Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder — Food avoidance that can result in significant weight loss, nutritional deficiency, dependence on supplements or enteral feeding, and marked interference with functioning. Avoidance may relate to s…"]}]},{"f":"week4-schizophrenia.html","t":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","secs":[{"s":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Schizophrenia, Psychosis & the Spectrum","a":"what-it-is","k":["Schizophrenia, Psychosis & the Spectrum","Schizophrenia — A psychosis characterized by abnormalities in perception, in the content of thought, and in thought processes, plus extensive withdrawal of interest from other people and the outside world.","Psychosis — A state in which the individual is experiencing hallucinations, delusions, or disorganized thoughts, speech or behavior.","A mental illness that interferes with the ability to think clearly, manage emotions, make decisions and relate to others.","The Schizophrenia Spectrum Disorders","Schizophrenia","Schizoaffective disorder","Schizophreniform disorder","Schizotypal personality disorder","Delusional disorder","Brief psychotic disorder","Substance-induced psychotic disorder","Psychosis associated with another medical condition","Psychotic disorder not otherwise specified","Schizotypal personality disorder sits on this spectrum and is taught in full in Personality Disorders","DSM-5 Diagnostic Criteria","Two or more of the following present for a significant portion of a 1-month period, and at least one must be from the first three: delusions · hallucinations · disorganized speech · disorganized or catatonic behavior · negative symptoms.","One or more major areas of social or occupational functioning (work, interpersonal relations, self-care) markedly below the previously achieved level.","Continuous signs persisting for at least 6 months.","Absence or insignificant duration of major depressive, manic or mixed episodes occurring concurrently with active symptoms.","Not a direct physiologic effect of a substance or medical condition.","Where a prior history of autistic disorder or another pervasive developmental disorder exists, prominent delusions or hallucinations must be present."]},{"s":"Epidemiology, Etiology & Comorbidity","a":"epidemiology","k":["Epidemiology, Etiology & Comorbidity","Prevalence — 1% to 1.1% of the world's population, regardless of racial, ethnic or economic background. Approximately 3.5 million individuals in the United States, and one of the leading causes of disability.","More frequently diagnosed — Among males, and in urban areas.","Peak onset — Between 15 and 35, with 50% occurring before age 25. Men between 15 and 25, women between 25 and 35.","Age limits — New cases are rare before age 10 and after age 40. Children can be diagnosed, though this is uncommon at 1 in 40,000.","Etiology — Genetics · environmental · brain chemistry · substance use. The vulnerability-stress model applies.","Comorbidity","Psychiatric: substance use disorders, nicotine dependence, and anxiety, depression and suicide.","Medical: diabetes, cardiovascular disease, obesity, malignant neoplasm, HIV/AIDS, osteoporosis and hepatitis C.","Children & Adolescents","Early Developmental Signs","Language delays","Late or unusual crawling, and late walking","Other abnormal motor behaviors such as rocking or arm flapping","Later Signs & Treatment","Hallucinations, especially auditory, delusions, lack of emotion or emotions inappropriate to the situation, social withdrawal, poor school performance, decreased self-care, strange eating rituals, incoherent speech, illogical thinking and a…","Treatment: risperidone and aripiprazole, individual and family psychotherapy, social and academic skills training, and hospitalization."]},{"s":"The Phases of Schizophrenia","a":"phases","k":["The Phases of Schizophrenia","Prodromal — Mild changes in thinking, reality testing and mood, insufficient to meet DSM-5 criteria. Symptoms appear 1 to 12 months before the first full episode. Speech and thought may be odd or eccentric, with anxiety, obsessive thoughts …","Acute — Symptoms range from few and mild to many and disabling: hallucinations, delusions, apathy, social withdrawal, diminished affect, anhedonia, disorganized behavior and impaired judgment and cognition, resulting in functional impairmen…","Stabilization — Symptoms are stabilizing and diminishing, with movement toward a previous level of functioning. Care in an outpatient mental health center or partial hospitalization program may be needed.","Maintenance or residual — The condition has stabilized and a new baseline is established. Positive symptoms are usually absent or significantly diminished, but negative and cognitive symptoms continue to be a concern."]},{"s":"Positive Symptoms: Alterations in Perception","a":"positive-perception","k":["Positive Symptoms: Alterations in Perception","Positive symptoms are symptoms that exist but should not be there. The four main symptom categories are positive, negative, cognitive and affective. Positive symptoms cover alterations in perception, speech, thought and behavior.","Paranoia & Delusions","Paranoia — An irrational fear ranging from mild to profound, with deep mistrust or suspicion of others. May result in dangerous defensive actions, such as harming another person first. Can develop into delusional thoughts.","Delusions — False beliefs held despite a lack of evidence, which are not corrected by reasoning.","Persecutory — Belief that one is being watched, plotted against or ridiculed.","Referential — Belief that events with no connection to you are somehow related to you.","Grandiose — Belief that one is a powerful or important person.","Erotomanic — Belief that another person desires you romantically.","Nihilistic — The delusion of nonexistence: a fixed belief that the mind, body or the world at large no longer exists.","Somatic — Belief that the body is changing in unusual ways.","Religious — Belief in a special relationship with God, a mission for God, or that they are sinners.","Control — Belief that another person, group or external force controls your thoughts, feelings, impulses or behavior.","Hallucinations","Alterations in perception involving errors in how one interprets perceptions or perceives reality. A hallucination occurs when a person perceives a sensory experience for which no external source exists, and can involve all five senses: aud…","What Hallucinations Are Like","Very real to the person experiencing them, and distracting during interactions.","Supportive or terrifying, faint or loud, episodic or constant, and sometimes attributed to a specific source such as a parent or God.","Accompanied by varying degrees of anxiety and distress.","Command Hallucinations","Auditory hallucinations that instruct a patient to act in a specific way. Content ranges from innocuous to dangerous, including commands to harm self or others. They must be assessed and monitored for.","Suicidal or Homicidal Themes Require Immediate Safety Measures Ask directly: \"Are you hearing a voice that is telling you to do something?\" then \"Do you believe what you hear is real?\" If the answer is yes, the patient is at increased risk …","Suicidal or Homicidal Themes Require Immediate Safety Measures","Ask directly: \"Are you hearing a voice that is telling you to do something?\" then \"Do you believe what you hear is real?\" If the answer is yes, the patient is at increased risk for acting on the command."]},{"s":"Intervening with Delusions & Hallucinations","a":"nursing-perception","k":["Intervening with Delusions & Hallucinations","Delusions","Do","Remain calm, acknowledge and accept the patient's experience and feelings.","Focus on helping the patient feel safe, addressing the underlying theme of fear.","Ask the patient to describe the delusion, and assess its intensity, frequency and duration.","Validate the part that is real and present reality. Identify what triggered it.","As reality testing improves, supportively convey doubt and clarify misinterpretations of the environment.","Do Not","Never debate or argue the delusional content. It interferes with the development of trust.","Do not focus on the delusion itself. Focusing on the fear and what helps the patient feel secure is what is therapeutic.","Until reality testing improves, never try to prove the delusion is incorrect. This intensifies the delusion and makes staff appear untrustworthy.","Hallucinations","Assessing","Observe for indicators: moving the eyes back and forth, muttering or talking to self, appearing distracted, suddenly stopping conversation as if interrupted, or intently watching a vacant area.","Ask direct questions about the type and content. This is considered best practice.","Assess when they began and how the patient experiences them: supportive or distressing, background or intrusive.","Intervening","Introduce yourself, approach in a non-threatening, calm manner, and reassure the patient they are safe.","Call the patient by name, speak clearly and keep sentences simple.","Do not respond to hallucinations as if they are real, and do not deny the person's experience. Offer your own perceptions and present reality.","Be alert for signs of anxiety, which may mean they are intensifying or are command in type.","Engage the patient in reality-based activities, since it is difficult to focus on both at once.","Teaching the Patient to Cope","Manage stress and stimulation, and use other sounds to compete with the hallucinations.","Check with others to find out what is and is not real.","Tell yourself the voices are a symptom and are not real, and tell the voices to go away.","Make contact with others, and develop a plan for how to cope.","Documentation","For both delusions and hallucinations, document the type, content or theme, and characteristics, in the patient's own words where possible. Record the behavior before and during the episode and any actions taken. Where the patient posed a t…"]},{"s":"Positive Symptoms: Speech, Thought & Behavior","a":"positive-other","k":["Positive Symptoms: Speech, Thought & Behavior","Alterations in Speech","Circumstantiality — Extremely detailed and lengthy conversation about a topic that eventually gets to the point.","Tangentiality — Wandering off topic or going off on tangents and never reaching the point.","Looseness of association — Absence of the normal connectedness of thoughts, ideas and topics.","Flight of ideas — Topic changes repeatedly and rapidly with only superficial connections, making it difficult for others to follow.","Echolalia — Pathological repetition of another's words.","Clang association — Choosing words based on their sound rather than their meaning, often a rhyme or similar beginning sound.","Symbolic speech — Using words based on what they symbolize, not what they mean. A patient reporting that demons are sticking needles in him may mean he feels a sharp pain.","Pressured speech — Urgent or intense speech, with reluctance to allow comments from others.","Word salad — A string of totally unconnected words, a jumble meaningless to a listener.","Neologisms — Made-up words with meaning for the patient only.","Alterations in Thought","Thought blocking — A reduction or stoppage of thought. Mid-sentence the patient pauses and cannot remember what they were saying.","Thought insertion — The belief that someone else has inserted thoughts into the patient's brain.","Thought deletion — A belief that thoughts have been taken or are missing.","Alterations in Behavior","Catatonia — A pronounced increase or decrease in the rate and amount of movement. Excessive motor activity is purposeless.","Echopraxia — Involuntary imitation of another's movements and gestures.","Motor retardation — A pronounced slowing of movement.","Motor agitation — Excited behavior such as running or pacing rapidly, often in response to internal or external stimuli.","Negativism — A tendency to resist or oppose the requests or wishes of others.","Stereotyped behaviors — Repetitive, purposeless movements peculiar to the person that serve no purpose.","Gesturing or posturing — Posture held in a fixed position.","Boundary impairment — An impaired ability to sense where one's body or influence ends and another's begins.","Impaired impulse control — A reduced ability to resist impulses."]},{"s":"Negative, Cognitive & Affective Symptoms","a":"negative-cognitive","k":["Negative, Cognitive & Affective Symptoms","Negative symptoms are ones that should be there but are not. Positive symptoms are obvious to others and can make treatment seem more urgent, yet negative symptoms are a serious problem because they amount to the absence of what are conside…","Negative Symptoms","Affective blunting — Reduced or constricted affect.","Alogia — A reduction in speech, also called poverty of speech.","Anhedonia — A reduced ability or inability to experience pleasure or joy.","Apathy — A decreased interest in activities or beliefs that would otherwise be interesting or important.","Asociality — A decreased desire for social interaction, or discomfort while doing it. Social withdrawal.","Avolition — Reduced or lost motivation or goal-directed behavior: difficulty beginning or sustaining goal-directed activities.","The six negative symptoms all begin with A, except affective blunting: Alogia, Anhedonia, Apathy, Asociality, Avolition.","Cognitive Symptoms","Anosognosia — Inability to realize one is ill, caused by the illness itself. May result in resistance to or cessation of treatment, and is often combined with paranoia so that accepting help becomes impossible.","Concrete thinking — Impaired ability to think abstractly, resulting in interpreting things in a literal manner.","Impaired memory — Impacts short-term memory and the ability to learn.","Impaired information processing — Delayed responses, misperceptions or difficulty understanding others.","Impaired executive functioning — Difficulty with reasoning, setting priorities, comparing options and planning.","Affective Symptoms & Describing Affect","Affective symptoms involve an altered experience and expression of emotions. Mood may be unstable, erratic, labile (changing rapidly and easily), or incongruent (not what would be expected for the circumstances).","Flat — Immobile or blank facial expression.","Blunted — Reduced or minimal emotional response.","Constricted — Reduced in range or intensity, showing sadness or anger but no other moods.","Inappropriate — Incongruent with the actual emotional state or situation, such as laughing in response to a tragedy.","Bizarre — Odd, illogical, inappropriate or unfounded, including grimacing."]},{"s":"Schizoaffective Disorder","a":"schizoaffective","k":["Schizoaffective Disorder","A chronic mental health condition characterized primarily by symptoms of schizophrenia, such as hallucinations or delusions, plus symptoms of a mood disorder, such as mania and depression. Lifetime prevalence is 0.3%. Men and women are affe…","Major mood episodes last days or weeks before they subside, but the schizophrenia symptoms generally persist even after they end. That persistence is what distinguishes it from a mood disorder. Many patients are initially misdiagnosed with …","The Two Types","Bipolar Type","Both depressive and manic episodes alongside the schizophrenia symptoms.","Increased energy to the point of feeling no need for sleep, euphoria that may not match circumstances, and risky unsafe behavior from feeling invulnerable, then tiredness that does not resolve with sleep and sadness.","Depressive Type","No episodes of mania. The psychotic symptoms plus the depressed mood of major depressive disorder, experienced as long periods of intense depression rather than highs and lows.","Lethargy, loss of pleasure in favorite activities, inability to sleep or sleeping excessively, loss of appetite, and a feeling of never being happy again.","Manic and depressive episodes are named throughout this section and are taught in full in Bipolar & Related Disorders","DSM-5 Criteria","An uninterrupted duration of illness during which there is a major mood episode, manic or depressive, in addition to criterion A for schizophrenia. A major depressive episode must include depressed mood.","Hallucinations and delusions for two or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode, at some point during the lifetime duration of the illness.","Symptoms meeting criteria for a major mood episode are present for most of the total duration of both the active and residual portions of the illness.","Not the result of a substance or another medical condition."]},{"s":"Care Considerations, Outcomes & Relapse","a":"care","k":["Care Considerations, Outcomes & Relapse","Priority Problems","Safety Comes First","Risk for violence toward others","Risk for suicide or self-harm","Disturbed thought processes, and disturbed sensory perception","Risk for treatment non-adherence","Ineffective coping, risk for social isolation, risk for self-care deficits","Knowledge deficits, and risk for hopelessness or powerlessness","Outcomes & Interventions","Outcomes are specific and measurable with a timeframe, and are developed alongside the patient. Avoid open words like increase or decrease, and attach a number and a deadline: the patient will state 2 to 3 coping strategies prior to dischar…","Safety — Assess using the C-SSRS each shift, assess for thoughts of harm to self or others, and assess the environment and implement safety measures such as removing harmful objects.","Orientation — Assess orientation to person, place, time and situation each shift.","Coping — Assess current coping strategies, and teach one coping strategy each shift.","Medication — Administer as prescribed, give PRN medication for acute agitation or aggression, and assess understanding of the treatment plan.","Education — Educate the patient and family about the illness, its symptoms and the importance of treatment adherence.","The C-SSRS is used here as a standing shift assessment and is taught in full in Suicide","Relapse","Non-Adherence Is the Major Reason for Relapse 80% to 90% experience relapse during the course of their illness, and with each relapse there is usually a longer period to recover. Stopping medication almost certainly leads to relapse, with 8…","Non-Adherence Is the Major Reason for Relapse","80% to 90% experience relapse during the course of their illness, and with each relapse there is usually a longer period to recover.","Stopping medication almost certainly leads to relapse, with 80% relapsing within 2 years.","Failure to take prescribed medication risks relapse, suicide or self-harm, violence toward others, increased mortality, hospital readmission, decline in quality of life, and social and occupational difficulty.","Combining medication and psychotherapy diminishes the severity and frequency of relapses.","Goals of treatment across schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder: safety in all settings, physical care of the patient, stabilization on medications, patient and family education about the illness and its treatment, and psychosocial sup…","Antipsychotic medications, their side effects and the treatment of those side effects are taught in full in Antipsychotic Medications"]},{"s":"Additional Treatments & Prognosis","a":"prognosis","k":["Additional Treatments & Prognosis","Additional Treatment Approaches","Electroconvulsive therapy, which may be helpful with catatonia","Cognitive behavioral therapy, important for long-term recovery","Group therapy and family therapy","Social skills training","Case management and support groups","Factors Predicting a Poorer Outcome","Slow onset of the disease, more than 2 to 3 years","Younger age at onset","Longer duration between first symptoms and first treatment","Longer periods of untreated illness","More negative symptoms","The goal is to reduce the frequency, intensity and duration of relapses, which is believed to improve long-term prognosis. For most individuals symptoms improve with medication and psychosocial interventions, and many experience a good qual…","Electroconvulsive therapy is named here as a treatment and is taught in full in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]}]},{"f":"week4-antipsychotic-medications.html","t":"Antipsychotic Medications","secs":[{"s":"Antipsychotic Medications","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Typical & Atypical Antipsychotics","a":"classifications","k":["Typical & Atypical Antipsychotics","Antipsychotics come in two classifications: typical and atypical. It usually takes 2 to 6 weeks for them to become effective. Patient-specific dosage adjustments are required to balance effectiveness against side effects, and monotherapy, t…","Typical Antipsychotics","chlorpromazine · haloperidol","Reduce positive symptoms, with little effect on negative symptoms.","Used less often than atypicals because of that, and because their side effects are more challenging to manage.","As effective as newer antipsychotics for positive symptoms, and much less expensive.","The most common and distressing problem is extrapyramidal side effects, which often drive non-adherence.","Also anticholinergic effects, sedation, weight gain, metabolic syndrome, NMS, sexual dysfunction, endocrine disturbance, orthostatic hypotension, arrhythmias and an increased risk of seizures.","Atypical Antipsychotics","clozapine · olanzapine · quetiapine · risperidone · aripiprazole","Treat both positive and negative symptoms.","Less likely to cause tardive dyskinesia or significant EPS, and side effects are generally fewer, milder and better tolerated.","Also less likely to cause anticholinergic effects, orthostatic hypotension and seizures.","Disadvantage: a tendency to cause significant weight gain and metabolic syndrome, plus sedation, sexual dysfunction and potential cardiac dysrhythmias.","May be more costly than typicals, which can be a barrier to adherence.","Anticholinergic Medications Used to Treat Side Effects","benztropine · trihexyphenidyl","Available Forms","PO — Pill, tablet or capsule.","Liquid or fast-dissolving — For patients with difficulty swallowing pills, or at risk for cheeking or palming medications.","Short-acting injectable — Primarily for agitation in psychiatric emergencies, such as assaultive behavior or refusal of court-mandated oral antipsychotics. Side effects can be intensified and are less easily managed when given intramuscular…","Long-acting injectable — For patients at risk for or currently non-adherent. Given every 2 to 4 weeks or up to months, which reduces conflict about taking medication and improves adherence. Requires transportation to receive the injection. …","Non-adherence and relapse drive the choice of form and are covered in Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"]},{"s":"Extrapyramidal Side Effects","a":"eps","k":["Extrapyramidal Side Effects","Serious side effects that affect motor control and coordination. There are four, and the first three usually begin within a few weeks of starting a new medication or increasing a dose. They cause discomfort and social stigma, and contribute…","Acute Dystonic Reactions","A sudden, sustained contraction of one or several muscle groups, usually of the head and neck. Painful and frightening, and a significant source of anxiety.","Airway Involvement Is an Emergency Dystonic reactions must be monitored for and acted on emergently when they affect the airway. Torticollis: spasmodic and painful spasm of muscles, with the head pulled to one side. Oculogyric crisis: the e…","Airway Involvement Is an Emergency","Dystonic reactions must be monitored for and acted on emergently when they affect the airway.","Torticollis: spasmodic and painful spasm of muscles, with the head pulled to one side.","Oculogyric crisis: the eyes roll back toward the head.","Laryngeal dystonia: spasm of the throat impairing breathing and swallowing.","Akathisia","Motor restlessness manifested as excessive pacing, inability to remain still, rocking while seated, or shifting from one foot to the other while standing. It can be severe and distressing, and is easily mistaken for anxiety or agitation. A …","Assess Before Medicating Mistaking akathisia for anxiety or agitation leads to administering more of the drug that caused it, making it worse.","Assess Before Medicating","Mistaking akathisia for anxiety or agitation leads to administering more of the drug that caused it, making it worse.","Treatment: a dosage reduction where possible, or a change in medication · an anticholinergic agent such as benztropine · the provider may add propranolol, lorazepam or diazepam, with benzodiazepines for short-term use only · relaxation exer…","Pseudoparkinsonism","A temporary group of symptoms resembling Parkinson's disease: stiff and stooped posture, shuffling gait, bradykinesia, pill-rolling finger movements, tremulousness in the hands and arms at rest, and dysphagia or reduced spontaneous swallowi…","Treatment: identification of the medication and slow, safe discontinuation · dosage reduction · addition of an oral anticholinergic agent such as benztropine or trihexyphenidyl. Sometimes the causative medication is necessary for the patien…","Tardive Dyskinesia","An involuntary rhythmic movement disorder that occurs with long-term antipsychotic treatment, varying from mild to severe. It is more common in women than in men and develops in about 25% of patients on antipsychotics. It usually involves t…","Assess with the AIMS Scale at Least Every 3 Months Routinely assess every patient taking antipsychotic medication using the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale.","Assess with the AIMS Scale at Least Every 3 Months","Routinely assess every patient taking antipsychotic medication using the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale.","Treatment: discontinue or reduce the dose depending on severity, with thorough documentation and close follow-up. If discontinued, switch to an atypical by tapering one down while tapering the other up. Symptoms usually persist even after t…"]},{"s":"Anticholinergic Effects & Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","a":"anticholinergic-nms","k":["Anticholinergic Effects & Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","Anticholinergic Side Effects","Dry mouth, blurred vision, dry eyes, constipation, urinary retention or hesitancy, drowsiness, dizziness, confusion, hallucinations, tachycardia and skin flushing from decreased sweating. They can progress to anticholinergic toxicity. Taken…","Anticholinergic effects: \"Mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, hot as a hare, dry as a bone.\"","Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","Rare, Serious and Potentially Fatal Usually associated with antipsychotics or drugs that block dopamine receptors, and usually occurs early in therapy. Early detection increases the chance of survival. It carries a 10% fatality rate and is …","Rare, Serious and Potentially Fatal","Usually associated with antipsychotics or drugs that block dopamine receptors, and usually occurs early in therapy. Early detection increases the chance of survival. It carries a 10% fatality rate and is difficult to diagnose in an emergent…","Signs: severe muscle rigidity · altered mental status · temperature over 103 degrees Fahrenheit · hypertension · tachycardia · tachypnea · diaphoresis · incontinence.","Treatment","Immediately stop all antipsychotics.","Symptomatic and supportive treatment: hydration with oral and IV fluids, monitoring vital signs, correcting electrolyte imbalances, and treating any dysrhythmia.","Hospitalization in the ICU is required.","dantrolene sodium and bromocriptine mesylate relieve muscle rigidity and reduce fever. lorazepam is used for agitation.","Cooling measures as ordered: cooling blankets, alcohol bath, cool water or an ice bath.","Complications as It Progresses","Rhabdomyolysis, protein in the blood from muscle breakdown, which causes organ failure in about 30% of patients.","Acute respiratory failure, which is the strongest predictor of mortality.","Acute kidney injury.","Sepsis and other systemic infections."]},{"s":"Metabolic Syndrome","a":"metabolic","k":["Metabolic Syndrome","Of special concern with atypical antipsychotics, and all of them carry the risk. It includes weight gain, especially abdominal, dyslipidemia, increased blood glucose and insulin resistance, and it raises the risk of diabetes, certain cancer…","Monitoring","Monitor weight and girth.","Order an initial glucose tolerance test, and monitor blood glucose regularly.","Provide nutrition and activity support.","Consider the patient's lifestyle."]},{"s":"Clozapine & Agranulocytosis","a":"clozapine","k":["Clozapine & Agranulocytosis","The gold standard in treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Individuals with refractory schizophrenia make up about 30% of the thought disorder population, and this group is prone to violence and suicide. A clozapine trial is justified after th…","Agranulocytosis Is the Reason Clozapine Is Not First-Line A reduction in circulating granulocytes and decreased production of granulocytes that limits the ability to fight off infection. A life-threatening condition in which white blood cel…","Agranulocytosis Is the Reason Clozapine Is Not First-Line","A reduction in circulating granulocytes and decreased production of granulocytes that limits the ability to fight off infection. A life-threatening condition in which white blood cell counts drop to dangerous levels.","Greatest risk during the first months of treatment.","Monitor WBC weekly for the first 18 weeks, then as directed by the provider.","Discontinue if the patient develops leucopenia or neutropenia.","Reversible if treated early.","Every drug on this page is gathered by class with indication, monitoring and teaching on the Medications page"]}]}];
